  ($q$cs-268$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Cross-Text Connections$q$, 2, $q$Text 1: Management researcher A argues that a four-day workweek increases overall productivity, citing a trial in which participating companies reported the same output in fewer hours.

Text 2: Management researcher B argues that the reported productivity gains largely reflect companies that volunteered for the trial already being unusually efficient, since similar gains did not appear when the schedule was later required at less efficient firms.$q$, $q$Which choice best describes the relationship between the two researchers' findings?$q$, $q$["Researcher B's results suggest that the gains Researcher A observed may not generalize beyond unusually efficient companies.","Researcher B's findings confirm that all companies see identical productivity gains.","Both researchers studied firms that were required, rather than volunteered, to join the trial.","Researcher A argues that productivity fell under the four-day schedule."]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Researcher B's finding that less efficient, required firms did not replicate the gains qualifies Researcher A's conclusion about the four-day workweek's general effect.$q$, null),
  ($q$cs-269$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Cross-Text Connections$q$, 2, $q$Text 1: Dietitian A argues that artificial sweeteners help reduce overall calorie intake, citing studies showing people who switch from sugary drinks to diet versions consume fewer total calories per day.

