  ($q$cs-479$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Text Structure and Purpose$q$, 1, $q$In the early 1900s, city planners treated streets primarily as channels for traffic, widening them to move cars quickly. Today, many planners prioritize pedestrians, adding narrow lanes, benches, and trees. The shift reflects a growing recognition that streets can serve as public spaces, not merely routes.$q$, $q$Which choice best describes the overall structure of the text?$q$, $q$["It criticizes pedestrian-friendly design as impractical for modern cities.","It contrasts an earlier approach to street design with a current one and explains what motivates the change.","It describes a single street and traces how its appearance has changed.","It argues that traffic planners have ignored pedestrians entirely."]$q$::jsonb, 1, $q$The text contrasts early-1900s planning with today's planning and ends by explaining the shift in thinking.$q$, null),
