  ($q$cs-665$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Words in Context$q$, 2, $q$Some economists believe the policy will have a ______ effect on small businesses, offering them modest benefits without dramatically changing how they operate.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical and precise word or phrase?$q$, $q$["unpredictable","transformative","marginal","devastating"]$q$::jsonb, 2, $q$Modest benefits without dramatic change describe a small effect, so "marginal" fits.$q$, null),
  ($q$cs-666$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Cross-Text Connections$q$, 1, $q$Text 1: Homework helps students practice what they learned in class. Without it, many students would quickly forget new skills.

Text 2: A recent study found that elementary students who had little or no homework did just as well on tests as those who had a lot. The researchers suggest that, for young children, homework may not be as helpful as many people think.$q$, $q$Based on the texts, how would the researchers in Text 2 most likely respond to the claim in Text 1?$q$, $q$["They would argue that homework may not help young students as much as Text 1 claims.","They would argue that young students should be given longer homework.","They would agree that homework is necessary for students of every age.","They would claim that test scores are not a useful measure of learning."]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Text 1 says homework is essential; Text 2's researchers found it may not help young students much. They would question Text 1's claim.$q$, null),
  ($q$cs-667$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Text Structure and Purpose$q$, 2, $q$Archaeologists have long debated whether the ancient city of Cahokia, near present-day St. Louis, was abandoned because of drought or because of conflict. A new study of lake sediments found no evidence of a severe drought during the period when the city's population declined. The authors suggest that political or social factors may better explain Cahokia's decline.$q$, $q$Which choice best describes the main purpose of the text?$q$, $q$["To describe daily life in the ancient city of Cahokia before its decline","To present research that casts doubt on one explanation for a city's decline","To prove that conflict among residents was what destroyed Cahokia","To explain how archaeologists collect and study ancient lake sediments"]$q$::jsonb, 1, $q$The study found no evidence of drought, weakening that explanation. The authors only "suggest" other factors, so the text doesn't prove conflict caused the decline.$q$, null),
  ($q$cs-668$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Words in Context$q$, 1, $q$The speaker's voice was so ______ that even people in the back row of the large hall had trouble hearing her.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical and precise word or phrase?$q$, $q$["loud","soft","confident","clear"]$q$::jsonb, 1, $q$People had trouble hearing, so her voice must have been quiet. "Soft" fits.$q$, null),
  ($q$cs-669$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Words in Context$q$, 2, $q$The composer's later works are more ______ than his early ones, abandoning traditional melodies in favor of unusual rhythms and harsh harmonies.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical and precise word or phrase?$q$, $q$["popular","experimental","repetitive","conventional"]$q$::jsonb, 1, $q$Abandoning tradition for unusual rhythms and harmonies is the mark of "experimental" music.$q$, null),
  ($q$cs-670$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Words in Context$q$, 2, $q$Although critics initially dismissed her abstract paintings as ______, art historian Lowery Stokes Sims argues that their careful layering of color reflects years of deliberate experimentation.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical and precise word or phrase?$q$, $q$["haphazard","expensive","derivative","methodical"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$"Although" sets up a contrast with "careful" and "deliberate." Critics must have seen the work as random or careless: "haphazard."$q$, null),
  ($q$cs-671$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Words in Context$q$, 2, $q$The documentary does not ______ the difficulties the early settlers faced; it shows hunger, illness, and failed harvests in unflinching detail.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical and precise word or phrase?$q$, $q$["exaggerate","gloss over","depict","dwell on"]$q$::jsonb, 1, $q$Showing hardships "in unflinching detail" is the opposite of skipping past them. The documentary does not "gloss over" the difficulties.$q$, null),
  ($q$cs-672$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Words in Context$q$, 2, $q$The new evidence does little to settle the question of when humans first arrived in the Americas.$q$, $q$As used in the text, what does the word “settle” most nearly mean?$q$, $q$["resolve","pay","inhabit","calm"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$To settle a question is to resolve it.$q$, null),
  ($q$cs-673$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Text Structure and Purpose$q$, 2, $q$Photographer Gordon Parks is best known for his images of American life in the mid-twentieth century. Less well known is that he was also a composer, a novelist, and a film director: in 1971, he directed Shaft, one of the first major Hollywood films by a Black director.$q$, $q$Which choice best describes the main purpose of the text?$q$, $q$["To analyze the style of Gordon Parks's photographs of American life","To describe the plot of Shaft and explain why it became popular","To argue that Parks was a better film director than photographer","To highlight lesser-known accomplishments of a famous photographer"]$q$::jsonb, 3, $q$The text notes that Parks is known for photography and then describes his "less well known" work in other fields.$q$, null),
  ($q$cs-674$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Text Structure and Purpose$q$, 2, $q$The following text is from a 1920 short story. Mr. Hale had kept the same schedule for thirty years: breakfast at seven, the office by eight, home by six. So when he was seen strolling through the park at ten on a Tuesday morning, whistling, his neighbors could talk of nothing else.$q$, $q$Which choice best describes the main purpose of the text?$q$, $q$["To criticize Mr. Hale's neighbors for gossiping about his life","To describe the layout of the park where Mr. Hale took his walk","To show how a break in a character's routine attracted attention","To explain why Mr. Hale decided to leave his job at the office"]$q$::jsonb, 2, $q$The text establishes Mr. Hale's rigid routine and then shows the neighbors' reaction when he breaks it. The reason he left his job is never given.$q$, null),
  ($q$cs-675$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Text Structure and Purpose$q$, 2, $q$Researchers studying elephants in Kenya have found that the animals appear to use specific calls for individual elephants, similar to names. When researchers played recordings of these calls, the elephant being "named" responded more strongly than other elephants did.$q$, $q$Which choice best describes the main purpose of the text?$q$, $q$["To describe the equipment researchers use to record elephant sounds","To describe research suggesting that elephants may use names","To argue that elephants are more intelligent than most other mammals","To explain why elephants in Kenya tend to live in large family groups"]$q$::jsonb, 1, $q$The text describes findings that elephants respond to calls that seem to identify individuals. It makes no comparison with human intelligence.$q$, null),
  ($q$cs-676$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Cross-Text Connections$q$, 2, $q$Text 1: Art critic Lena Ortiz argues that museums should display artworks without explanatory labels so that visitors can form their own impressions.

Text 2: Museum educator Samuel Greene notes that many visitors, especially those new to art, report feeling confused or excluded when they don't know anything about a work's background. Labels, he argues, invite more people into the conversation.$q$, $q$Based on the texts, how would Greene (Text 2) most likely respond to Ortiz's proposal (Text 1)?$q$, $q$["He would warn that removing labels could make museums less welcoming to some.","He would argue that museums should be designed mainly for art experts.","He would agree that labels keep visitors from forming their own impressions.","He would suggest that museums display fewer artworks with longer labels."]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Greene says labels help newcomers feel included, so he would worry that Ortiz's proposal would exclude some visitors.$q$, null),
  ($q$cs-677$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Text Structure and Purpose$q$, 1, $q$Earthworms might seem unimportant, but they play a big role in healthy soil. As they tunnel, they loosen the earth so that air and water can reach plant roots. Their waste also adds nutrients to the soil.$q$, $q$Which choice best describes the overall structure of the text?$q$, $q$["It tells the story of a scientist who spent years studying earthworms.","It makes a claim about earthworms and then supports it with details.","It describes a problem caused by earthworms and proposes a solution.","It compares earthworms with several other animals that live in soil."]$q$::jsonb, 1, $q$The text claims earthworms play a big role in soil and then explains two ways they help.$q$, null),
  ($q$cs-678$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Words in Context$q$, 1, $q$The fox's reddish fur helps it ______ among the autumn leaves, making it hard for prey to spot.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical and precise word or phrase?$q$, $q$["stand out","fall asleep","blend in","keep warm"]$q$::jsonb, 2, $q$Being hard to spot means the fox matches its surroundings, so "blend in" fits.$q$, null),
  ($q$cs-679$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Text Structure and Purpose$q$, 1, $q$Sharks have existed for more than 400 million years, longer than trees. They survived several mass extinctions, including the one that wiped out the dinosaurs.$q$, $q$Which choice best describes the main purpose of the text?$q$, $q$["To explain why trees evolved later than sharks did","To describe how sharks hunt and what they eat","To emphasize how long sharks have survived on Earth","To argue that sharks are more dangerous than dinosaurs"]$q$::jsonb, 2, $q$Comparing sharks' history with trees' and noting that sharks survived mass extinctions emphasizes their long survival.$q$, null),
  ($q$cs-680$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Words in Context$q$, 2, $q$Rather than ______ the town's history, the new museum puts it front and center, with exhibits on every era from its founding to the present.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical and precise word or phrase?$q$, $q$["obscuring","documenting","displaying","celebrating"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$"Rather than" introduces the opposite of putting history front and center. "Obscuring" means hiding, the logical contrast.$q$, null),
  ($q$cs-681$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Words in Context$q$, 2, $q$Though modest in size, the gallery commands the attention of critics with its bold and unusual exhibitions.$q$, $q$As used in the text, what does the word “commands” most nearly mean?$q$, $q$["requires","draws","controls","orders"]$q$::jsonb, 1, $q$The gallery attracts critics' attention, so "commands" means "draws." It doesn't literally give orders.$q$, null),
  ($q$cs-682$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Cross-Text Connections$q$, 2, $q$Text 1: Urban planner Dana Liu argues that cities should convert some car lanes into bus-only lanes. She notes that buses carry far more people per lane than cars do.

Text 2: Business owner Marco Ruiz worries that removing car lanes could make it harder for customers to reach shops downtown. However, he acknowledges that in cities that added bus lanes, foot traffic near stores often increased.$q$, $q$Based on the texts, which choice best describes how Ruiz (Text 2) would likely view Liu's proposal (Text 1)?$q$, $q$["With some concern, though he sees evidence that it may help businesses","With strong opposition, because he thinks buses are unsafe for shoppers","With indifference, because he believes traffic has no effect on businesses","With complete support, because he believes cars should be banned downtown"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Ruiz voices a worry but also admits that bus lanes have often increased foot traffic, so his view is mixed.$q$, null),
  ($q$cs-683$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Words in Context$q$, 2, $q$The novelist is known for her ______ prose: she rarely uses more than a few words where many writers would use a full paragraph.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical and precise word or phrase?$q$, $q$["economical","dramatic","ornate","careless"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Using few words where others use many describes "economical" prose. "Ornate" means the opposite.$q$, null),
  ($q$cs-684$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Cross-Text Connections$q$, 1, $q$Text 1: Video games are a waste of time for young people.

Text 2: Researchers found that some video games improve players' problem-solving skills and their ability to pay attention to several things at once.$q$, $q$Which choice best describes how Text 2 relates to Text 1?$q$, $q$["Text 2 describes how popular video games are designed.","Text 2 presents findings that challenge the view in Text 1.","Text 2 presents findings that support the view in Text 1.","Text 2 argues that young people should play more sports."]$q$::jsonb, 1, $q$Text 2 describes benefits of video games, which challenges the claim that they are a waste of time.$q$, null),
  ($q$cs-685$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Cross-Text Connections$q$, 1, $q$Text 1: Plastic straws are a major source of ocean pollution, so banning them will greatly reduce plastic in the sea.

Text 2: Straws make up less than 1 percent of the plastic that enters the oceans. Fishing nets and other gear account for a much larger share.$q$, $q$Based on the texts, how would the author of Text 2 most likely respond to the claim in Text 1?$q$, $q$["By claiming that the oceans contain much less plastic than people think","By arguing that fishing nets should be made of plastic instead of rope","By noting that banning straws alone would do little to reduce ocean plastic","By agreeing that plastic straws are the largest source of ocean plastic"]$q$::jsonb, 2, $q$If straws are less than 1 percent of ocean plastic, banning them alone wouldn't greatly reduce pollution.$q$, null),
  ($q$cs-686$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Words in Context$q$, 1, $q$After the long winter, the farmers were ______ to see the first green shoots appear in their fields.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical and precise word or phrase?$q$, $q$["puzzled","annoyed","frightened","relieved"]$q$::jsonb, 3, $q$Seeing crops start to grow after a long winter would make farmers feel glad that their worries were over, or "relieved."$q$, null),
  ($q$cs-687$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Text Structure and Purpose$q$, 1, $q$Writer Ernest Hemingway once said that he rewrote the ending of his novel A Farewell to Arms 39 times. When asked why, he replied that he was trying to "get the words right."$q$, $q$Which choice best describes the main purpose of the text?$q$, $q$["To show how hard one writer worked to perfect his writing","To summarize the plot and ending of the novel A Farewell to Arms","To explain how Hemingway became one of the most famous writers","To argue that novels should end in ways that satisfy most readers"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Rewriting an ending 39 times to "get the words right" shows how much effort Hemingway put into his writing.$q$, null),
  ($q$cs-688$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Cross-Text Connections$q$, 1, $q$Text 1: Social media helps people stay connected with friends and family who live far away.

Text 2: A survey of teenagers found that many feel more isolated after spending several hours on social media.$q$, $q$Which choice best describes how Text 2 relates to Text 1?$q$, $q$["Text 2 presents evidence of a drawback of the technology praised in Text 1.","Text 2 explains how social media was invented and how it spread.","Text 2 argues that teenagers should not be allowed to own phones.","Text 2 provides additional evidence that supports the claim in Text 1."]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Text 1 describes a benefit of social media; Text 2 describes feelings of isolation, a drawback.$q$, null),
  ($q$cs-689$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Words in Context$q$, 2, $q$The biologist's findings ______ the long-held assumption that the frogs were extinct in the region; her team recorded more than two dozen of them in a single night.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical and precise word or phrase?$q$, $q$["predicted","confirmed","ignored","overturned"]$q$::jsonb, 3, $q$Recording two dozen frogs disproves the idea that they were extinct, so the findings "overturned" the assumption.$q$, null),
  ($q$cs-690$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Text Structure and Purpose$q$, 1, $q$Pluto was considered the ninth planet for 76 years. In 2006, however, astronomers changed the definition of a planet, and Pluto was reclassified as a dwarf planet.$q$, $q$Which choice best describes the main purpose of the text?$q$, $q$["To argue that Pluto should be a planet again","To describe the surface and size of Pluto","To explain how Pluto's classification changed","To compare Pluto with the other eight planets"]$q$::jsonb, 2, $q$The text explains that Pluto was once a planet but was reclassified in 2006.$q$, null),
  ($q$cs-691$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Words in Context$q$, 1, $q$Before the race, the coach gave a short talk to raise the team's spirits. Her words seemed to work: the runners left the locker room smiling and confident.$q$, $q$As used in the text, what does the word “raise” most nearly mean?$q$, $q$["grow","build","collect","lift"]$q$::jsonb, 3, $q$The coach wanted to make the team feel better, so "raise" means "lift" here, as in lifting someone's mood.$q$, null),
  ($q$cs-692$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Text Structure and Purpose$q$, 2, $q$Tomatoes were once feared in Europe. Because they belong to the same plant family as deadly nightshade, many people assumed they were poisonous. It wasn't until the 1800s that tomatoes became widely accepted as food in much of Europe and North America.$q$, $q$Which choice best describes the overall structure of the text?$q$, $q$["It compares several foods that were once considered dangerous to eat in Europe.","It describes how tomatoes are grown and when they were first planted in Europe.","It describes a past belief about a food, explains it, and notes when it changed.","It argues that people should eat more tomatoes because they are healthy."]$q$::jsonb, 2, $q$The text presents the fear of tomatoes, gives the reason (their plant family), and says when attitudes changed.$q$, null),
  ($q$cs-693$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Text Structure and Purpose$q$, 2, $q$The following text is adapted from a 1913 novel. The old house stood at the end of the lane, its windows dark and its garden overgrown. Yet when Clara stepped inside, she found the parlor freshly swept and a vase of new roses on the table, as though someone had been expecting her.$q$, $q$Which choice best describes the overall structure of the text?$q$, $q$["It describes a place that seems abandoned and then reveals a detail suggesting otherwise.","It explains why the house at the end of the lane was abandoned years ago.","It describes Clara's journey down the lane to the house in chronological order.","It compares the old house with another house that Clara once lived in."]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$The text first presents the house as neglected, then reveals signs that someone has been caring for it. "Yet" marks that shift.$q$, null),
  ($q$cs-694$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Text Structure and Purpose$q$, 2, $q$The novelist Octavia E. Butler kept notebooks full of motivational messages to herself. "I will find a way to do this," she wrote in one. Scholars studying the notebooks say they reveal how Butler pushed through years of rejection before becoming one of the most celebrated science fiction writers of her time.$q$, $q$Which choice best describes the main purpose of the text?$q$, $q$["To compare Butler's career with those of other science fiction writers","To summarize the plots of several of Octavia Butler's novels","To show how a writer's personal notes reveal her persistence","To argue that all writers should keep notebooks like Butler's"]$q$::jsonb, 2, $q$The text uses Butler's notebooks to show how she persevered through rejection. It doesn't recommend notebooks for everyone or compare her to others.$q$, null),
  ($q$cs-695$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Words in Context$q$, 1, $q$Mount Everest's summit is so high that climbers often carry extra oxygen because the air there is ______.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical and precise word or phrase?$q$, $q$["heavy","thin","polluted","warm"]$q$::jsonb, 1, $q$At high altitude, the air contains less oxygen, which is described as "thin."$q$, null),
  ($q$cs-696$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Cross-Text Connections$q$, 2, $q$Text 1: Linguist Mira Patel argues that texting has weakened young people's writing. She points to essays in which students use abbreviations and skip punctuation, habits she traces to their phones.

Text 2: Linguist John McWhorter contends that texting is not writing in the traditional sense at all but a kind of "fingered speech" with its own rules. People who text fluently, he notes, can still switch to formal writing when the situation calls for it.$q$, $q$Based on the texts, how would McWhorter (Text 2) most likely respond to Patel's argument (Text 1)?$q$, $q$["He would argue that students should stop using abbreviations when they text.","He would claim that formal writing is no longer necessary for most students.","He would argue that texting has its own conventions and needn't harm formal writing.","He would agree that texting has seriously damaged students' formal writing skills."]$q$::jsonb, 2, $q$McWhorter sees texting as a separate form of communication and says texters can still write formally, so he would dispute Patel's claim that texting weakens writing.$q$, null),
  ($q$cs-697$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Words in Context$q$, 2, $q$The painter's early landscapes betray the influence of the Dutch masters she studied as a young student.$q$, $q$As used in the text, what does the word “betray” most nearly mean?$q$, $q$["reveal","abandon","hide","deceive"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$The paintings show signs of the Dutch masters' influence, so "betray" means "reveal."$q$, null),
  ($q$cs-698$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Cross-Text Connections$q$, 1, $q$Text 1: Breakfast is the most important meal of the day, and skipping it leads to weight gain.

Text 2: A review of several studies found no clear link between skipping breakfast and gaining weight.$q$, $q$Based on the texts, how would the authors of the review in Text 2 most likely respond to the claim in Text 1?$q$, $q$["They would say the evidence doesn't support Text 1's claim about weight gain.","They would say that people should avoid eating breakfast altogether.","They would agree that skipping breakfast leads people to gain weight.","They would argue that breakfast should be the largest meal of the day."]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$The review found no clear link, so its authors would say Text 1's claim isn't supported.$q$, null),
  ($q$cs-699$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Words in Context$q$, 1, $q$The scientist hoped her research would shed light on why some trees survive droughts better than others.$q$, $q$As used in the text, what does the word “shed light on” most nearly mean?$q$, $q$["remove","explain","hide","brighten"]$q$::jsonb, 1, $q$Research that sheds light on something helps make it clear, or explains it.$q$, null),
  ($q$cs-700$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Words in Context$q$, 1, $q$The rules of the game are ______: players may not use their hands under any circumstances.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical and precise word or phrase?$q$, $q$["unclear","strict","optional","flexible"]$q$::jsonb, 1, $q$A rule that applies "under any circumstances" allows no exceptions, so the rules are "strict."$q$, null),
  ($q$cs-701$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Text Structure and Purpose$q$, 2, $q$Urban gardens offer neighborhoods fresh produce and green space. They also bring residents together: in one survey, 70 percent of community gardeners said they had made new friends through gardening. Some cities have begun turning vacant lots into gardens for exactly these reasons.$q$, $q$Which choice best describes the overall structure of the text?$q$, $q$["It explains step by step how residents can start a community garden.","It identifies benefits of urban gardens and notes how cities are responding.","It compares the benefits of urban gardens with those of rural farms.","It describes a problem with urban gardens and then suggests a solution."]$q$::jsonb, 1, $q$The text lists benefits (produce, green space, friendships) and ends with cities acting on them.$q$, null),
  ($q$cs-702$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Text Structure and Purpose$q$, 2, $q$In 1854, a cholera outbreak killed hundreds of people in London. Many doctors believed the disease spread through bad air. Physician John Snow mapped the deaths and found that they clustered around a single water pump. After the pump's handle was removed, new cases dropped sharply.$q$, $q$Which choice best describes the main purpose of the text?$q$, $q$["To explain how cholera affects the human body once a person is infected","To criticize London's government for failing to respond to the outbreak","To describe how a doctor's investigation identified an outbreak's source","To argue that all diseases, including cholera, spread mainly through water"]$q$::jsonb, 2, $q$The text describes Snow's mapping and the pump's link to the outbreak. It doesn't claim that all diseases spread through water.$q$, null),
  ($q$cs-703$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Words in Context$q$, 1, $q$The museum guide gave a ______ explanation of the painting, covering its history, technique, and meaning in great detail.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical and precise word or phrase?$q$, $q$["thorough","confusing","careless","brief"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Covering many topics in great detail is the definition of a "thorough" explanation.$q$, null),
  ($q$cs-704$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Cross-Text Connections$q$, 2, $q$Text 1: Nutritionist Paul Adams argues that people should avoid eating eggs because they are high in cholesterol, which he says raises the risk of heart disease.

Text 2: A large review of studies found that, for most healthy people, eating one egg a day was not associated with a higher risk of heart disease. The authors note, however, that people with diabetes may need to be more cautious.$q$, $q$Based on the texts, how would the authors of the review in Text 2 most likely respond to Adams's advice in Text 1?$q$, $q$["They would argue that eating eggs every day helps prevent heart disease.","They would fully agree that everyone should avoid eating eggs because of cholesterol.","They would say it is too broad, since most healthy people can eat eggs safely.","They would say that people with diabetes should eat more eggs than others."]$q$::jsonb, 2, $q$The review found no added risk for most healthy people but caution for some, so the authors would see Adams's blanket advice as too broad.$q$, null),
  ($q$cs-705$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Words in Context$q$, 2, $q$Coral reefs are ______ ecosystems: even a small rise in water temperature can cause corals to expel the algae they depend on.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical and precise word or phrase?$q$, $q$["fragile","resilient","ancient","colorful"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Being harmed by even a small change shows that reefs are easily damaged, or "fragile."$q$, null),
  ($q$cs-706$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Text Structure and Purpose$q$, 2, $q$Most species of bat use echolocation, emitting high-pitched sounds and listening for the echoes to find prey. Fruit bats, however, mostly rely on their large eyes and keen sense of smell to locate the ripe fruit they eat.$q$, $q$Which choice best describes the main purpose of the text?$q$, $q$["To explain in detail how bats use echolocation to locate their prey","To argue that fruit bats are more intelligent than other kinds of bats","To point out that one group of bats finds food differently from most","To describe the kinds of ripe fruit that fruit bats prefer to eat"]$q$::jsonb, 2, $q$The text describes how most bats find food and then contrasts that with fruit bats. It makes no claim about intelligence.$q$, null),
  ($q$cs-707$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Words in Context$q$, 2, $q$The athlete's recovery from her injury was ______; doctors had expected it to take a year, but she was competing again in four months.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical and precise word or phrase?$q$, $q$["incomplete","swift","gradual","painful"]$q$::jsonb, 1, $q$Recovering in four months rather than a year was fast, so "swift" fits.$q$, null),
  ($q$cs-708$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Words in Context$q$, 1, $q$The library has a ______ collection of maps, with more than 50,000 items from every continent.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical and precise word or phrase?$q$, $q$["vast","tiny","damaged","recent"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$More than 50,000 maps from every continent describes a very large collection, so "vast" fits.$q$, null),
  ($q$cs-709$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Cross-Text Connections$q$, 1, $q$Text 1: The best way to protect forests from wildfires is to put out every fire as quickly as possible.

Text 2: Ecologists note that small, controlled fires clear away dead plants that would otherwise fuel larger, more dangerous wildfires.$q$, $q$Based on the texts, how would the ecologists in Text 2 most likely respond to the approach in Text 1?$q$, $q$["They would argue that dead plants on the forest floor help prevent fires.","They would argue that putting out every fire may make severe wildfires more likely.","They would say that forests do not need any protection from wildfires.","They would agree that every fire in a forest should be put out immediately."]$q$::jsonb, 1, $q$If small fires clear fuel, putting out all of them lets fuel build up, which could lead to worse fires.$q$, null),
  ($q$cs-710$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Text Structure and Purpose$q$, 1, $q$Every autumn, millions of monarch butterflies travel from Canada and the United States to forests in central Mexico. Some fly more than 2,500 miles. Scientists believe the butterflies use the position of the sun to find their way.$q$, $q$Which choice best describes the main purpose of the text?$q$, $q$["To argue that the forests where monarchs spend the winter should be protected","To describe the monarch's long migration and how it may navigate","To compare the distances traveled by monarchs with those of other insects","To explain why the forests of central Mexico stay warm in the winter"]$q$::jsonb, 1, $q$The text describes where monarchs travel, how far, and how scientists think they navigate. It doesn't argue for protection, compare monarchs with other insects, or explain Mexico's climate.$q$, null),
  ($q$cs-711$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Text Structure and Purpose$q$, 1, $q$Many people believe that goldfish have three-second memories. Experiments, however, have shown that goldfish can remember how to find food in a maze for months.$q$, $q$Which choice best describes the overall structure of the text?$q$, $q$["It states a popular belief and then presents evidence against it.","It explains why goldfish are among the most popular pets kept.","It compares the memories of goldfish with those of other pets.","It describes how to care for goldfish so that they stay healthy."]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$The text gives the three-second-memory belief and then evidence that disproves it.$q$, null),
  ($q$cs-712$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Text Structure and Purpose$q$, 1, $q$The town's old train station sat empty for 20 years. Last year, a group of residents raised money to repair it. Today, the building is a busy community center with a café, a library, and art classes.$q$, $q$Which choice best describes the overall structure of the text?$q$, $q$["It describes how a building changed over time.","It explains how trains work.","It compares two community centers.","It argues against building new train stations."]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$The text moves from the station's empty past to its repair and then to its current use, tracing change over time.$q$, null),
  ($q$cs-713$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Words in Context$q$, 2, $q$Early maps of the region were often ______, placing rivers in the wrong locations and omitting entire mountain ranges.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical and precise word or phrase?$q$, $q$["detailed","inaccurate","beautiful","valuable"]$q$::jsonb, 1, $q$Misplaced rivers and missing mountains are errors, so the maps were "inaccurate."$q$, null),
  ($q$cs-714$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Words in Context$q$, 1, $q$The new bridge is much ______ than the old one: it can carry twice as many cars and has survived three major storms without damage.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical and precise word or phrase?$q$, $q$["narrower","shorter","older","sturdier"]$q$::jsonb, 3, $q$Carrying more cars and surviving storms shows strength, so "sturdier" is the logical word.$q$, null),
  ($q$cs-715$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Words in Context$q$, 1, $q$The mayor's speech was ______, lasting only three minutes, but it covered all of the key points.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical and precise word or phrase?$q$, $q$["dull","lengthy","concise","rambling"]$q$::jsonb, 2, $q$A three-minute speech that still covered the key points was brief and to the point, or "concise."$q$, null),
  ($q$cs-716$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Words in Context$q$, 2, $q$The mayor's proposal drew sharp criticism from residents who felt that the new parking fees would hurt local businesses.$q$, $q$As used in the text, what does the word “drew” most nearly mean?$q$, $q$["attracted","selected","pulled","sketched"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$The proposal caused residents to criticize it, so "drew" means "attracted" or "brought about."$q$, null),
  ($q$cs-717$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Words in Context$q$, 2, $q$The theory gained currency among biologists in the 1970s, after several field studies seemed to confirm it.$q$, $q$As used in the text, what does the word “currency” most nearly mean?$q$, $q$["money","freshness","acceptance","speed"]$q$::jsonb, 2, $q$A theory that "gained currency" became widely accepted.$q$, null),
  ($q$cs-718$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Words in Context$q$, 1, $q$The author's first novel was a ______ success, selling millions of copies and winning several major awards.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical and precise word or phrase?$q$, $q$["minor","tremendous","doubtful","temporary"]$q$::jsonb, 1, $q$Millions of copies and several awards indicate a very large success, so "tremendous" fits.$q$, null),
  ($q$cs-719$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Cross-Text Connections$q$, 2, $q$Text 1: Some educators argue that handwriting instruction is no longer necessary because students now do most of their writing on computers.

Text 2: Researchers found that students who took notes by hand understood and remembered lectures better than those who typed. They suggest that writing by hand forces students to summarize rather than copy word for word.$q$, $q$Based on the texts, how would the researchers in Text 2 most likely respond to the educators in Text 1?$q$, $q$["They would claim that typing improves memory more than writing by hand does.","They would argue that students should never use computers to take notes.","They would argue that writing by hand still offers benefits that typing may not.","They would agree that schools should stop teaching handwriting to students."]$q$::jsonb, 2, $q$The researchers found that handwriting improved understanding, which challenges the view that handwriting is no longer needed. They don't call for banning computers.$q$, null),
  ($q$cs-720$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Words in Context$q$, 2, $q$The chemist's notebooks were so ______ that other researchers could repeat her experiments exactly, decades after her death.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical and precise word or phrase?$q$, $q$["secretive","meticulous","disorganized","brief"]$q$::jsonb, 1, $q$Records detailed enough for others to repeat experiments exactly must have been extremely careful, or "meticulous."$q$, null),
  ($q$cs-721$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Text Structure and Purpose$q$, 1, $q$When it rains, the soil in the valley quickly becomes muddy, and the roads wash out. To solve this problem, the town planted thousands of trees along the hillsides. Their roots now hold the soil in place.$q$, $q$Which choice best describes the overall structure of the text?$q$, $q$["It describes a problem and explains how it was solved.","It compares how two different towns responded to heavy rain.","It explains how rain forms and why it falls on the valley.","It argues that every town should plant trees on its hillsides."]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$The text describes muddy soil and washed-out roads, then tells how planting trees solved the problem.$q$, null),
  ($q$cs-722$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Text Structure and Purpose$q$, 2, $q$In 1977, NASA launched two spacecraft, Voyager 1 and Voyager 2. Their main mission was to study Jupiter and Saturn. But after completing that mission, both spacecraft kept traveling, and in 2012 Voyager 1 became the first human-made object to enter interstellar space.$q$, $q$Which choice best describes the overall structure of the text?$q$, $q$["It explains why the Voyager mission failed to study Jupiter and Saturn.","It compares the designs of the Voyager 1 and Voyager 2 spacecraft.","It argues that NASA should launch more spacecraft to interstellar space.","It describes a mission's original goal and then an achievement beyond it."]$q$::jsonb, 3, $q$The text gives the original goal (studying Jupiter and Saturn) and then describes reaching interstellar space, an accomplishment beyond that goal.$q$, null),
  ($q$cs-723$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Cross-Text Connections$q$, 1, $q$Text 1: Many scientists believe dinosaurs were cold-blooded like modern reptiles.

Text 2: Paleontologist Holly Woodward studied the growth rings in dinosaur bones and found that many dinosaurs grew quickly, as warm-blooded animals do. She argues that at least some dinosaurs were probably warm-blooded.$q$, $q$Based on the texts, how would Woodward (Text 2) most likely respond to the belief described in Text 1?$q$, $q$["She would say that the bones of dinosaurs cannot be studied reliably.","She would agree that all dinosaurs were cold-blooded like modern reptiles.","She would disagree, citing evidence that some dinosaurs grew like warm-blooded animals.","She would argue that modern reptiles are actually warm-blooded animals."]$q$::jsonb, 2, $q$Woodward's growth-ring evidence suggests some dinosaurs were warm-blooded, which challenges the view in Text 1.$q$, null),
  ($q$cs-724$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Text Structure and Purpose$q$, 2, $q$Researchers asked two groups of volunteers to memorize a list of words. One group took a 20-minute walk afterward; the other sat quietly. The next day, the walkers remembered about 15 percent more words. The researchers say that light exercise after learning may help the brain store new information.$q$, $q$Which choice best describes the main purpose of the text?$q$, $q$["To describe how the brain stores information in detail","To describe an experiment and what its results may suggest","To argue that people should exercise for at least an hour a day","To explain why some people have better memories than others"]$q$::jsonb, 1, $q$The text describes the experiment, the result, and the researchers' tentative interpretation ("may help").$q$, null),
  ($q$cs-725$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Text Structure and Purpose$q$, 2, $q$For decades, scientists assumed that plants could not respond to sound. Recent experiments have complicated that view. When researchers played recordings of caterpillars chewing leaves, nearby plants began producing chemicals that discourage insects from eating them, even though no caterpillars were present.$q$, $q$Which choice best describes the main purpose of the text?$q$, $q$["To describe a new method that farmers can use to protect crops from insects","To explain how caterpillars damage the leaves of the plants they eat","To argue that plants are just as intelligent as most kinds of animals","To present findings that challenge an earlier assumption about plants"]$q$::jsonb, 3, $q$The text gives an old assumption (plants can't respond to sound) and an experiment that complicates it. It doesn't claim plants are intelligent or recommend a crop-protection method.$q$, null),
  ($q$cs-726$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Text Structure and Purpose$q$, 2, $q$Many people picture the Vikings as violent raiders. Raids were certainly part of Viking life. But archaeological finds show that most Vikings were farmers, craftspeople, and traders, whose trade routes stretched from North America to Baghdad.$q$, $q$Which choice best describes the overall structure of the text?$q$, $q$["It compares the Vikings with other peoples of medieval Europe and Asia.","It argues that the Vikings never raided other lands, despite popular belief.","It acknowledges part of a common image and then broadens it with evidence.","It describes a single Viking raid in the order in which events took place."]$q$::jsonb, 2, $q$The text concedes that raids happened but adds evidence that most Vikings had other occupations. It doesn't deny the raids.$q$, null),
  ($q$cs-727$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Words in Context$q$, 2, $q$The ambassador struck a hopeful tone in her speech, predicting that the two countries would soon reach an agreement.$q$, $q$As used in the text, what does the word “struck” most nearly mean?$q$, $q$["adopted","hit","discovered","removed"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$To strike a tone is to adopt or take on a particular manner of speaking.$q$, null),
  ($q$cs-728$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Words in Context$q$, 1, $q$The owl's hearing is remarkably ______; it can locate a mouse moving under a foot of snow.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical and precise word or phrase?$q$, $q$["ordinary","sharp","weak","slow"]$q$::jsonb, 1, $q$Locating a mouse under snow requires excellent hearing, so "sharp" fits.$q$, null),
  ($q$cs-729$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Words in Context$q$, 2, $q$In her memoir, the diplomat is notably ______ about her own role in the negotiations, giving most of the credit to her colleagues.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical and precise word or phrase?$q$, $q$["bitter","boastful","modest","secretive"]$q$::jsonb, 2, $q$Giving others most of the credit shows humility, so "modest" fits.$q$, null),
  ($q$cs-730$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Words in Context$q$, 1, $q$The city's population has grown ______ over the past decade, increasing by only about 1 percent.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical and precise word or phrase?$q$, $q$["rapidly","slowly","suddenly","dramatically"]$q$::jsonb, 1, $q$An increase of only about 1 percent over ten years is slow growth.$q$, null),
  ($q$cs-731$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Text Structure and Purpose$q$, 1, $q$The Statue of Liberty was a gift from France to the United States. It was designed by Frédéric-Auguste Bartholdi and dedicated in 1886. Its copper surface turned green over time because of exposure to air and water.$q$, $q$Which choice best describes the main purpose of the text?$q$, $q$["To argue that the statue's green surface should be cleaned","To compare the statue with other famous monuments in France","To give some basic facts about the Statue of Liberty","To describe a family's first trip to visit the statue"]$q$::jsonb, 2, $q$The text provides several facts about the statue: its origin, designer, date, and color.$q$, null),
  ($q$cs-732$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Cross-Text Connections$q$, 2, $q$Text 1: Ecologist Ruth Mbeki argues that reintroducing wolves to Yellowstone National Park caused elk populations to decline, which allowed streamside trees to recover.

Text 2: Ecologist Tom Harlan agrees that trees recovered after wolves returned but notes that the elk decline also coincided with severe droughts and increased hunting outside the park. He suggests that wolves were one factor among several.$q$, $q$Based on the texts, how would Harlan (Text 2) most likely respond to Mbeki's argument (Text 1)?$q$, $q$["He would agree that the wolves were the only cause of the decline in elk.","He would caution that she may give wolves too much credit for changes with several causes.","He would claim that the return of wolves actually increased the elk population.","He would argue that the streamside trees did not recover after the wolves returned."]$q$::jsonb, 1, $q$Harlan accepts that trees recovered but points to other factors, so he would think Mbeki overstates the wolves' role.$q$, null),
  ($q$cs-733$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Text Structure and Purpose$q$, 2, $q$The following text is adapted from a 1902 novel. Lucia had always believed that the city would feel like freedom. But on her first night there, surrounded by strangers and noise, she found herself missing the quiet fields of home more than she had ever thought possible.$q$, $q$Which choice best describes the main purpose of the text?$q$, $q$["To explain why Lucia decided to leave her quiet home in the country","To describe the noise and crowds of the city in vivid detail","To compare life in the city with life in the countryside","To show a character's expectations being contradicted by experience"]$q$::jsonb, 3, $q$Lucia expected freedom in the city but instead felt homesick, so the text shows her expectations being contradicted.$q$, null),
  ($q$cs-734$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Words in Context$q$, 1, $q$The volunteers worked to ______ the old theater, repairing its roof, repainting its walls, and replacing its seats.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical and precise word or phrase?$q$, $q$["demolish","sell","abandon","restore"]$q$::jsonb, 3, $q$Repairing and replacing parts of the theater means bringing it back to good condition, or restoring it.$q$, null),
  ($q$cs-735$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Cross-Text Connections$q$, 2, $q$Text 1: Psychologists have long argued that multitasking reduces efficiency because the brain must constantly switch between tasks.

Text 2: A study found that a small group of people, called "supertaskers," could drive in a simulator while solving math problems without any drop in performance on either task. The researchers estimate that only about 2 percent of people have this ability.$q$, $q$Based on the texts, how would the researchers in Text 2 most likely view the psychologists' argument in Text 1?$q$, $q$["As fully proven, because no one can perform two demanding tasks at once","As completely wrong, because most people can multitask without difficulty","As irrelevant, because very few people try to do two tasks at once","As accurate for most people, though a small minority are exceptions"]$q$::jsonb, 3, $q$Since only about 2 percent of people are supertaskers, the researchers would see the argument as true for most people but with rare exceptions.$q$, null),
  ($q$cs-736$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Words in Context$q$, 1, $q$Although the instructions looked complicated at first, the model turned out to be ______ to assemble; Ana finished it in ten minutes.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical and precise word or phrase?$q$, $q$["impossible","simple","dangerous","expensive"]$q$::jsonb, 1, $q$"Although" signals a contrast with "complicated," and finishing in ten minutes shows it was easy, so "simple" fits.$q$, null),
  ($q$cs-737$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Text Structure and Purpose$q$, 2, $q$Some city planners argue that the best way to reduce traffic is to build more roads. However, studies have repeatedly found that new roads tend to attract more drivers, a phenomenon called "induced demand." Within a few years, traffic on expanded highways often returns to its previous levels.$q$, $q$Which choice best describes the overall structure of the text?$q$, $q$["It compares traffic levels in several cities that have built new roads.","It traces the history of highway construction in the United States.","It describes a traffic problem and then praises a solution that worked.","It presents a proposed solution and then explains why it may not work."]$q$::jsonb, 3, $q$The text gives a proposed solution (more roads) and then evidence of induced demand, which suggests it doesn't work.$q$, null),
  ($q$cs-738$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Words in Context$q$, 1, $q$The chef will run the new restaurant downtown while her partner manages the original location.$q$, $q$As used in the text, what does the word “run” most nearly mean?$q$, $q$["operate","flow","hurry","race"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Running a restaurant means managing or operating it.$q$, null),
  ($q$cs-739$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Words in Context$q$, 2, $q$The city's plan to reduce traffic has been ______: congestion has fallen by 20 percent since the program began.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical and precise word or phrase?$q$, $q$["costly","controversial","effective","temporary"]$q$::jsonb, 2, $q$A 20 percent drop in congestion shows the plan is working, so "effective" fits.$q$, null),
  ($q$cs-740$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Cross-Text Connections$q$, 1, $q$Text 1: The ancient Egyptian pyramids were built by enslaved people.

Text 2: Archaeologists have found workers' villages near the pyramids, along with evidence that the builders were paid laborers who received food and medical care.$q$, $q$Based on the texts, how would the archaeologists in Text 2 most likely respond to the claim in Text 1?$q$, $q$["They would say evidence suggests the builders were paid workers.","They would say that the pyramids were built much more recently.","They would agree that the builders were enslaved people.","They would argue that no one knows how the pyramids were built."]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$The evidence of paid laborers with food and medical care challenges the claim that enslaved people built the pyramids.$q$, null),
  ($q$cs-741$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Cross-Text Connections$q$, 1, $q$Text 1: Reading on paper helps people remember more of what they read than reading on screens.

Text 2: In a study of college students, those who read a passage on paper and those who read it on a tablet scored about the same on a memory test.$q$, $q$Based on the texts, how would the study in Text 2 most likely affect the claim in Text 1?$q$, $q$["It would strongly support the claim.","It would cast doubt on the claim.","It would prove that paper books are more popular.","It would show that screens improve memory."]$q$::jsonb, 1, $q$If students remembered the same amount either way, the study weakens the claim that paper improves memory.$q$, null),
  ($q$cs-742$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Words in Context$q$, 2, $q$The author's argument rests on a single study, which some reviewers say is too small to support such broad conclusions.$q$, $q$As used in the text, what does the word “rests on” most nearly mean?$q$, $q$["depends on","relaxes in","stops at","lies beneath"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$The argument relies on the study, so "rests on" means "depends on."$q$, null),
  ($q$cs-743$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Text Structure and Purpose$q$, 2, $q$Many people assume that the Great Barrier Reef is a single structure. In fact, it is made up of nearly 3,000 individual reefs and about 900 islands stretching over 1,400 miles.$q$, $q$Which choice best describes the main purpose of the text?$q$, $q$["To argue that the Great Barrier Reef is in danger of disappearing","To correct a misconception about the Great Barrier Reef's structure","To describe the many animals that live on the Great Barrier Reef","To explain how individual coral reefs form over thousands of years"]$q$::jsonb, 1, $q$The text corrects the assumption that the reef is one structure by explaining that it is made of thousands of separate reefs.$q$, null),
  ($q$cs-744$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Cross-Text Connections$q$, 1, $q$Text 1: Zoos protect endangered animals and help educate the public about wildlife.

Text 2: Some critics argue that zoos keep animals in spaces far smaller than their natural habitats, which can cause stress and unusual behavior.$q$, $q$Which choice best describes how Text 2 relates to Text 1?$q$, $q$["Text 2 describes the history of the zoos that Text 1 mentions.","Text 2 provides an example that supports the claim made in Text 1.","Text 2 raises a concern about zoos, while Text 1 describes their benefits.","Text 2 restates the main point of Text 1 using different evidence."]$q$::jsonb, 2, $q$Text 1 presents zoos' benefits; Text 2 presents a criticism of zoos.$q$, null),
  ($q$cs-745$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Words in Context$q$, 1, $q$The team's star player was out for the season with a knee injury, so the coaches had to change their plans.$q$, $q$As used in the text, what does the word “out” most nearly mean?$q$, $q$["finished","missing from home","unable to play","outside"]$q$::jsonb, 2, $q$Because of an injury, the player couldn't participate, so "out" means "unable to play."$q$, null),
  ($q$cs-746$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Text Structure and Purpose$q$, 1, $q$Running and swimming are both excellent forms of exercise. Running is easy to start and requires little equipment. Swimming, on the other hand, is gentler on the joints, making it a good choice for people with injuries.$q$, $q$Which choice best describes the overall structure of the text?$q$, $q$["It describes the history of running and swimming as sports.","It compares two activities by describing an advantage of each.","It argues that running is a better form of exercise than swimming.","It explains how a beginner should train for a first running race."]$q$::jsonb, 1, $q$The text names an advantage of running and an advantage of swimming, comparing the two.$q$, null),
  ($q$cs-747$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Text Structure and Purpose$q$, 2, $q$Critics have often called the poet Emily Dickinson a recluse who wrote only for herself. Yet Dickinson sent nearly 250 poems to friends and family in her letters, and she corresponded regularly with editors and writers. Her poems, it seems, were meant to be read.$q$, $q$Which choice best describes the overall structure of the text?$q$, $q$["It compares Dickinson's habits with those of several other poets of her time.","It describes the poet's childhood and then traces the course of her later career.","It presents a common view of a poet, offers evidence against it, and draws a conclusion.","It summarizes several of Dickinson's poems and explains what each one means."]$q$::jsonb, 2, $q$The text gives the view of Dickinson as a recluse, offers counterevidence (letters and poems sent to others), and concludes that her poems were meant to be read.$q$, null),
  ($q$cs-748$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Text Structure and Purpose$q$, 2, $q$Some economists predicted that online shopping would put small bookstores out of business. Instead, the number of independent bookstores in the United States grew by about 50 percent between 2009 and 2019. Owners credit events, personalized recommendations, and a sense of community that online retailers can't easily replicate.$q$, $q$Which choice best describes the overall structure of the text?$q$, $q$["It explains how online retailers operate and why they attract so many customers.","It presents a prediction, shows it was not borne out, and gives possible reasons.","It describes the history of a single independent bookstore from 2009 to 2019.","It argues that online shopping should be restricted to protect small businesses."]$q$::jsonb, 1, $q$The prediction (bookstores would close) is followed by contrary data (bookstores grew) and the owners' explanations.$q$, null),
  ($q$cs-749$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Words in Context$q$, 1, $q$The baker was ______ about her ingredients, insisting on flour from one specific mill and butter from one local farm.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical and precise word or phrase?$q$, $q$["careless","generous","particular","uncertain"]$q$::jsonb, 2, $q$Insisting on specific sources shows she was choosy, so "particular" fits.$q$, null),
  ($q$cs-750$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Cross-Text Connections$q$, 1, $q$Text 1: Bees are disappearing because of pesticides used on farms.

Text 2: Pesticides harm bees, but scientists say other factors, including disease, habitat loss, and climate change, are also driving bee populations down.$q$, $q$Based on the texts, how would the scientists in Text 2 most likely respond to Text 1?$q$, $q$["They would say pesticides do not affect bees in any way.","They would argue that bee populations are actually growing.","They would say pesticides are only one of several causes.","They would agree that pesticides are the only cause."]$q$::jsonb, 2, $q$Text 2 lists several causes, so the scientists would see Text 1's single cause as incomplete.$q$, null),
  ($q$cs-751$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Text Structure and Purpose$q$, 1, $q$The following is from a short story. Nadia stood at the edge of the diving board, her toes curled over the end. Below her, the water looked very far away. She took a deep breath, closed her eyes, and jumped.$q$, $q$Which choice best describes the main purpose of the text?$q$, $q$["To show a character overcoming her hesitation","To describe the rules of a diving competition","To explain why swimming is good exercise for children","To compare Nadia's diving with that of other swimmers"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Nadia hesitates (the water looks far away) and then jumps, showing her overcoming her fear.$q$, null),
  ($q$cs-752$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Cross-Text Connections$q$, 2, $q$Text 1: Historian Jonas Berg argues that the printing press was the main reason literacy spread in Europe after 1450.

Text 2: Historian Lucia Ferraro agrees that printing mattered but notes that literacy rates rose most in regions that also built new schools. Without schools to teach reading, she argues, cheaper books alone would have had limited effect.$q$, $q$Based on the texts, how would Ferraro (Text 2) most likely respond to Berg's argument (Text 1)?$q$, $q$["She would argue that the printing press had no effect on literacy.","She would argue that it overlooks the importance of schools.","She would agree that cheaper books alone explain the rise in literacy.","She would claim that literacy declined after 1450."]$q$::jsonb, 1, $q$Ferraro accepts that printing mattered but stresses schools, so she would see Berg's account as incomplete. She doesn't dismiss printing entirely.$q$, null),
  ($q$cs-753$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Words in Context$q$, 1, $q$Because the river had ______ during the dry summer, the boats could no longer reach the upper docks.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical and precise word or phrase?$q$, $q$["widened","frozen","receded","flooded"]$q$::jsonb, 2, $q$A dry summer would lower the river, keeping boats from reaching the docks. "Receded" means pulled back or lowered.$q$, null),
  ($q$cs-754$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Words in Context$q$, 1, $q$The desert can be dangerous for travelers because water is ______ there.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical and precise word or phrase?$q$, $q$["free","scarce","plentiful","cold"]$q$::jsonb, 1, $q$The danger comes from a lack of water, so "scarce" fits.$q$, null),
  ($q$cs-755$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Words in Context$q$, 2, $q$The researchers took pains to ensure that every sample was stored at the same temperature, recording readings every hour.$q$, $q$As used in the text, what does the word “took pains” most nearly mean?$q$, $q$["showed sorrow","made great efforts","caused trouble","felt discomfort"]$q$::jsonb, 1, $q$Recording temperatures every hour shows careful effort, so "took pains" means "made great efforts."$q$, null),
  ($q$cs-756$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Words in Context$q$, 2, $q$The committee's report was ______; it criticized nearly every aspect of the project, from its budget to its timeline.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical and precise word or phrase?$q$, $q$["inconclusive","lukewarm","enthusiastic","scathing"]$q$::jsonb, 3, $q$Criticizing nearly every aspect is harsh and severe, so "scathing" fits. "Lukewarm" would be too mild.$q$, null),
  ($q$cs-757$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Words in Context$q$, 2, $q$Scientists are still trying to account for the sudden disappearance of the lake's fish population in the 1990s.$q$, $q$As used in the text, what does the word “account for” most nearly mean?$q$, $q$["report","calculate","explain","pay for"]$q$::jsonb, 2, $q$Scientists are seeking the reason for the fish's disappearance, so "account for" means "explain."$q$, null),
  ($q$cs-758$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Words in Context$q$, 2, $q$Researchers were surprised by how ______ the ancient seeds were: after more than 2,000 years in storage, several of them sprouted when planted.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical and precise word or phrase?$q$, $q$["viable","toxic","fragile","plentiful"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Seeds that sprout after 2,000 years were still capable of growing, or "viable."$q$, null),
  ($q$cs-759$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Cross-Text Connections$q$, 2, $q$Text 1: For centuries, sailors told stories of rogue waves—sudden walls of water more than 80 feet high—but scientists dismissed them as exaggerations, since models predicted such waves would be extremely rare.

Text 2: In 1995, an instrument on an oil platform in the North Sea recorded a wave 84 feet high. Since then, satellite data have shown that rogue waves occur far more often than older models predicted.$q$, $q$Based on the texts, how would the author of Text 2 most likely describe the scientists' view in Text 1?$q$, $q$["As correct, because measurements show that rogue waves almost never occur","As reasonable, because sailors' stories about the sea are often exaggerated","As mistaken, because measurements show rogue waves are real and not very rare","As irrelevant, because ships no longer cross the North Sea where waves form"]$q$::jsonb, 2, $q$Text 2's measurements show rogue waves occur more often than predicted, so the scientists' dismissal was mistaken.$q$, null),
  ($q$cs-760$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Words in Context$q$, 2, $q$Though the hummingbird appears ______, it is capable of migrating more than 2,000 miles each year.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical and precise word or phrase?$q$, $q$["aggressive","sturdy","enormous","delicate"]$q$::jsonb, 3, $q$"Though" sets up a contrast with the bird's impressive endurance, so it must seem fragile, or "delicate."$q$, null),
  ($q$cs-761$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Cross-Text Connections$q$, 2, $q$Text 1: Some economists argue that a four-day workweek would reduce productivity because employees would have less time to complete their work.

Text 2: In a six-month trial involving more than 60 companies, employees worked four days a week with no reduction in pay. Most companies reported that productivity stayed the same or improved, and nearly all chose to continue the schedule after the trial ended.$q$, $q$Based on the texts, how would the results described in Text 2 most likely be used to respond to the economists in Text 1?$q$, $q$["As evidence that a shorter workweek does not necessarily lower productivity","As support for the claim that employees need more time to finish their work","As proof that a four-day workweek works for every type of business","As evidence that employees should be paid less"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$The trial found productivity stayed the same or improved, countering the economists' prediction. One trial doesn't prove it works for every business.$q$, null),
  ($q$cs-762$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Text Structure and Purpose$q$, 2, $q$Sea turtles return to the beaches where they hatched to lay their own eggs, sometimes after traveling thousands of miles. For years, how they found their way was a mystery. Researchers now think the turtles detect the unique magnetic signature of their home beach and follow it back.$q$, $q$Which choice best describes the overall structure of the text?$q$, $q$["It compares the navigation of sea turtles with that of migrating birds.","It describes the life cycle of sea turtles from hatching to adulthood.","It argues that nesting beaches should be protected from development.","It describes a behavior, notes it was long unexplained, and offers an explanation."]$q$::jsonb, 3, $q$The text describes the homing behavior, calls it a mystery, and then gives the magnetic explanation researchers now favor.$q$, null),
  ($q$cs-763$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Words in Context$q$, 2, $q$Historians have long praised the treaty as a model of compromise, but newly discovered letters suggest that one side ______ agreed to its terms only under heavy pressure.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical and precise word or phrase?$q$, $q$["secretly","immediately","reluctantly","eagerly"]$q$::jsonb, 2, $q$Agreeing "only under heavy pressure" indicates unwillingness, so "reluctantly" fits.$q$, null),
  ($q$cs-764$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Cross-Text Connections$q$, 2, $q$Text 1: Many historians describe the Industrial Revolution as a period that improved living standards, citing the rise in goods available to ordinary people.

Text 2: Historian Emma Griffin studied hundreds of autobiographies by working-class people of the period. She found that while wages did rise for many, workers also described long hours, dangerous conditions, and crowded housing.$q$, $q$Based on the texts, how would Griffin (Text 2) most likely respond to the historians in Text 1?$q$, $q$["She would say that workers' autobiographies are unreliable as evidence.","She would agree that the period improved workers' lives in every way.","She would argue that workers' wages fell during the Industrial Revolution.","She would suggest that their view leaves out hardships workers described."]$q$::jsonb, 3, $q$Griffin acknowledges rising wages but adds workers' descriptions of hardship, so she would see Text 1's view as incomplete. She doesn't claim wages fell.$q$, null),
  ($q$cs-765$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Text Structure and Purpose$q$, 2, $q$The painter Georgia O'Keeffe is famous for her large, close-up paintings of flowers. Many critics in the 1920s interpreted these works as symbols of hidden emotions. O'Keeffe rejected those readings, insisting that she simply wanted people to see the flowers as she saw them.$q$, $q$Which choice best describes the main purpose of the text?$q$, $q$["To describe the painting techniques O'Keeffe used to create her flowers","To explain why O'Keeffe chose to paint flowers instead of portraits","To argue that the 1920s critics' interpretations of O'Keeffe were correct","To contrast critics' readings of an artist's work with her own explanation"]$q$::jsonb, 3, $q$The text gives the critics' symbolic reading and O'Keeffe's rejection of it. It doesn't take the critics' side.$q$, null),
  ($q$cs-766$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Words in Context$q$, 1, $q$The two paintings look ______ at first glance, but a closer look reveals many small differences in color and brushwork.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical and precise word or phrase?$q$, $q$["colorful","ancient","identical","unusual"]$q$::jsonb, 2, $q$"But a closer look reveals many small differences" contrasts with an initial impression that they're the same, so "identical" fits.$q$, null),
  ($q$cs-767$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Words in Context$q$, 2, $q$The novel's ending is open to interpretation; readers have debated for decades what happens to the main character.$q$, $q$As used in the text, what does the word “open” most nearly mean?$q$, $q$["available","subject","honest","unlocked"]$q$::jsonb, 1, $q$An ending that readers debate is "subject to" different interpretations.$q$, null),
  ($q$cs-768$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Text Structure and Purpose$q$, 1, $q$First, preheat the oven to 350°F. Next, mix the flour, sugar, and eggs in a large bowl. Then pour the batter into a greased pan and bake for 30 minutes.$q$, $q$Which choice best describes the overall structure of the text?$q$, $q$["It compares two different recipes that use flour, sugar, and eggs.","It tells a story about a baker who learned to make a new cake.","It presents a series of steps in the order they should be done.","It describes a problem with a recipe and then offers a solution."]$q$::jsonb, 2, $q$The words "First," "Next," and "Then" signal a sequence of steps.$q$, null),
  ($q$cs-769$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Cross-Text Connections$q$, 2, $q$Text 1: Many astronomers believed that planets could form only around single stars like the Sun, since the gravity of two stars would disrupt the material needed to form planets.

Text 2: In 2011, NASA's Kepler telescope discovered Kepler-16b, a planet orbiting two stars at once. Since then, astronomers have found more than a dozen such planets.$q$, $q$Which choice best describes how Text 2 relates to Text 1?$q$, $q$["Text 2 describes how the Sun and its planets first formed.","Text 2 offers new evidence that supports the belief in Text 1.","Text 2 explains the reasoning behind the belief in Text 1.","Text 2 reports discoveries that undermine the belief in Text 1."]$q$::jsonb, 3, $q$Planets orbiting two stars show that planets can form around more than a single star, undermining the belief in Text 1.$q$, null),
  ($q$cs-770$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Text Structure and Purpose$q$, 2, $q$In 2018, a team of volunteers began removing invasive plants from a small island in the river. Within three years, native wildflowers had returned, and the island now hosts dozens of bird species that had not been seen there in decades.$q$, $q$Which choice best describes the main purpose of the text?$q$, $q$["To compare the restored island with other wildlife areas in the region","To argue that all invasive plants should be removed from river islands","To explain why invasive plants are able to spread so quickly on islands","To describe the positive results of an effort to restore a habitat"]$q$::jsonb, 3, $q$The text describes the volunteers' work and its outcomes: returning wildflowers and birds.$q$, null),
  ($q$cs-771$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Words in Context$q$, 1, $q$The hikers were ______ after climbing for six hours, so they rested in the shade before starting down the mountain.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical and precise word or phrase?$q$, $q$["energized","exhausted","confused","hurried"]$q$::jsonb, 1, $q$Climbing for six hours and needing rest suggests the hikers were very tired, so "exhausted" fits.$q$, null),
  ($q$cs-772$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Text Structure and Purpose$q$, 2, $q$When the Brooklyn Bridge opened in 1883, it was the longest suspension bridge in the world. Its chief engineer, John Roebling, died before construction began. His son Washington took over, but he was soon injured and confined to his room. For years, Washington's wife, Emily, carried his instructions to the workers, and she eventually took on much of the engineering work herself.$q$, $q$Which choice best describes the main purpose of the text?$q$, $q$["To argue that John Roebling's original design for the bridge was flawed","To compare the Brooklyn Bridge with suspension bridges built today","To explain how engineers in the 1880s built long suspension bridges","To highlight the role of a person who was crucial to building the bridge"]$q$::jsonb, 3, $q$The text builds toward Emily Roebling's important but often overlooked role in completing the bridge.$q$, null),
  ($q$cs-773$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Cross-Text Connections$q$, 2, $q$Text 1: Architect Rafael Mendes argues that new buildings in historic neighborhoods should copy the style of older buildings so that the area's character is preserved.

Text 2: Architect Hana Sato believes that new buildings should reflect the time in which they are built. Copying old styles, she says, creates a false sense of history, though she agrees that new buildings should match the scale of their neighbors.$q$, $q$Based on the texts, on which point would Mendes and Sato most likely agree?$q$, $q$["New buildings in modern styles create a false sense of history in old areas.","Historic neighborhoods should not allow any new buildings to be constructed.","New buildings in historic areas should fit in with their surroundings in some way.","New buildings in historic areas should copy the styles of the older buildings."]$q$::jsonb, 2, $q$Mendes wants new buildings to match older styles, and Sato wants them to match the scale of their neighbors. Both want new buildings to fit in somehow.$q$, null),
  ($q$cs-774$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Words in Context$q$, 2, $q$The senator's position on the bill remained ______ throughout the debate; reporters could not determine whether she would vote for or against it.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical and precise word or phrase?$q$, $q$["ambiguous","controversial","consistent","popular"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Reporters couldn't tell how she would vote, so her position was unclear, or "ambiguous."$q$, null),
  ($q$cs-775$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Words in Context$q$, 2, $q$The museum's curators decided to ______ the exhibit for three more months because attendance had far exceeded expectations.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical and precise word or phrase?$q$, $q$["shorten","extend","relocate","cancel"]$q$::jsonb, 1, $q$High attendance would lead curators to keep the exhibit open longer, so "extend" fits.$q$, null),
  ($q$cs-776$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Text Structure and Purpose$q$, 1, $q$Some cities are painting roofs white to fight rising temperatures. White surfaces reflect sunlight instead of absorbing it, which can keep buildings cooler and reduce the need for air conditioning.$q$, $q$Which choice best describes the main purpose of the text?$q$, $q$["To compare summer temperatures in cities with and without white roofs","To argue that cities should ban air conditioning in most buildings","To explain how one strategy can help cities stay cooler","To describe the history of how roofs have been built in cities"]$q$::jsonb, 2, $q$The text describes painting roofs white and explains how it keeps buildings cooler.$q$, null),
  ($q$cs-777$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Text Structure and Purpose$q$, 1, $q$Many people think of deserts as lifeless. But the Sonoran Desert is home to more than 2,000 species of plants, along with hundreds of kinds of birds, reptiles, and mammals.$q$, $q$Which choice best describes the main purpose of the text?$q$, $q$["To describe the hot, dry climate of the Sonoran Desert in detail","To explain how desert plants are able to survive without water","To challenge a common belief about deserts by giving an example","To list the most dangerous animals that live in the Sonoran Desert"]$q$::jsonb, 2, $q$The text presents the belief that deserts are lifeless and then counters it with the Sonoran Desert's variety of life.$q$, null),
  ($q$cs-778$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Text Structure and Purpose$q$, 1, $q$Some students prefer to study in silence, while others like background music. Studies suggest the best choice depends on the person and the task, so students may want to try both and see which works better for them.$q$, $q$Which choice best describes the overall structure of the text?$q$, $q$["It describes how study habits have changed over time.","It presents two preferences and then offers advice.","It tells a story about one student's study habits.","It argues that music always helps students focus."]$q$::jsonb, 1, $q$The text describes two study preferences and then suggests trying both.$q$, null),
  ($q$cs-779$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Cross-Text Connections$q$, 1, $q$Text 1: Electric cars are better for the environment because they don't produce exhaust.

Text 2: Electric cars produce no exhaust, but building their batteries requires mining metals like lithium, which can harm local environments.$q$, $q$Which choice best describes how Text 2 relates to Text 1?$q$, $q$["Text 2 repeats the claim made in Text 1 without adding new information.","Text 2 completely rejects the claim that electric cars produce no exhaust.","Text 2 explains how gasoline cars produce exhaust that harms the air.","Text 2 agrees with part of Text 1 but adds an environmental drawback."]$q$::jsonb, 3, $q$Text 2 accepts that electric cars produce no exhaust but notes a harm from battery production.$q$, null),
  ($q$cs-780$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Words in Context$q$, 1, $q$The scientist was ______ that the experiment would work, but she was pleased when it succeeded on the first try.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical and precise word or phrase?$q$, $q$["proud","doubtful","certain","angry"]$q$::jsonb, 1, $q$"But she was pleased when it succeeded" signals that she hadn't expected success, so "doubtful" fits.$q$, null),
  ($q$cs-781$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Cross-Text Connections$q$, 1, $q$Text 1: Learning a second language is easiest in early childhood.

Text 2: While young children often pick up accents easily, research shows that teenagers and adults can learn vocabulary and grammar faster than young children when given the same amount of instruction.$q$, $q$Which choice best describes how Text 2 relates to Text 1?$q$, $q$["Text 2 complicates Text 1 by noting where older learners have an advantage.","Text 2 argues that adults are unable to learn a second language well.","Text 2 explains how accents form when people learn new languages.","Text 2 fully supports the claim that children learn languages most easily."]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Text 2 agrees children learn accents easily but shows older learners pick up vocabulary and grammar faster, complicating Text 1.$q$, null),
  ($q$cs-782$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Text Structure and Purpose$q$, 1, $q$The chess club meets every Tuesday in Room 204. New members are always welcome, and no experience is needed. Boards and pieces are provided.$q$, $q$Which choice best describes the main purpose of the text?$q$, $q$["To describe the history of the chess club and its members","To explain the basic rules of chess to people new to the game","To argue that chess is a better game than most other games","To invite people to join a club by giving basic information"]$q$::jsonb, 3, $q$The text gives the meeting time and place and says new members are welcome, which invites people to join.$q$, null),
  ($q$cs-783$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Cross-Text Connections$q$, 2, $q$Text 1: Biologist Grace Kim argues that the bright colors of poison dart frogs evolved to warn predators that the frogs are toxic.

Text 2: Biologist Andre Okafor agrees that the colors serve as warnings, but his research suggests that the colors also help the frogs attract mates. Females, he found, prefer brighter males.$q$, $q$Based on the texts, how would Okafor (Text 2) most likely respond to Kim's argument (Text 1)?$q$, $q$["He would argue that poison dart frogs are not actually toxic.","He would accept it but argue that the colors serve an additional purpose.","He would reject it, arguing that the colors have nothing to do with predators.","He would claim that predators prefer brightly colored prey."]$q$::jsonb, 1, $q$Okafor "agrees that the colors serve as warnings" but adds mate attraction, so he would accept Kim's view and extend it.$q$, null),
  ($q$cs-h1$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Text Structure and Purpose$q$, 3, $q$For much of the twentieth century, ecologists treated dead trees as waste—debris to be cleared so that healthy forests could grow. <u>Yet a standing dead tree, or snag, may host more life than a living one.</u> Woodpeckers excavate nest cavities in its softening wood; later, owls, bats, and flying squirrels move into the abandoned holes. Fungi and beetles break down the trunk, returning nutrients to the soil. Forest managers in many regions now deliberately leave snags in place, recognizing that removing them can impoverish the very ecosystems they hoped to protect.$q$, $q$Which choice best describes the function of the underlined sentence in the text as a whole?$q$, $q$["It summarizes the evidence that led ecologists in the twentieth century to treat dead trees as waste.","It presents a hypothesis about snags that the text goes on to question by describing the ways in which dead trees can endanger the health of the living trees around them.","It identifies a practice of modern forest managers that the text later argues is ineffective.","It introduces a claim that challenges the view described in the previous sentence and that the rest of the text supports with examples."]$q$::jsonb, 3, $q$The underlined sentence ("Yet a standing dead tree... may host more life") contradicts the earlier view of dead trees as waste. The following sentences give examples of the life snags support, and the final sentence describes how managers now act on this idea.$q$, null),
  ($q$cs-h2$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Text Structure and Purpose$q$, 3, $q$In 1957, the economist Hideo Tanabe predicted that Japan's rapid industrial growth would eventually strain its supply of fresh water. Planners largely ignored him; the country's rivers were abundant, and rainfall was reliable. By the 1970s, however, groundwater pumping in several industrial cities had caused the land to sink by as much as four meters, cracking foundations and worsening floods. Only then did the government impose strict limits on pumping and invest in recycling industrial water—measures that, in many cases, Tanabe had proposed two decades earlier.$q$, $q$Which choice best describes the main purpose of the text?$q$, $q$["To argue that Japan's rapid industrial growth after 1957 was unsustainable and should have been slowed","To explain why pumping groundwater from beneath industrial cities causes the land to sink and how sinking land can make flooding in those cities worse","To show that a warning initially dismissed proved accurate and that the measures eventually taken echoed the original proposal","To compare the economic policies of Japan in the 1950s with those it adopted in the 1970s"]$q$::jsonb, 2, $q$The text traces Tanabe's ignored prediction, the problems that later confirmed it, and the eventual adoption of measures he had proposed. The other choices focus on secondary details or make claims the text does not.$q$, null),
  ($q$cs-h3$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Text Structure and Purpose$q$, 3, $q$Most accounts of the printing press focus on the books it produced. Historian Elena Varga argues that its most transformative product was something more ephemeral: the pamphlet. Cheap, quick to print, and easy to pass from hand to hand, pamphlets allowed ordinary people to follow—and join—debates that had once been confined to scholars and clergy. During the religious conflicts of the sixteenth century, Varga notes, pamphlets circulated in such numbers that authorities found it impossible to suppress them, and public opinion became, for the first time, a force that rulers had to reckon with.$q$, $q$Which choice best describes the overall structure of the text?$q$, $q$["It describes the invention of the printing press and then traces its effect on the production of books in sixteenth-century Europe.","It presents a historian's argument that an overlooked product of the printing press had a major social effect, then explains the reasoning behind that argument.","It compares the influence of books and pamphlets and concludes that both were equally important to religious debates.","It explains why authorities in the sixteenth century tried and failed to suppress pamphlets, and it then argues that books were less important than pamphlets in that era."]$q$::jsonb, 1, $q$The text first notes the usual focus on books, then introduces Varga's claim that pamphlets were more transformative, and explains why (cheapness, wide circulation, the rise of public opinion).$q$, null),
  ($q$cs-h4$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Text Structure and Purpose$q$, 3, $q$The bar-tailed godwit, a shorebird weighing about 300 grams, makes one of the most extraordinary journeys in the animal kingdom: a nonstop flight of more than 11,000 kilometers from Alaska to New Zealand. <u>Before departing, a godwit nearly doubles its body weight, storing fat to fuel the trip, while organs it won't need in flight, such as its gizzard, shrink dramatically.</u> During the flight, which lasts about nine days, the bird neither eats nor drinks, and it is thought to sleep only by resting one half of its brain at a time.$q$, $q$Which choice best describes the function of the underlined sentence in the text as a whole?$q$, $q$["It explains some of the physical changes that make the godwit's journey possible.","It introduces a scientific debate about how the godwit navigates over open ocean.","It provides evidence that the godwit's flight is longer than that of any other bird.","To compare the everyday meaning of the word \"theory\" with its meaning in science and to argue that the scientific meaning should replace the everyday one"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$The underlined sentence describes how the godwit prepares physically—storing fat and shrinking unneeded organs—which helps explain how such a long flight is possible. It doesn't address navigation or post-flight health, and it offers no comparison with other birds.$q$, null),
  ($q$cs-h5$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Text Structure and Purpose$q$, 3, $q$Composer Marisol Ibarra was born in Havana and moved to New York in her twenties, intending to stay only briefly. Instead, she became the first music director of a newly founded Harlem dance company. Her own compositions blend Cuban son and Afro-Cuban rhythms with techniques drawn from European modernism, yet she has long resisted labels that would confine her to a single tradition. <u>Asked once whether she considered herself a Cuban composer or an American one, she replied that she considered herself simply a composer.</u> Her orchestral works are now performed by ensembles on four continents.$q$, $q$Which choice best describes the function of the underlined sentence in the text as a whole?$q$, $q$["It explains why Ibarra chose to remain in New York rather than return to Havana.","It suggests that Ibarra's music draws more heavily on European modernism than on Cuban music.","The text describes a problem facing a fishing village, explains the villagers' view of its cause, and then shows how a scientist's research helped the village solve it.","It illustrates Ibarra's reluctance to be identified with any single musical or national tradition."]$q$::jsonb, 3, $q$The underlined sentence follows directly from the statement that Ibarra "has long resisted labels that would confine her to a single tradition," and it illustrates that resistance through her own answer.$q$, null),
  ($q$cs-h6$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Text Structure and Purpose$q$, 3, $q$Many people assume that a scientific theory is a guess that has not yet been proven. In science, however, a theory is something quite different: a well-tested explanation that accounts for a wide range of observations and has survived repeated attempts to disprove it. The theory of plate tectonics, for instance, explains the distribution of earthquakes, the shapes of the continents, and the ages of rocks on the ocean floor. Calling it "just a theory" misunderstands the term; in science, "theory" represents one of the highest levels of confidence an explanation can achieve.$q$, $q$Which choice best describes the main purpose of the text?$q$, $q$["To argue that the theory of plate tectonics should be considered a scientific law rather than a theory","To describe the process by which scientists test and eventually prove a theory","To correct a common misunderstanding of a term by explaining its scientific meaning and illustrating it with an example","To compare the everyday meaning of \"theory\" with its meaning in other fields such as law and philosophy"]$q$::jsonb, 2, $q$The text begins with a common assumption about "theory," corrects it by explaining the scientific meaning, and gives plate tectonics as an example.$q$, null),
  ($q$cs-h7$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Text Structure and Purpose$q$, 3, $q$In the early 1900s, the residents of a Norwegian fishing village noticed that their catches of cod rose and fell sharply from one period of years to the next. Local fishers attributed the swings to luck or weather. A marine biologist working in the region suspected something else. By measuring the age of thousands of fish using the growth rings in their scales, he found that a single exceptionally successful spawning year could dominate the catch for several years afterward, as that generation of fish grew large enough to be caught. The swings, in other words, reflected the uneven survival of young fish, not chance.$q$, $q$Which choice best describes the overall structure of the text?$q$, $q$["The text describes a scientist's method, explains why fishers rejected it, and then describes how the method was eventually accepted.","The text describes a pattern, notes a popular explanation for it, and then presents a scientist's alternative explanation along with the evidence for it.","To describe how dolphins use echolocation to navigate and communicate, and to explain how a study showed that echolocation requires a large brain","The text describes a problem facing a fishing village and then explains how a scientist's research helped solve it."]$q$::jsonb, 1, $q$The text describes the pattern in the catches, mentions the fishers' explanation (luck or weather), and then explains the biologist's alternative explanation and the evidence for it (fish ages from scale rings).$q$, null),
  ($q$cs-h8$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Text Structure and Purpose$q$, 3, $q$The architect Lina Bo Bardi designed the São Paulo Museum of Art so that its main gallery would appear to float: the entire building is suspended between four massive red pillars, leaving a public plaza open beneath it. Inside, she rejected the conventional practice of hanging paintings on walls. Instead, she mounted each work on a sheet of clear glass set in a concrete block, so that visitors could wander among the paintings as if through a forest. <u>Bo Bardi believed that museums too often intimidated ordinary people and that art should be encountered as part of everyday life.</u>$q$, $q$Which choice best describes the function of the underlined sentence in the text as a whole?$q$, $q$["It provides a rationale for the unconventional design choices described earlier in the text.","It describes a criticism that other architects made of Bo Bardi's design.","It explains why the São Paulo Museum of Art is supported by four red pillars.","It suggests that Bo Bardi's design was more popular with visitors than with art critics."]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$The underlined sentence explains the beliefs behind Bo Bardi's choices—the open plaza and the glass mounts that let visitors wander—so it gives the reasoning for the design described before it.$q$, null),
  ($q$cs-h9$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Text Structure and Purpose$q$, 3, $q$Some scientists have proposed that the large brains of dolphins evolved to support complex social relationships, since dolphins live in groups whose membership shifts constantly. Others have argued that dolphins' brains are large mainly because echolocation—the processing of returning sound waves to "see" underwater—demands enormous computing power. A study comparing dozens of whale and dolphin species found that brain size was more closely linked to the size and complexity of social groups than to reliance on echolocation, though its authors acknowledged that the two factors may have reinforced each other.$q$, $q$Which choice best describes the main purpose of the text?$q$, $q$["The text describes a technological change, presents early evidence about its effects, and then argues that the change permanently harmed film as an art form.","To describe how dolphins use echolocation to navigate and communicate within groups","To explain why the social groups of dolphins shift constantly in membership","To present two hypotheses about a trait and describe a study whose results favored one while not ruling out the other"]$q$::jsonb, 3, $q$The text presents the social and echolocation hypotheses, then describes a study that found social group size more closely linked to brain size, while acknowledging that both factors may have played a role.$q$, null),
  ($q$cs-h10$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Text Structure and Purpose$q$, 3, $q$The following text is from Willa Cather's 1913 novel O Pioneers! Alexandra Bergson has inherited her father's struggling farm on the Nebraska prairie.<br><br>For the first time, perhaps, since that land emerged from the waters of geologic ages, a human face was set toward it with love and yearning. It seemed beautiful to her, rich and strong and glorious. Her eyes drank in the breadth of it, until her tears blinded her. <u>Then the Genius of the Divide, the great, free spirit which breathes across it, must have bent lower than it ever bent to a human will before.</u>$q$, $q$Which choice best describes the function of the underlined sentence in the text as a whole?$q$, $q$["It indicates that Alexandra is overwhelmed by the harshness of the prairie and doubts her ability to farm it.","It shifts the focus from Alexandra's feelings to a description of the prairie's weather and landscape.","It suggests that the land itself responds to Alexandra's devotion, emphasizing the depth of her connection to it.","It reveals that Alexandra's neighbors regard her love for the land as foolish and naive."]$q$::jsonb, 2, $q$After describing Alexandra's love for the land, the narrator imagines the spirit of the land "bend[ing]" to her will, suggesting a mutual relationship and emphasizing how deeply she is connected to the land.$q$, null),
  ($q$cs-h11$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Text Structure and Purpose$q$, 3, $q$When the first sound films appeared in the late 1920s, many critics predicted the decline of film as an art form. Silent cinema, they argued, had developed a sophisticated visual language—expressive faces, inventive camera angles, montage—that dialogue would make unnecessary. Early sound films seemed to confirm these fears: bulky microphones forced actors to stand still, and cameras were confined to soundproof booths. Within a few years, however, technical advances freed both cameras and actors, and directors began using sound creatively, layering music, dialogue, and noise to create effects silent films could not achieve.$q$, $q$Which choice best describes the overall structure of the text?$q$, $q$["The text describes a technological change and then argues that the change permanently harmed film as an art form.","The text describes a prediction, presents early evidence that seemed to support it, and then explains developments that undercut it.","The text compares silent and sound films and concludes that silent films were more artistically sophisticated.","It introduces a method of estimating the snow leopard population that researchers continue to rely on despite its limitations."]$q$::jsonb, 1, $q$The text presents critics' prediction, notes that early sound films seemed to confirm it, and then explains how technical advances and creative use of sound undermined it.$q$, null),
  ($q$cs-h12$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Text Structure and Purpose$q$, 3, $q$Snow leopards are notoriously difficult to study. They live at altitudes above 3,000 meters, range over enormous territories, and are so well camouflaged against rocky slopes that researchers sometimes pass within meters of one without noticing. <u>For decades, estimates of the global population rested largely on guesswork.</u> In recent years, however, scientists have combined camera traps, genetic analysis of droppings, and satellite collars to produce far more reliable counts, which suggest that between 4,000 and 6,500 snow leopards remain in the wild.$q$, $q$Which choice best describes the function of the underlined sentence in the text as a whole?$q$, $q$["It describes a limitation in earlier knowledge that the text goes on to say has been partly overcome.","To argue that Felon should be read primarily as a work of political argument rather than as poetry, since it raises questions about society","It provides evidence that the snow leopard population has declined over several decades.","It introduces a method that scientists still use to estimate the number of snow leopards."]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$The underlined sentence notes that population estimates used to be unreliable; the next sentence describes new methods that have produced more reliable counts.$q$, null),
  ($q$cs-h13$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Text Structure and Purpose$q$, 3, $q$In her poetry collection Felon, Reginald Dwayne Betts draws on his own experience of incarceration. Rather than presenting prison as a distant, abstract institution, the poems focus on concrete details: the sound of a cell door, the texture of a letter from home, the paperwork required to seek a job after release. Some critics have described the collection as political, and in a sense it is; it raises questions about how society treats people who have served their sentences. But Betts largely avoids direct argument, allowing the accumulated details to make his case for him.$q$, $q$Which choice best describes the main purpose of the text?$q$, $q$["It provides the main conclusion of the text, summarizing what the evidence in the preceding sentences reveals about how Teotihuacan was governed.","To summarize the events of the poet's life that are described in Felon","To compare Felon with other poetry collections that deal with the experience of incarceration","To describe the approach a poet takes in a collection and explain how that approach shapes the collection's political dimension"]$q$::jsonb, 3, $q$The text describes Betts's focus on concrete details and explains that, while the collection has a political dimension, it makes its case through details rather than direct argument.$q$, null),
  ($q$cs-h14$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Text Structure and Purpose$q$, 3, $q$The ancient city of Teotihuacan, in what is now central Mexico, was one of the largest cities in the world around 500 CE, with perhaps 100,000 residents. Unlike many ancient cities, it appears to have had no single ruler celebrated in monuments or inscriptions. Archaeologists have found no royal tombs and no images of individual kings. <u>Some researchers have proposed that Teotihuacan was governed collectively, perhaps by a council representing the city's neighborhoods.</u> Others caution that the evidence is ambiguous: rulers may simply have chosen not to depict themselves.$q$, $q$Which choice best describes the function of the underlined sentence in the text as a whole?$q$, $q$["It provides the conclusion of the text, summarizing the evidence presented in the preceding sentences.","It introduces a new piece of evidence that contradicts the earlier claim that Teotihuacan had no royal tombs.","It offers one interpretation of the evidence described earlier, which the following sentence notes is not the only possible interpretation.","It explains why Teotihuacan grew to be one of the largest cities in the world around 500 CE."]$q$::jsonb, 2, $q$The underlined sentence proposes collective governance as an explanation for the lack of royal monuments. The next sentence notes that the evidence could also be explained differently.$q$, null),
  ($q$cs-h15$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Text Structure and Purpose$q$, 3, $q$Mosquitoes find people partly by detecting the carbon dioxide we exhale, but carbon dioxide alone does not explain why some people seem to attract far more bites than others. Neuroscientist Leslie Vosshall and her colleagues spent three years testing the skin odors of volunteers, who wore nylon stockings on their arms to collect their scent. The researchers placed the stockings in pairs in a chamber and released mosquitoes to see which they preferred. One volunteer, nicknamed "Subject 33," was consistently far more attractive than everyone else. Chemical analysis revealed that the most attractive volunteers produced unusually high levels of certain fatty acids on their skin.$q$, $q$Which choice best describes the main purpose of the text?$q$, $q$["To argue that carbon dioxide is the main factor mosquitoes use to locate people","To describe a study that helped explain why some people attract more mosquitoes than others","To explain how mosquitoes use their sense of smell to find water for laying eggs","To describe the challenges researchers faced in recruiting volunteers for a long-term study"]$q$::jsonb, 1, $q$The text introduces the puzzle of why some people attract more mosquitoes, then describes Vosshall's study and its finding about fatty acids.$q$, null),
  ($q$cs-h16$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Text Structure and Purpose$q$, 3, $q$The following text is from a short story. Inés, who is sixteen, has walked out to the orchard behind her grandmother's house on the first warm afternoon of spring.<br><br>She lay on her back beneath the oldest of the pear trees and let the hum of the bees settle over her like a second skin. The light came down through the blossoms in pieces, gold and white and gold again, and the breeze moved the branches as if the whole tree were breathing. She watched a single bee, heavy with pollen, sink into the heart of a flower, and the flower seemed to bend to meet it. Something she had no words for opened inside her then, as wide and bright as the sky beyond the branches.$q$, $q$Which choice best describes the main purpose of the text?$q$, $q$["It portrays a moment in which a character experiences an intense awakening while observing the natural world.","It contrasts a character's peaceful surroundings with her growing sense of unease about the future.","It explains why researchers initially doubted the age of the evidence found at Monte Verde and how they eventually confirmed that age.","It establishes a conflict between a character and the family members who disapprove of her daydreaming."]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$The text vividly describes Inés lying under a pear tree, watching a bee enter a blossom, and feeling "something she had no words for" open inside her—a moment of awakening. There is no sense of unease, no scientific purpose, and no conflict with others.$q$, null),
  ($q$cs-h17$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Text Structure and Purpose$q$, 3, $q$For years, archaeologists believed that the first Americans arrived from Asia about 13,000 years ago, walking through an ice-free corridor that opened between two massive ice sheets in what is now Canada. Then, in the 1970s, excavations at Monte Verde in southern Chile uncovered evidence of human habitation dating to at least 14,500 years ago. <u>The finding posed a puzzle: the ice-free corridor had not yet opened at that time.</u> Many researchers now believe that the first Americans traveled along the Pacific coast, perhaps by boat, rather than through the interior.$q$, $q$Which choice best describes the function of the underlined sentence in the text as a whole?$q$, $q$["It provides evidence that supports the original theory about how the first Americans arrived.","It explains why researchers initially doubted the age of the evidence found at Monte Verde.","It introduces a new archaeological site that challenges the findings at Monte Verde.","It identifies a problem raised by a discovery, which the following sentence offers a possible way to resolve."]$q$::jsonb, 3, $q$The underlined sentence explains why the Monte Verde finding is puzzling (people arrived before the corridor existed), and the next sentence proposes the coastal route as a solution.$q$, null),
  ($q$cs-h18$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Text Structure and Purpose$q$, 3, $q$Critics have often described the paintings of Agnes Martin as minimalist: most consist of little more than faint pencil grids or pale horizontal bands on a square canvas. Martin herself rejected the label. Minimalism, as she understood it, aimed to strip art of emotion and reference, presenting objects simply as objects. Her own work, she insisted, was about feelings—innocence, happiness, the experience of beauty—that she believed were best evoked by the simplest possible means. The spareness of her canvases, in other words, served a purpose quite different from the one critics assumed.$q$, $q$Which choice best describes the main purpose of the text?$q$, $q$["To argue that minimalist art is more emotionally expressive than critics have recognized","To describe the techniques an artist used to create pencil grids and horizontal bands","To explain why an artist rejected a label commonly applied to her work","It presents the main conclusion of the text, which the rest of the text then supports with additional examples of patients with similar injuries."]$q$::jsonb, 2, $q$The text explains that critics called Martin's work minimalist but that she rejected this label because her spare paintings were intended to evoke feelings, unlike minimalism's aims.$q$, null),
  ($q$cs-h19$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Text Structure and Purpose$q$, 3, $q$In 1848, a railroad construction foreman named Phineas Gage survived an accident in which an iron rod passed completely through his skull, destroying much of the left frontal lobe of his brain. He could walk, speak, and remember, but according to his physician, his personality changed: once reliable and well liked, he became impulsive and profane. <u>Gage's case became one of the most frequently cited in the history of neuroscience, offering early evidence that specific regions of the brain are associated with personality and behavior.</u> Recent historians, however, note that the most dramatic accounts of his transformation were written years after his death and may have been exaggerated.$q$, $q$Which choice best describes the function of the underlined sentence in the text as a whole?$q$, $q$["It summarizes the medical treatment Gage received after his accident.","It explains the historical significance of the case described earlier, before the text introduces a reason to question some accounts of it.","It argues that Gage's personality change was caused by factors other than his brain injury.","To explain why large chocolate companies have pledged to pay premiums for cacao certified as sustainably grown, and to evaluate those pledges"]$q$::jsonb, 1, $q$The underlined sentence describes why Gage's case mattered to neuroscience. The final sentence then notes that some accounts of his change may be exaggerated.$q$, null),
  ($q$cs-h20$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Text Structure and Purpose$q$, 3, $q$Most of the world's chocolate comes from cacao grown on small family farms in West Africa, where farmers typically earn a tiny fraction of the price consumers pay for a chocolate bar. Several large companies have pledged to improve farmers' incomes, often by paying small premiums for cacao certified as sustainably grown. Economist Ama Owusu-Ansah argues that such premiums, while welcome, do little to address the underlying problem: farmers have almost no bargaining power, since they sell to intermediaries who set prices. Lasting change, he contends, would require farmers to organize into cooperatives large enough to negotiate directly with buyers.$q$, $q$Which choice best describes the main purpose of the text?$q$, $q$["To present an economist's view that a common approach to a problem is insufficient and to describe the alternative he proposes","To describe the process by which cacao is grown, harvested, and sold to chocolate companies","To argue that consumers should pay more for chocolate in order to raise farmers' incomes","To explain why large companies have pledged to pay premiums for sustainably grown cacao"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$The text describes the problem, notes companies' premiums, then presents Owusu-Ansah's argument that premiums are insufficient and that cooperatives are needed.$q$, null),
  ($q$cs-h21$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Text Structure and Purpose$q$, 3, $q$Urban planners have long assumed that wider roads reduce traffic. Transportation researcher Delia Marsh argues that the opposite is often true. When a road is widened, driving along it becomes faster, at least at first, and people who had been avoiding the route—or taking buses, or not making the trip at all—begin to drive it. <u>Within a few years, Marsh found, traffic on widened roads in the cities she studied typically returned to its original level of congestion, now with more cars.</u> Her conclusion: building more lanes rarely solves congestion and may simply produce more driving.$q$, $q$Which choice best describes the function of the underlined sentence in the text as a whole?$q$, $q$["It describes an exception to the pattern the researcher observed in most cities.","It explains why people choose to drive rather than take public transportation.","It introduces a counterargument to the researcher's view, which the text then refutes by explaining how wider roads attract new drivers.","It presents a finding that supports the researcher's argument against a common assumption."]$q$::jsonb, 3, $q$The underlined sentence reports Marsh's finding that widened roads returned to congestion with more cars—evidence for her argument that widening roads doesn't reduce traffic, contrary to planners' assumption.$q$, null),
  ($q$cs-h22$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Text Structure and Purpose$q$, 3, $q$The following text is from a novel. Teodor, an elderly clockmaker, is speaking to his apprentice.<br><br>"You think the work is in the hands," he said, setting down the tiny brass gear he had been polishing for most of the afternoon. "Everyone thinks so at the beginning. They want to be fast. They want to see a clock running by the end of the week." He held the gear up to the window so that the light caught each of its teeth. "But a clock is mostly patience. The hands only do what the patience allows."$q$, $q$Which choice best describes the main purpose of the text?$q$, $q$["To show that a character regrets having spent his life making clocks instead of pursuing another career","To describe in technical detail how the gears of a clock are made and polished","To convey a character's view that careful, unhurried attention matters more than speed or skill alone","To reveal a character's frustration that his apprentice is unwilling to learn"]$q$::jsonb, 2, $q$Teodor contrasts the beginner's desire for speed with his belief that "a clock is mostly patience," conveying his view that patient attention matters more than quick handiwork.$q$, null),
  ($q$cs-h23$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Text Structure and Purpose$q$, 3, $q$The kakapo, a flightless parrot from New Zealand, breeds only in years when the rimu tree produces an unusually large crop of fruit—which happens just once every two to four years. Conservationists once considered this a problem to be overcome, and they tried feeding the parrots supplementary food to encourage breeding every year. <u>The birds ignored the extra food and continued to wait for the rimu.</u> Researchers now believe the parrots may be responding to a specific nutrient in the fruit, and they are studying whether supplements containing it could succeed where general feeding failed.$q$, $q$Which choice best describes the function of the underlined sentence in the text as a whole?$q$, $q$["It provides evidence that kakapo populations are declining because the rimu tree produces large crops of fruit only once every few years.","It reports the outcome of an effort described in the previous sentence, setting up the new approach described next.","It explains why the rimu tree produces large crops of fruit only every few years.","It suggests that conservationists should stop trying to influence kakapo breeding altogether."]$q$::jsonb, 1, $q$The underlined sentence shows that supplementary feeding failed; the next sentence explains researchers' new idea (a specific nutrient) prompted by that failure.$q$, null),
  ($q$cs-h24$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Text Structure and Purpose$q$, 3, $q$Historians have often portrayed the medieval guilds of Europe as rigid organizations that stifled innovation, protecting established craftsmen by limiting competition. Economic historian Hana Novák offers a more mixed assessment. Guilds did restrict who could practice a trade, she acknowledges, and some resisted new techniques that threatened their members' livelihoods. But they also trained apprentices systematically, enforced quality standards that made goods more reliable, and pooled resources to help members through hard times. In many cases, Novák argues, these functions made guilds engines of skill and stability rather than mere barriers to progress.$q$, $q$Which choice best describes the main purpose of the text?$q$, $q$["To present a historian's argument that a common view of an institution is incomplete","To explain how medieval guilds trained apprentices and enforced quality standards, and why these practices limited competition","To argue that guilds were the main cause of economic growth in medieval Europe","To describe why medieval craftsmen resisted new techniques"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$The text describes the usual negative view of guilds and then presents Novák's more balanced assessment, which acknowledges the drawbacks but adds positive functions—showing that the common view is incomplete.$q$, null),
  ($q$cs-h25$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Text Structure and Purpose$q$, 3, $q$Desert ants of the genus Cataglyphis forage far from their nests across featureless sand, yet they return home in a nearly straight line. How do they know the way? Experiments have shown that the ants count their steps. When researchers attached tiny stilts to the legs of ants that had just reached a food source, the ants overshot their nests on the return trip; when researchers shortened the ants' legs, the ants stopped short. <u>The ants, it seems, keep track of distance by something like a pedometer, combined with a sense of direction from the pattern of polarized light in the sky.</u>$q$, $q$Which choice best describes the function of the underlined sentence in the text as a whole?$q$, $q$["It describes a hypothesis that the experiment described earlier failed to support.","It introduces a question that the rest of the text attempts to answer.","It offers an alternative explanation that contradicts the results of the experiment.","It states a conclusion drawn from the experiment described earlier."]$q$::jsonb, 3, $q$The underlined sentence draws the conclusion (ants track distance by counting steps, plus a sense of direction) suggested by the stilt experiment.$q$, null),
  ($q$cs-h26$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Text Structure and Purpose$q$, 3, $q$In the late nineteenth century, many American cities were plagued by horse manure: tens of thousands of horses pulled carriages, wagons, and streetcars, and the waste piled up faster than it could be removed. Some commentators predicted that cities would become uninhabitable. The problem was not solved by better sanitation but by the automobile, which within a few decades replaced the horse almost entirely. The episode is sometimes cited as a warning against assuming that present trends will continue indefinitely; a seemingly intractable problem was resolved not by confronting it directly but by a technological change that made it irrelevant.$q$, $q$Which choice best describes the overall structure of the text?$q$, $q$["The text describes a historical problem and then argues that the solution created new problems that were even worse.","The text compares two approaches to solving a problem and concludes that the more direct approach was more effective.","The text describes a historical problem, explains how it was ultimately resolved, and then draws a broader lesson from the episode.","The text describes predictions made about a problem and then explains why those predictions came true."]$q$::jsonb, 2, $q$The text describes the manure problem, notes that the automobile resolved it, and then explains the broader lesson (present trends may not continue).$q$, null),
  ($q$cs-h27$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Text Structure and Purpose$q$, 3, $q$The violinist Hyun-ji Park has recorded all six of Bach's sonatas and partitas for solo violin twice, once at age twenty-four and again at fifty. The two recordings could hardly be more different. The first is brilliant and fast, emphasizing the music's technical difficulty. The second is slower and more spacious; Park lingers over harmonies and lets silences stretch. <u>In an interview, Park said that in her youth she wanted to prove she could play the music, while now she simply wants to listen to it as she plays.</u>$q$, $q$Which choice best describes the function of the underlined sentence in the text as a whole?$q$, $q$["It suggests that critics generally prefer the first recording to the second.","It offers the performer's own explanation for the contrast between the two recordings described earlier.","It describes a technical challenge that Park overcame between the two recordings.","It indicates that Park now regrets the faster, more brilliant approach she took when she made her first recording of the music."]$q$::jsonb, 1, $q$After describing the differences between the two recordings, the text gives Park's own account of why they differ—a change in her goals as a performer.$q$, null),
  ($q$cs-h28$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Text Structure and Purpose$q$, 3, $q$Most people assume that a fossil is formed when an animal's bones turn to stone. In fact, fossilization can happen in several ways. Bones may be gradually replaced, molecule by molecule, with minerals from groundwater—the process most people imagine. But an animal can also leave an impression in mud that hardens, preserving the shape of its body without any of the original material. Insects trapped in tree resin can be preserved almost intact in amber. And in some rare cases, such as the woolly mammoths found in Siberian permafrost, freezing preserves even skin and hair.$q$, $q$Which choice best describes the main purpose of the text?$q$, $q$["It corrects a common assumption by describing several different ways that fossils can form.","It argues that fossils preserved in amber are more scientifically valuable than other kinds.","It explains why most animals never become fossils after they die.","It describes the discovery of woolly mammoths in Siberian permafrost."]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$The text begins with a common but incomplete assumption and then describes multiple processes of fossilization, broadening the reader's understanding.$q$, null),
  ($q$cs-h29$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Text Structure and Purpose$q$, 3, $q$The jazz pianist Thelonious Monk was often accused by early critics of playing "wrong" notes; his chords clashed, his rhythms stumbled, and his melodies leapt in unexpected directions. Many musicians, however, recognized that Monk's dissonances were deliberate and carefully placed. <u>Musicians who learned his compositions often reported that the hardest part was resisting the urge to "correct" them.</u> Today, Monk is regarded as one of the most original composers in jazz, and his pieces are standard repertoire for musicians around the world.$q$, $q$Which choice best describes the function of the underlined sentence in the text as a whole?$q$, $q$["It presents an example of the criticism that early reviewers directed at Monk's playing, which the rest of the text goes on to dispute.","It explains why Monk's compositions became standard repertoire for jazz musicians.","It suggests that musicians who worked with Monk found his music impossible to learn.","It supports the claim that Monk's unusual choices were intentional by describing musicians' experiences with his music."]$q$::jsonb, 3, $q$The text claims musicians recognized Monk's dissonances as deliberate; the underlined sentence supports this by noting that musicians learning his pieces had to resist "correcting" them—implying the oddities were intentional.$q$, null),
  ($q$cs-h30$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Text Structure and Purpose$q$, 3, $q$In many traditional societies, elders have been the primary source of practical knowledge: how to find water in a drought, which plants are safe to eat, how to predict a storm. Anthropologist Yusuf Adeyemi argues that rapid environmental change may be eroding this authority. When climate patterns shift faster than they did in the past, he notes, knowledge accumulated over a lifetime may become unreliable—rains no longer arrive when they once did, and familiar plants fail. In several communities Adeyemi studied, young people increasingly turned to weather apps and agricultural extension agents rather than to their elders for advice.$q$, $q$Which choice best describes the main purpose of the text?$q$, $q$["To argue that weather apps are more reliable than traditional knowledge for predicting rainfall","To describe the kinds of practical knowledge that elders in traditional societies possess and explain why that knowledge is always reliable","To present an anthropologist's argument about how environmental change may be affecting the role of elders in traditional societies","To explain why young people in traditional societies are moving to cities"]$q$::jsonb, 2, $q$The text explains that elders traditionally held practical knowledge, then presents Adeyemi's argument that rapid environmental change may be undermining their authority.$q$, null),
  ($q$cs-h31$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Text Structure and Purpose$q$, 3, $q$The giant panda's diet presents a puzzle. Pandas eat almost nothing but bamboo, consuming up to 12 kilograms a day. Yet their digestive systems are those of a carnivore: short, simple guts with none of the specialized chambers that help cows and other grazers break down tough plant material. Pandas digest only about 17 percent of the bamboo they eat. <u>They compensate by eating constantly—up to fourteen hours a day—and by moving as little as possible to conserve energy.</u>$q$, $q$Which choice best describes the function of the underlined sentence in the text as a whole?$q$, $q$["It provides evidence that pandas are evolving a more efficient digestive system.","It explains how pandas manage to survive despite the difficulty described in the preceding sentences.","It describes a behavior that makes pandas vulnerable to predators.","It introduces a new puzzle about the panda's diet—why pandas eat bamboo at all—that the rest of the text does not resolve."]$q$::jsonb, 1, $q$The preceding sentences describe a problem (a carnivore's gut that digests little bamboo); the underlined sentence explains how pandas cope (eating constantly, conserving energy).$q$, null),
  ($q$cs-h32$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Text Structure and Purpose$q$, 3, $q$The following text is from a short story. Ruth has returned to the farmhouse where she grew up, which has been empty for several years.<br><br>The kitchen was smaller than she remembered, as kitchens from childhood always are. The table was still there, pushed against the wall, its oilcloth faded from red to the color of dried roses. She ran her hand along its edge and found the notch her brother had carved the summer he was nine, the summer he had been punished for it, the summer before everything changed. She stood for a long time with her finger in the groove, as if it might tell her something she had forgotten.$q$, $q$Which choice best describes the main purpose of the text?$q$, $q$["To depict a character's encounter with a familiar place that stirs memories of the past","To explain why a character's family decided to leave their farmhouse","To describe a conflict between a character and her brother over the family's property","To show that a character is disappointed by how poorly the farmhouse has been maintained"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Ruth returns to the farmhouse, notices familiar objects, and lingers over the notch her brother carved, recalling the past—the text focuses on memory triggered by place.$q$, null),
  ($q$cs-h33$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Text Structure and Purpose$q$, 3, $q$Sociologist Mira Patel set out to understand why some neighborhoods recover quickly from disasters while others, with similar resources, struggle for years. She expected that income would be the strongest predictor of recovery. Instead, after studying communities affected by floods in three countries, she found that the strongest predictor was what she called "social infrastructure": the density of relationships among neighbors, built through shared spaces such as parks, libraries, and places of worship. Neighborhoods with strong social ties shared information, pooled resources, and checked on vulnerable residents, often well before official help arrived.$q$, $q$Which choice best describes the overall structure of the text?$q$, $q$["The text describes a researcher's question and then explains why her research was unable to answer it.","The text presents two competing theories about disaster recovery and argues that both are incorrect.","The text describes how neighborhoods recover from disasters and then recommends specific policies for governments.","The text describes a researcher's question, her initial expectation, and the different finding that her research produced."]$q$::jsonb, 3, $q$The text poses Patel's question, states her expectation (income), and then reports her unexpected finding (social infrastructure) and how it worked.$q$, null),
  ($q$cs-h34$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Text Structure and Purpose$q$, 3, $q$Glass appears to be a solid, but it lacks the ordered internal structure of most solids; its molecules are arranged randomly, like those of a liquid. This fact gave rise to a popular claim that old window panes are thicker at the bottom because glass flows slowly downward over centuries. <u>Physicists have shown, however, that at room temperature glass would take far longer than the age of the universe to flow noticeably.</u> The uneven thickness of old windows is now attributed to the way glass was made at the time: early techniques produced panes of uneven thickness, and glaziers often installed them with the thicker edge at the bottom.$q$, $q$Which choice best describes the function of the underlined sentence in the text as a whole?$q$, $q$["It provides evidence for the claim that glass is a liquid rather than a solid.","It explains why glass molecules are arranged randomly.","It refutes a popular claim described in the previous sentence.","It describes a technique that early glassmakers used to make panes of uniform thickness."]$q$::jsonb, 2, $q$The underlined sentence shows that glass cannot flow noticeably at room temperature, disproving the claim that old panes thickened at the bottom by flowing.$q$, null),
  ($q$cs-h35$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Text Structure and Purpose$q$, 3, $q$For centuries, the ruins of Angkor in Cambodia were thought to consist of a cluster of temples surrounded by dense jungle. In 2012, archaeologists used lidar—laser scanning from aircraft that can see through forest canopy—to map the area. The scans revealed a vast network of roads, canals, and residential neighborhoods hidden beneath the trees, showing that the temples had been the center of an enormous urban landscape. The findings have changed estimates of Angkor's population and raised new questions about why such a large city was eventually abandoned.$q$, $q$Which choice best describes the main purpose of the text?$q$, $q$["To argue that Angkor was abandoned because its population grew too large to support","To describe how a new technology revealed that an ancient site was larger and more complex than previously believed","To explain how lidar technology works and why it is useful to archaeologists studying sites that are hidden by dense forest canopy","To compare Angkor with other ancient cities discovered using lidar"]$q$::jsonb, 1, $q$The text describes the earlier view of Angkor and how lidar revealed a much larger urban landscape, changing understanding of the site.$q$, null),
  ($q$cs-h36$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Text Structure and Purpose$q$, 3, $q$In the 1960s, psychologist Walter Mischel offered young children a choice: one marshmallow now, or two if they could wait fifteen minutes. Follow-up studies found that children who waited tended to have better outcomes years later, and the experiment became famous as evidence that self-control predicts success. <u>A larger study in 2018, however, found that once children's family background and early cognitive abilities were taken into account, the ability to wait predicted much less.</u> Some researchers now suggest that waiting may reflect a child's circumstances—such as whether promises from adults are usually kept—as much as an inborn trait.$q$, $q$Which choice best describes the function of the underlined sentence in the text as a whole?$q$, $q$["It presents a finding that complicates the earlier interpretation of the experiment.","It provides additional evidence that a child's ability to wait strongly predicts later success, regardless of family background.","It describes the original design of the marshmallow experiment.","It explains why the original experiment became famous."]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$The underlined sentence reports that the predictive power of waiting shrinks after accounting for background, challenging the earlier interpretation; the next sentence offers an alternative explanation.$q$, null),
  ($q$cs-h37$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Text Structure and Purpose$q$, 3, $q$Sea ice in the Arctic reflects most of the sunlight that strikes it back into space. Open ocean water, by contrast, is dark and absorbs most of the sunlight that reaches it. As the climate warms and sea ice melts, more open water is exposed, which absorbs more heat, which in turn melts more ice. Climate scientists call this a positive feedback loop, and it is one reason the Arctic is warming roughly four times faster than the global average.$q$, $q$Which choice best describes the main purpose of the text?$q$, $q$["It argues that efforts to reduce global warming should focus primarily on the Arctic.","It compares the effects of sea ice and open ocean on global weather patterns.","It describes how scientists measure the amount of sunlight that sea ice reflects and compare it with the amount absorbed by open water.","It explains a mechanism that helps account for the unusually rapid warming of a particular region."]$q$::jsonb, 3, $q$The text explains the ice-albedo feedback loop and connects it to the Arctic's rapid warming.$q$, null),
  ($q$cs-h38$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Text Structure and Purpose$q$, 3, $q$Rice farmers in parts of Bangladesh have begun raising ducks in their flooded paddies. The ducks eat insects and weeds that would otherwise damage the rice, reducing the need for pesticides and herbicides. Their droppings fertilize the fields. And as they paddle through the water, they stir up the soil, which helps oxygen reach the rice plants' roots. The farmers, meanwhile, gain a second source of income from selling ducks and eggs. <u>Not every farm can adopt the system, however; it requires fields that stay flooded for much of the growing season and access to markets for the ducks.</u>$q$, $q$Which choice best describes the function of the underlined sentence in the text as a whole?$q$, $q$["It provides an additional benefit of raising ducks in rice paddies.","It explains why the ducks eat insects and weeds rather than rice plants.","It qualifies the benefits described earlier by noting conditions that limit the system's use.","It argues that the duck-rice system, despite its benefits, is less profitable than conventional rice farming in most of Bangladesh."]$q$::jsonb, 2, $q$After listing many benefits, the underlined sentence notes limitations—the system requires certain conditions—qualifying the earlier positive account.$q$, null),
  ($q$cs-h39$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Text Structure and Purpose$q$, 3, $q$Many people believe that Napoleon Bonaparte was unusually short. In fact, he was about 1.69 meters tall, roughly average for a Frenchman of his time. The myth may have several sources. French and English units of measurement differed, and his height recorded in French inches was misread by English speakers as a smaller figure. British cartoonists, eager to mock an enemy, routinely drew him as a tiny, tantrum-prone figure. And he was often seen surrounded by his Imperial Guard, whose members were selected in part for their height.$q$, $q$Which choice best describes the main purpose of the text?$q$, $q$["To argue that Napoleon's height affected his military decisions","To debunk a popular belief and suggest reasons why the belief arose","To compare the systems of measurement used in France and England","To describe the role of political cartoons in British society"]$q$::jsonb, 1, $q$The text states the myth, corrects it with Napoleon's actual height, and then explains possible sources of the misconception.$q$, null),
  ($q$cs-h40$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Text Structure and Purpose$q$, 3, $q$In the 1840s, the Hungarian physician Ignaz Semmelweis noticed that women who gave birth in a hospital ward staffed by doctors died of fever far more often than those in a ward staffed by midwives. He observed that the doctors often came directly from performing autopsies. Suspecting that they carried some kind of contamination on their hands, he required them to wash with a chlorine solution. Deaths in the doctors' ward fell dramatically. <u>Yet many of his colleagues rejected his conclusions, offended by the suggestion that physicians themselves were spreading disease.</u>$q$, $q$Which choice best describes the function of the underlined sentence in the text as a whole?$q$, $q$["It describes a reaction to the physician's findings that contrasts with the success those findings had demonstrated.","It presents additional evidence supporting the physician's conclusion that doctors were carrying contamination from autopsies.","It explains how the physician developed the chlorine solution he used.","It suggests that the physician's findings were later shown to be mistaken."]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$The text describes Semmelweis's successful intervention; the underlined sentence, introduced with "Yet," contrasts this success with his colleagues' rejection.$q$, null),
  ($q$cs-h41$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Text Structure and Purpose$q$, 3, $q$Beginning in the 1970s, the city of Curitiba in Brazil developed a bus system that, by the 1990s, functioned much like a subway: buses ran in dedicated lanes, passengers paid before boarding at raised tube-shaped stations, and doors opened at platform level, allowing rapid boarding. The system cost a small fraction of what a subway would have cost and carried more passengers per day than many subways. It became a model for "bus rapid transit" systems in cities from Bogotá to Jakarta, many of which could not afford the expense of building underground rail.$q$, $q$Which choice best describes the main purpose of the text?$q$, $q$["To argue that subways are no longer necessary in large cities","To compare the bus rapid transit systems of Curitiba, Bogotá, and Jakarta and determine which carries the most passengers","To explain why passengers prefer buses to subways","To describe an innovative transit system and its influence on other cities"]$q$::jsonb, 3, $q$The text describes Curitiba's bus system and notes that it became a model for other cities.$q$, null),
  ($q$cs-h42$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Text Structure and Purpose$q$, 3, $q$For much of the nineteenth century, doctors believed that disease was spread by miasma—bad air from decaying matter. The theory was wrong, but it led to some beneficial reforms. Cities built sewers to remove waste and drained swamps to eliminate foul odors, and these measures did reduce the spread of diseases such as cholera and malaria—though for reasons the reformers did not understand. Only later did germ theory explain that the true causes were microorganisms carried in contaminated water and by mosquitoes.$q$, $q$Which choice best describes the main purpose of the text?$q$, $q$["To argue that germ theory was developed by scientists in direct response to the failures of the reforms based on miasma theory","To describe how nineteenth-century cities built sewer systems","To show that a mistaken theory nonetheless prompted actions that had beneficial effects","To compare the spread of cholera with the spread of malaria"]$q$::jsonb, 2, $q$The text explains that although miasma theory was wrong, it led to sewers and drainage that reduced disease, for reasons later explained by germ theory.$q$, null),
  ($q$cs-h43$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Text Structure and Purpose$q$, 3, $q$The following text is from a novel. Amara has just arrived in a new city after leaving her hometown.<br><br>Everything was louder here. The trains screamed into the station, the vendors shouted over one another, and even the pigeons seemed to quarrel. Amara stood on the platform with her single suitcase and let the noise wash over her. At home, she had always been told to speak softly. <u>Here, she thought, no one would ever hear her unless she learned to raise her voice.</u> The idea frightened her, and then, to her surprise, it didn't.$q$, $q$Which choice best describes the function of the underlined sentence in the text as a whole?$q$, $q$["It explains why the character decided to leave her hometown, where she had always been told to speak softly, for the city.","It reveals a character's realization about how she may have to change, setting up her final shift in feeling.","It describes the specific sounds of the city that the character finds most unpleasant.","It suggests that the character plans to return home as soon as possible."]$q$::jsonb, 1, $q$The underlined sentence shows Amara recognizing that she must adapt (raise her voice); the final sentence shows her fear turning into something else.$q$, null),
  ($q$cs-h44$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Text Structure and Purpose$q$, 3, $q$Economists have long debated whether raising the minimum wage reduces employment. Traditional theory predicts that it should: if labor becomes more expensive, employers will hire fewer workers. In a widely cited study, two economists compared fast-food restaurants in New Jersey, which had just raised its minimum wage, with restaurants in neighboring Pennsylvania, which had not. They found no decline in employment in New Jersey relative to Pennsylvania. The study did not end the debate, but it prompted many economists to reconsider the assumption that higher wages always cost jobs.$q$, $q$Which choice best describes the overall structure of the text?$q$, $q$["The text introduces a debate, presents a theoretical prediction, and then describes a study whose results challenged that prediction.","The text describes a study and then explains why its methods were flawed.","The text compares economic conditions in two states and recommends policies for both.","The text presents two theories about the minimum wage and then argues that a study proved one of them to be correct in all cases."]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$The text introduces the minimum-wage debate, states the traditional prediction, describes the New Jersey–Pennsylvania study, and notes how it challenged the prediction.$q$, null),
  ($q$cs-h45$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Text Structure and Purpose$q$, 3, $q$Most trees in temperate forests drop their leaves in autumn, but the reason is not simply that the leaves are no longer useful. As days shorten and temperatures fall, a tree begins breaking down the green chlorophyll in its leaves and reabsorbing valuable nutrients such as nitrogen. <u>The yellow and orange pigments that were present in the leaves all along, but masked by the chlorophyll, become visible.</u> Finally, the tree seals off each leaf with a layer of cells at the base of its stem, and the leaf falls.$q$, $q$Which choice best describes the function of the underlined sentence in the text as a whole?$q$, $q$["It identifies the main reason that trees drop their leaves in autumn, which is that the leaves' pigments are no longer useful.","It describes a step that occurs after the leaves have fallen from the tree.","It presents an exception to the process that most trees follow in autumn.","It explains a visible change that results from the process described in the previous sentence."]$q$::jsonb, 3, $q$The underlined sentence explains why leaves turn yellow and orange: breaking down chlorophyll (described previously) reveals pigments that were masked.$q$, null),
  ($q$cs-h46$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Text Structure and Purpose$q$, 3, $q$The ancient Library of Alexandria is often described as having been destroyed in a single catastrophic fire. Historians now believe its decline was more gradual. Funding from the Ptolemaic rulers dwindled; scholars were expelled during political purges in the second century BCE; a fire set during Julius Caesar's siege in 48 BCE may have damaged part of the collection; and later conflicts and neglect further diminished it. By the time the last remnants disappeared, the library had long since ceased to be the great center of learning it once was.$q$, $q$Which choice best describes the main purpose of the text?$q$, $q$["To argue that Julius Caesar's siege of Alexandria in 48 BCE was primarily responsible for destroying the library's collection","To describe the kinds of texts that were kept in the Library of Alexandria","To challenge a popular account of an event by describing a more complex historical process","To explain why the Ptolemaic rulers founded the Library of Alexandria"]$q$::jsonb, 2, $q$The text contrasts the popular story of a single fire with historians' view of a gradual decline caused by multiple factors.$q$, null),
  ($q$cs-h47$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Text Structure and Purpose$q$, 3, $q$The violin maker Yuki Tanabe spent three years trying to reproduce the varnish used by Italian craftsmen in the eighteenth century. Chemical analysis of old instruments had identified its main ingredients, but every batch she made looked dull and lifeless. The breakthrough came when she read an old letter describing how varnished instruments were left to dry for months in direct sunlight. <u>When Tanabe did the same, the varnish developed the deep, glowing color she had been seeking.</u>$q$, $q$Which choice best describes the function of the underlined sentence in the text as a whole?$q$, $q$["It explains why chemical analysis failed to identify the varnish's main ingredients.","It describes the result of applying an idea from the letter mentioned in the previous sentence.","It reveals that the violin maker eventually abandoned her effort to reproduce the varnish.","It introduces a second problem that the violin maker encountered after her breakthrough."]$q$::jsonb, 1, $q$The previous sentence describes the letter's detail about drying instruments in sunlight; the underlined sentence describes the successful result when Tanabe tried this.$q$, null),
  ($q$cs-h48$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Text Structure and Purpose$q$, 3, $q$Seed banks store frozen seeds to protect crop diversity, but some important plants, such as cacao, coconut, and avocado, produce seeds that cannot survive freezing or drying. For these species, scientists must use other methods. Some maintain living collections in field gene banks—orchards of hundreds of varieties planted side by side—though these are vulnerable to disease and storms. Others are experimenting with cryopreservation, freezing tiny pieces of plant tissue in liquid nitrogen and later regrowing whole plants from them.$q$, $q$Which choice best describes the main purpose of the text?$q$, $q$["To explain why some plants cannot be preserved by conventional seed banks and to describe alternative approaches","To argue that field gene banks are more effective than cryopreservation for preserving seeds that cannot survive freezing","To describe how conventional seed banks freeze and store seeds","To explain why cacao, coconut, and avocado are important crops"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$The text explains that some seeds can't survive freezing and describes two alternative methods (field gene banks and cryopreservation).$q$, null),
  ($q$cs-h49$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Text Structure and Purpose$q$, 3, $q$The city's first public library opened in 1903 with 4,000 books. Its founder, a retired schoolteacher, insisted that it be free and open to everyone, a radical idea in a town where most institutions were segregated by race and class. <u>Within a year, more than a third of the town's adult residents held library cards.</u> The library's success inspired the creation of reading rooms in three surrounding villages and, later, a mobile library that brought books to farms by horse-drawn wagon.$q$, $q$Which choice best describes the function of the underlined sentence in the text as a whole?$q$, $q$["It explains why the founder insisted that the library be free and open to everyone.","It describes the kinds of books the library held when it first opened.","It suggests that many of the town's residents opposed the founder's decision to make the library free and open to everyone.","It provides evidence of the library's early popularity, which helps explain the developments described afterward."]$q$::jsonb, 3, $q$The underlined sentence shows the library's quick success, which the following sentence says inspired further reading rooms and a mobile library.$q$, null),
  ($q$cs-h50$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Text Structure and Purpose$q$, 3, $q$For decades, nutrition guidelines recommended that people avoid eating eggs because of the cholesterol in their yolks. The advice rested on the reasonable assumption that eating cholesterol would raise the level of cholesterol in the blood. Later research, however, found that for most people, dietary cholesterol has only a modest effect on blood cholesterol; the liver adjusts its own production to compensate. In 2015, U.S. dietary guidelines dropped the specific limit on cholesterol intake, though they continued to recommend eating it in moderation.$q$, $q$Which choice best describes the main purpose of the text?$q$, $q$["To argue that eggs should be part of every healthy diet","To explain how the liver produces cholesterol","To describe how a dietary recommendation changed as research challenged the assumption behind it","To criticize nutrition researchers for making recommendations about eggs that were based on assumptions rather than evidence"]$q$::jsonb, 2, $q$The text describes the original recommendation, its underlying assumption, later research that challenged it, and the resulting change in guidelines.$q$, null),
  ($q$cs-h51$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Text Structure and Purpose$q$, 3, $q$Linguists once assumed that the languages of small, isolated communities would be simpler than those of large societies, since fewer speakers would have less need for complex grammar. The evidence suggests otherwise. <u>Some of the most grammatically intricate languages known—with dozens of verb forms marking subtle distinctions of time, certainty, and source of information—are spoken by communities of only a few hundred people.</u> Indeed, some researchers now propose the reverse: that languages spoken by many adult learners tend to become simpler over time, as irregular forms that are hard to learn gradually disappear.$q$, $q$Which choice best describes the function of the underlined sentence in the text as a whole?$q$, $q$["It presents an assumption that the rest of the text supports with examples from several languages.","It provides evidence against an assumption described earlier, preparing for an alternative proposal.","It explains why the languages of small communities tend to lose irregular verb forms over time.","It describes a method linguists use to measure how complex the grammar of a language is."]$q$::jsonb, 1, $q$The underlined sentence offers counterevidence to the assumption that small-community languages are simpler; the final sentence then presents an alternative hypothesis.$q$, null),
  ($q$cs-h52$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Text Structure and Purpose$q$, 3, $q$When the Brooklyn Bridge opened in 1883, rumors spread that it was unstable, and a stampede on its walkway killed twelve people. To reassure the public, the circus showman P. T. Barnum led a parade of twenty-one elephants across the bridge in 1884. The bridge held, and confidence in it grew. The episode illustrates a persistent challenge for engineers: a structure's safety depends not only on its design but also, in practical terms, on whether people trust it enough to use it as intended.$q$, $q$Which choice best describes the main purpose of the text?$q$, $q$["To use a historical episode to illustrate a broader point about the relationship between public trust and engineering","To argue that P. T. Barnum's parade was the main reason the Brooklyn Bridge became a successful part of New York's transportation system","To describe the design features that made the Brooklyn Bridge strong enough to support the weight of twenty-one elephants","To explain why a stampede occurred on the Brooklyn Bridge shortly after it opened and how it could have been prevented"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$The text describes the Barnum episode and then draws a general lesson: a structure's practical safety depends on public trust as well as design.$q$, null),
  ($q$cs-h53$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Text Structure and Purpose$q$, 3, $q$The following text is from a novel. Hamid, a retired schoolteacher, is walking through the market where he once shopped with his late wife.<br><br>The fruit seller still called out the same prices in the same singsong voice, as if no time had passed at all. Hamid bought three oranges, though he would eat only one. Leila had always bought three—one for each of them and one, she said, for luck. <u>He did not believe in luck, but he found that he could not bring himself to buy only two.</u>$q$, $q$Which choice best describes the function of the underlined sentence in the text as a whole?$q$, $q$["It shows that a character has come to believe in luck since the death of someone close to him.","It explains why a character, who once shopped at the market with his late wife, no longer enjoys visiting it.","It suggests that a character buys extra oranges because the fruit seller has raised his prices.","It reveals that a character continues a habit associated with someone he has lost, despite not sharing her belief."]$q$::jsonb, 3, $q$Hamid buys three oranges as Leila did, though he doesn't believe in luck—the habit honors her memory, not a belief.$q$, null),
  ($q$cs-h54$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Text Structure and Purpose$q$, 3, $q$Coral reefs cover less than one percent of the ocean floor but support roughly a quarter of all marine species. This extraordinary diversity depends on a partnership between corals and microscopic algae that live inside their tissues, supplying the corals with food through photosynthesis. When water becomes too warm, the partnership breaks down: the corals expel the algae and turn white, a process called bleaching. <u>If temperatures fall quickly enough, the algae can return and the coral may survive; if not, the coral starves.</u>$q$, $q$Which choice best describes the function of the underlined sentence in the text as a whole?$q$, $q$["It describes the main reason that coral reefs support so many species of marine life.","It introduces a method scientists have developed to prevent coral bleaching in warming water.","It explains the possible outcomes of the process described in the previous sentence.","It provides evidence that coral bleaching occurs only in waters that are unusually polluted."]$q$::jsonb, 2, $q$The previous sentence describes bleaching; the underlined sentence explains what can happen afterward depending on temperature.$q$, null),
  ($q$cs-h55$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Text Structure and Purpose$q$, 3, $q$Philosopher Iris Holloway argues that we misunderstand boredom when we treat it merely as an unpleasant feeling to be avoided. Boredom, she contends, is a signal: it tells us that our current activity fails to engage our abilities or interests, much as hunger signals a need for food. Seen this way, the constant availability of distraction—phones, streaming video, endless feeds—may do us harm, not because entertainment is bad in itself but because it silences a signal that might otherwise prompt us to seek more meaningful activities.$q$, $q$Which choice best describes the main purpose of the text?$q$, $q$["To argue that people should give up their phones and streaming video in order to avoid boredom","To present a philosopher's view that a common feeling serves a useful purpose that modern distractions may undermine","To compare the feeling of boredom with the feeling of hunger and to explain how the body produces each of these feelings","To describe the results of studies showing that people who are often bored tend to be less successful"]$q$::jsonb, 1, $q$The text presents Holloway's argument that boredom is a useful signal and that constant distraction may silence it.$q$, null),
  ($q$cs-h56$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Text Structure and Purpose$q$, 3, $q$The ancient Romans built roads that lasted for centuries, many of them still visible today. Their durability came from careful layering. Builders first dug a trench and filled it with large stones; on top came layers of smaller stones and gravel bound with lime mortar; finally, the surface was paved with tightly fitted blocks. <u>The roads were also slightly curved, higher in the center than at the edges, so that rainwater would drain away rather than pool and seep into the foundation.</u>$q$, $q$Which choice best describes the function of the underlined sentence in the text as a whole?$q$, $q$["It describes an additional design feature that contributed to the roads' durability.","It explains why Roman roads were built in straight lines across the landscape.","It presents a weakness in Roman road construction that caused many roads to fail.","It introduces a technique that the Romans borrowed from earlier civilizations."]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$After describing the layered construction, the underlined sentence adds another feature (the curved surface for drainage) that helped the roads last.$q$, null),
  ($q$cs-h57$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Text Structure and Purpose$q$, 3, $q$In 1977, two spacecraft named Voyager were launched to study the outer planets. Each carried a gold-plated record containing sounds and images from Earth—greetings in fifty-five languages, music from many cultures, the sound of rain and of a human heartbeat—in case the craft were ever found by intelligent beings. The chance of such a discovery is extremely remote; space is vast, and the records will take tens of thousands of years to approach another star. The records, some have argued, may matter less as messages to others than as a statement of how humanity chose to represent itself.$q$, $q$Which choice best describes the overall structure of the text?$q$, $q$["The text describes an object and then argues that it should be replaced with a more effective way of communicating with other beings.","The text describes the mission of two spacecraft and then explains how the spacecraft's discoveries changed scientists' understanding of the planets.","The text describes the contents of a record and then explains why scientists chose each of the sounds and images that it contains.","The text describes an object, notes how unlikely it is to serve its stated purpose, and then suggests a different way to understand its significance."]$q$::jsonb, 3, $q$The text describes the Golden Records, explains that discovery by aliens is very unlikely, and then offers another interpretation of their significance.$q$, null),
  ($q$cs-h58$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Text Structure and Purpose$q$, 3, $q$Historians studying the diaries of nineteenth-century women have often focused on the entries that describe dramatic events: births, deaths, journeys, and wars. Historian Clara Whitfield argues that the most revealing entries are often the most routine. Notes about the price of flour, the weather, or which neighbor came to help with the washing, she contends, reveal the economic pressures and networks of mutual aid that shaped women's daily lives—subjects the writers rarely discussed directly because they took them for granted.$q$, $q$Which choice best describes the main purpose of the text?$q$, $q$["To argue that nineteenth-century women's diaries are more reliable than other sources from the same period","To describe the dramatic events that nineteenth-century women most often wrote about in their diaries","To present a historian's argument that overlooked material in a type of source can reveal important information","To explain why nineteenth-century women rarely discussed economic pressures directly in their diaries"]$q$::jsonb, 2, $q$The text contrasts historians' usual focus with Whitfield's argument that routine entries reveal important aspects of women's lives.$q$, null),
  ($q$cs-h59$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Text Structure and Purpose$q$, 3, $q$Cheetahs are the fastest land animals, capable of reaching speeds above 100 kilometers per hour. Yet speed alone does not explain their success as hunters. <u>A study that fitted wild cheetahs with tracking collars found that their hunts rarely involved top speed; instead, the cats relied on rapid acceleration and the ability to change direction sharply.</u> Their long tails, which act as counterweights during turns, and their semi-retractable claws, which grip the ground like cleats, may matter as much as their raw speed.$q$, $q$Which choice best describes the function of the underlined sentence in the text as a whole?$q$, $q$["It provides evidence that cheetahs are the fastest land animals in the world.","It presents evidence supporting the claim that speed alone does not account for the cheetah's hunting success.","It explains why cheetahs are less successful hunters than other large cats.","It describes a weakness of tracking collars as a method for studying how cheetahs hunt, which led researchers to doubt the cats' speed."]$q$::jsonb, 1, $q$The text claims speed alone doesn't explain cheetahs' success; the underlined sentence supports this with collar data showing acceleration and turning mattered more.$q$, null),
  ($q$cs-h60$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Text Structure and Purpose$q$, 3, $q$The following text is from an essay about learning to cook.<br><br>My grandmother never measured anything. When I asked how much salt to add, she would say "enough," and when I asked how long to knead the dough, she would say "until it's ready." For years I found this maddening. I wanted rules, numbers, a recipe I could follow. <u>It took me a long time to understand that she was not withholding the recipe from me; she was teaching me to pay attention.</u>$q$, $q$Which choice best describes the function of the underlined sentence in the text as a whole?$q$, $q$["It marks a shift from the author's past frustration to a later understanding of what her grandmother was doing.","It reveals that the author's grandmother refused to share her recipes because she wanted to keep them secret.","It explains why the author eventually stopped cooking with her grandmother and began following written recipes.","It suggests that the author still finds her grandmother's way of cooking frustrating and difficult to learn."]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$The underlined sentence moves from the author's earlier frustration to her later realization that the grandmother was teaching attentiveness.$q$, null),
  ($q$cs-h61$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Text Structure and Purpose$q$, 3, $q$For decades, the standard treatment for a sprained ankle was rest, ice, compression, and elevation. Recent research has questioned parts of this approach. Some studies suggest that applying ice may slow healing by reducing the inflammation that brings repair cells to the injury. Others have found that patients who begin gentle movement within a few days recover faster than those who rest completely. Many sports-medicine specialists now recommend a modified approach, though they disagree about how much ice, if any, is beneficial.$q$, $q$Which choice best describes the main purpose of the text?$q$, $q$["To argue that applying ice to a sprained ankle is always harmful and should be avoided completely","To explain how inflammation brings repair cells to an injury and why this process is necessary","To compare the recovery times of patients who rest a sprained ankle completely with those of patients who treat it with ice","To describe how research has led to changes in a standard medical recommendation, though some questions remain unresolved"]$q$::jsonb, 3, $q$The text describes the traditional treatment, research challenging it, and the resulting modified approach, noting continuing disagreement.$q$, null),
  ($q$cs-h62$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Text Structure and Purpose$q$, 3, $q$In the 1930s, the U.S. government hired writers to collect the life stories of people who had been enslaved. The resulting interviews, more than 2,300 in all, are an invaluable record. <u>Historians caution, however, that the circumstances of the interviews shaped what was said.</u> Most interviewers were white, many interviewees were elderly and poor, and in the segregated South, speaking frankly to a white stranger about the cruelty of slavery could be dangerous. Some accounts may therefore present a gentler picture of slavery than the speakers privately held.$q$, $q$Which choice best describes the function of the underlined sentence in the text as a whole?$q$, $q$["It summarizes the main reason historians consider the interviews an invaluable record.","It presents a criticism of the government program that collected the interviews, which the rest of the text then refutes.","It introduces a qualification about a historical source that the rest of the text explains.","It explains why the government decided to collect interviews in the 1930s."]$q$::jsonb, 2, $q$After calling the interviews invaluable, the underlined sentence introduces a caution; the following sentences explain the circumstances that may have shaped the accounts.$q$, null),
  ($q$cs-h63$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Text Structure and Purpose$q$, 3, $q$Beavers are often called ecosystem engineers. By building dams, they convert streams into ponds and wetlands, which slow the flow of water, trap sediment, and create habitat for fish, amphibians, and birds. During droughts, beaver ponds can keep water on the landscape when surrounding areas are dry; during wildfires, they can form green refuges where animals shelter. Some land managers in the western United States have begun reintroducing beavers or building artificial "beaver dam analogs" to restore degraded streams.$q$, $q$Which choice best describes the main purpose of the text?$q$, $q$["To argue that beavers should be reintroduced into every degraded stream in the western United States to prevent wildfires","To describe the ecological effects of an animal's behavior and how people are making use of them","To explain how beavers choose the locations where they build their dams","To compare the habitats created by beavers with those created by other animals"]$q$::jsonb, 1, $q$The text describes the benefits of beaver dams and notes that land managers are reintroducing beavers or imitating their dams.$q$, null),
  ($q$cs-h64$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Text Structure and Purpose$q$, 3, $q$The first maps of the ocean floor, made in the 1950s by geologist Marie Tharp, revealed a deep valley running along the center of the Mid-Atlantic Ridge. Tharp argued that the valley was a rift where the seafloor was being pulled apart—evidence for the then-controversial idea that continents drift. Her supervisor initially dismissed this interpretation as "girl talk." <u>Within a decade, however, accumulating evidence had made seafloor spreading a cornerstone of the theory of plate tectonics.</u>$q$, $q$Which choice best describes the function of the underlined sentence in the text as a whole?$q$, $q$["It shows that an interpretation that was initially dismissed came to be widely accepted.","It explains how the Mid-Atlantic Ridge was formed by the drifting of the continents.","It describes the methods Tharp used to create the first maps of the ocean floor.","It suggests that Tharp's supervisor eventually took credit for her interpretation."]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$The underlined sentence, beginning with "however," shows that the dismissed interpretation became central to plate tectonics.$q$, null),
  ($q$cs-h65$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Text Structure and Purpose$q$, 3, $q$Economists distinguish between two kinds of unemployment. Cyclical unemployment rises and falls with the economy: during a recession, businesses lay off workers, and when the economy recovers, many are rehired. Structural unemployment is different. It occurs when workers' skills no longer match the jobs available—when, for instance, factories close permanently because of automation. Structural unemployment may persist even when the economy is growing, since the jobs that return are often different from those that were lost.$q$, $q$Which choice best describes the overall structure of the text?$q$, $q$["The text describes a problem and then proposes several government policies that could help to solve it.","The text presents a theory about unemployment and then describes evidence that has challenged the theory.","The text describes the causes of recessions and then explains how businesses respond to economic downturns.","The text introduces a distinction and then explains each of the two categories, emphasizing how they differ."]$q$::jsonb, 3, $q$The text introduces the distinction, explains cyclical unemployment, then explains structural unemployment and how it differs.$q$, null),
  ($q$cs-h66$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Text Structure and Purpose$q$, 3, $q$The sculptor Ruth Asawa is known for her hanging wire sculptures, intricate forms that look like nested lanterns or seed pods. She learned the looping technique she used from craftspeople in Mexico who made wire baskets for carrying eggs. <u>Asawa rejected the idea that craft was inferior to fine art, treating a humble basket-making technique as a legitimate basis for serious sculpture.</u> Her sculptures, once dismissed by some critics as decorative, now hang in major museums around the world.$q$, $q$Which choice best describes the function of the underlined sentence in the text as a whole?$q$, $q$["It explains why the artist traveled to Mexico to learn how to make wire baskets.","It describes the criticism that some critics directed at the artist's sculptures.","It conveys the artist's view of a distinction, a view that her work's later reception seems to support.","It suggests that the artist preferred making traditional egg baskets to making the wire sculptures that now hang in museums."]$q$::jsonb, 2, $q$The underlined sentence gives Asawa's view that craft is not inferior to fine art; the final sentence notes that her once-"decorative" work now hangs in major museums, which supports her view.$q$, null),
  ($q$cs-h67$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Text Structure and Purpose$q$, 3, $q$Many people believe that humans use only ten percent of their brains. Brain-imaging studies show otherwise: over the course of a day, virtually every region of the brain is active, and even simple tasks engage multiple areas at once. The brain is also costly to run, consuming about twenty percent of the body's energy despite making up only about two percent of its weight. An organ that expensive, neuroscientists point out, would be unlikely to evolve if most of it went unused.$q$, $q$Which choice best describes the main purpose of the text?$q$, $q$["To explain how brain-imaging studies measure which areas of the brain are active","To refute a popular belief by presenting evidence and an argument against it","To argue that people could improve their intelligence by using more of their brains","To compare the energy needs of the brain with those of the body's other organs"]$q$::jsonb, 1, $q$The text states the ten-percent myth and refutes it with imaging evidence and an evolutionary argument about the brain's energy cost.$q$, null),
  ($q$cs-h68$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Text Structure and Purpose$q$, 3, $q$In the 1920s, the botanist Nikolai Vavilov traveled across five continents collecting seeds of crop plants and their wild relatives. He noticed that the diversity of many crops was concentrated in a few regions—the mountains of Ethiopia, the Andes, the Fertile Crescent—and proposed that these were the places where the crops had first been domesticated. <u>His seed collection, stored in what is now St. Petersburg, became one of the first seed banks in the world.</u> During the siege of the city in World War II, scientists guarding the collection starved rather than eat the seeds.$q$, $q$Which choice best describes the function of the underlined sentence in the text as a whole?$q$, $q$["It introduces the significance of the collection, which the next sentence illustrates with a dramatic episode.","It presents the main evidence supporting Vavilov's theory that crops were first domesticated in a few regions of the world.","It explains why the diversity of many crops is concentrated in mountainous regions.","It describes a problem that prevented Vavilov from completing his collection of seeds."]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$The underlined sentence identifies the importance of the seed collection; the final sentence describes the scientists' sacrifice to protect it.$q$, null),
  ($q$cs-h69$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Text Structure and Purpose$q$, 3, $q$Musicologist Teresa Lindqvist argues that the familiar distinction between "folk" and "classical" music obscures how often the two have borrowed from each other. Many celebrated classical composers built works on folk melodies, she notes, while folk musicians adopted instruments, forms, and harmonies from concert music. Treating the categories as separate, Lindqvist contends, has led scholars to overlook this exchange and to undervalue the musicians—often anonymous—who sustained the folk traditions that classical composers drew upon.$q$, $q$Which choice best describes the main purpose of the text?$q$, $q$["To argue that classical music is more sophisticated than folk music because it uses more complex harmonies and forms","To describe how folk musicians adopted instruments and forms from the composers of classical music","To explain why the musicians who sustained folk traditions have usually remained anonymous","To present a scholar's argument that a common distinction hides an important relationship and has had negative consequences"]$q$::jsonb, 3, $q$The text presents Lindqvist's argument that the folk/classical distinction obscures mutual borrowing and has led scholars to undervalue folk musicians.$q$, null),
  ($q$cs-h70$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Text Structure and Purpose$q$, 3, $q$When a hummingbird drinks nectar, its tongue was long thought to work like a straw, drawing liquid upward through capillary action. High-speed video has revealed a different mechanism. <u>As the tongue enters the nectar, its two thin tips spread apart and then curl inward, trapping the liquid and carrying it into the bird's mouth.</u> Because this process is faster than capillary action, it allows the bird to drink up to twenty times per second.$q$, $q$Which choice best describes the function of the underlined sentence in the text as a whole?$q$, $q$["It explains why scientists originally believed the tongue worked like a straw.","It presents evidence that hummingbirds drink more nectar than other birds do.","It describes the mechanism revealed by the method mentioned in the previous sentence.","It introduces a question about hummingbird feeding that the text leaves unanswered."]$q$::jsonb, 2, $q$The previous sentence says high-speed video revealed a new mechanism; the underlined sentence describes it.$q$, null),
  ($q$cs-h71$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Text Structure and Purpose$q$, 3, $q$The following text is from a short story. Maya is waiting for the results of an audition for a youth orchestra.<br><br>Maya told herself that it didn't matter. There would be other auditions, other orchestras, other years. She told herself this on the bus home and again at dinner, and again when she lay awake at midnight listening to the refrigerator hum. By morning she had told herself so many times that it no longer sounded like a sentence at all, only a sound, like the refrigerator, that went on and on without meaning anything.$q$, $q$Which choice best describes the main purpose of the text?$q$, $q$["To show that a character has accepted that she will not be chosen for the orchestra","To show how a character's attempts to reassure herself fail to convince her","To describe a character's careful preparations for an important audition","To show that a character regrets having auditioned for the youth orchestra"]$q$::jsonb, 1, $q$Maya repeats a reassurance until it becomes meaningless noise, revealing that her attempts to convince herself aren't working.$q$, null),
  ($q$cs-h72$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Text Structure and Purpose$q$, 3, $q$Some scientists have proposed injecting tiny reflective particles into the upper atmosphere to cool the planet by reflecting sunlight, imitating the cooling that follows large volcanic eruptions. Supporters argue that the method could be inexpensive and fast-acting. Critics raise several concerns: the particles would have to be replenished continually, since they settle out within a few years; regional rainfall patterns could be disrupted; and if the injections ever stopped suddenly, temperatures could rebound rapidly. Many researchers agree that more study is needed before any such project is attempted.$q$, $q$Which choice best describes the overall structure of the text?$q$, $q$["The text describes a proposal, presents arguments made for and against it, and notes a widely shared view about what should happen next.","The text describes a natural phenomenon and then explains how scientists first discovered its causes.","The text presents a proposal, describes its supporters' arguments, and then argues that it should be adopted quickly to prevent harm.","The text compares two methods of cooling the planet and concludes that one is clearly superior to the other."]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$The text describes the geoengineering proposal, gives supporters' and critics' arguments, and ends with the consensus that more study is needed.$q$, null),
  ($q$cs-h73$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Text Structure and Purpose$q$, 3, $q$In many cities, streetlights have been replaced with energy-efficient LEDs that emit more blue light than older bulbs. <u>Ecologists have raised concerns about this change.</u> Blue light is especially disruptive to nocturnal animals: it can draw insects away from their habitats, confuse migrating birds, and disorient sea turtle hatchlings, which use the brightness of the horizon over the ocean to find the water. Some cities have responded by installing warmer-colored LEDs that save energy while emitting less blue light.$q$, $q$Which choice best describes the function of the underlined sentence in the text as a whole?$q$, $q$["It summarizes the benefits of LEDs that were described in the previous sentence.","It explains why cities decided to replace older streetlights with LEDs.","It presents a conclusion that the text later shows to be mistaken.","It introduces a problem that the following sentences describe in detail."]$q$::jsonb, 3, $q$The underlined sentence introduces ecologists' concerns, which the next sentence explains (effects of blue light on animals).$q$, null),
  ($q$cs-h74$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Text Structure and Purpose$q$, 3, $q$The term "serendipity" is often used to describe accidental discoveries, such as penicillin or the microwave oven. Sociologist Dev Raman argues that this framing is misleading. In nearly every celebrated case he studied, the "accident" was noticed only because the discoverer had deep expertise that allowed them to recognize its significance; countless others had likely encountered the same phenomenon and ignored it. Serendipity, Raman concludes, is less a matter of luck than of prepared minds encountering chance events.$q$, $q$Which choice best describes the main purpose of the text?$q$, $q$["To describe how penicillin and the microwave oven were discovered by scientists who were working on other problems","To argue that most important scientific discoveries have been the result of luck rather than careful research","To present a sociologist's argument that a common understanding of accidental discovery underestimates the role of expertise","To explain why many people who encounter unusual phenomena ignore them, while experts tend to investigate them more thoroughly"]$q$::jsonb, 2, $q$The text presents Raman's argument that serendipitous discoveries depend on the expertise that lets discoverers recognize chance events.$q$, null),
  ($q$cs-h75$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Text Structure and Purpose$q$, 3, $q$Western scholars long assumed that the tradition of Indian classical dance had been transmitted unchanged for two thousand years, preserved in ancient treatises. Dance historian Anjali Rao argues that the forms performed today were substantially reconstructed in the twentieth century. <u>After colonial authorities discouraged temple dance in the late 1800s, many traditions nearly disappeared, and the dancers and scholars who revived them drew on surviving practitioners, ancient texts, and their own creative choices.</u> The result, Rao emphasizes, is no less authentic for being partly modern; all living traditions change.$q$, $q$Which choice best describes the function of the underlined sentence in the text as a whole?$q$, $q$["It presents evidence that the dance tradition was preserved unchanged by colonial authorities.","It provides the historical background supporting the historian's claim that the dance forms were reconstructed.","It explains why Western scholars believed that Indian classical dance had been transmitted unchanged for two thousand years.","It suggests that the revived forms are less authentic than the ancient forms described in treatises."]$q$::jsonb, 1, $q$Rao claims the forms were reconstructed; the underlined sentence explains how near-disappearance and revival led to that reconstruction.$q$, null),
  ($q$cs-h76$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Text Structure and Purpose$q$, 3, $q$Most medicines are tested on adults, and doses for children are often estimated by scaling down adult doses according to body weight. Pharmacologist Lena Kowalski points out that this approach can be dangerous. Children's livers and kidneys, which process drugs, mature at different rates; a newborn may clear a drug from the body far more slowly than an adult, while a toddler may clear it faster. As a result, a dose adjusted for weight may be too high for an infant and too low for a young child.$q$, $q$Which choice best describes the main purpose of the text?$q$, $q$["To explain why a common method of determining children's medicine doses can be unreliable","To argue that medicines should never be given to children until they have been tested on adults","To describe how the liver and kidneys process drugs in adults and in children","To compare the effectiveness of different medicines used to treat children and adults"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$The text describes the weight-based dosing method and explains, through Kowalski, why it can be unreliable given children's developing organs.$q$, null),
  ($q$cs-h77$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Text Structure and Purpose$q$, 3, $q$The Silk Road is often imagined as a single highway along which merchants carried silk from China to Rome. In reality, it was a shifting network of routes, and few traders traveled its full length. <u>Goods typically passed through many hands, each merchant carrying them a short distance before selling them to the next.</u> Along with silk, spices, and glass, the network carried ideas, religions, technologies, and diseases—including, historians believe, the plague that devastated Europe in the fourteenth century.$q$, $q$Which choice best describes the function of the underlined sentence in the text as a whole?$q$, $q$["It explains why silk was the most valuable good traded along the Silk Road.","It provides evidence that merchants on the Silk Road spread disease deliberately.","It describes the route that most merchants followed when they carried silk, spices, and glass from China all the way to Rome.","It elaborates on the claim that the Silk Road was not the single route of popular imagination."]$q$::jsonb, 3, $q$The text says few traders traveled the full length; the underlined sentence elaborates by describing how goods were passed along in short stages.$q$, null),
  ($q$cs-h78$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Text Structure and Purpose$q$, 3, $q$Mathematician Emmy Noether proved in 1915 that every symmetry in the laws of physics corresponds to a quantity that is conserved. Because the laws of physics are the same at all times, for example, energy is conserved; because they are the same in all places, momentum is conserved. The theorem, now known as Noether's theorem, gave physicists a powerful tool: by identifying the symmetries of a system, they could predict which quantities would remain constant. Physicists still regard it as one of the most important results in theoretical physics.$q$, $q$Which choice best describes the main purpose of the text?$q$, $q$["To argue that energy and momentum are the most important quantities in physics","To explain why the laws of physics are the same at all times and in all places, and how this makes energy and momentum constant","To describe a mathematical result, illustrate it with examples, and explain why it was useful","To compare the work of Emmy Noether with that of Albert Einstein"]$q$::jsonb, 2, $q$The text states Noether's theorem, gives examples (energy, momentum), and explains its usefulness to physicists.$q$, null),
  ($q$cs-h79$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Text Structure and Purpose$q$, 3, $q$Desert plants have evolved many strategies for surviving drought. Cacti store water in thick stems and have replaced leaves with spines, reducing water loss. Some desert annuals avoid drought altogether: their seeds lie dormant for years and sprout only after heavy rain, completing their entire life cycle in a few weeks. <u>The creosote bush takes yet another approach, growing roots that spread widely just below the surface to capture even light rainfall before it evaporates.</u>$q$, $q$Which choice best describes the function of the underlined sentence in the text as a whole?$q$, $q$["It presents an exception to the strategies described in the previous sentences.","It provides a third example of the general claim made in the first sentence.","It explains why creosote bushes grow larger than most other desert plants.","It describes the strategy that the text identifies as the most effective."]$q$::jsonb, 1, $q$The first sentence claims desert plants have many strategies; the text gives cacti, annuals, and then the creosote bush as a third example.$q$, null),
  ($q$cs-h80$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Words in Context$q$, 3, $q$Because the fossil record from that period is so sparse, paleontologist Ines Okafor cautions that any reconstruction of the animal's behavior must remain ______: new specimens could easily overturn the current picture.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical and precise word or phrase?$q$, $q$["provisional","definitive","arbitrary","conventional"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Okafor warns that new specimens could overturn the reconstruction, so it must be considered temporary and open to revision—"provisional." "Definitive" is the opposite, "arbitrary" suggests it lacks any basis, and "conventional" doesn't relate to the possibility of revision.$q$, null),
  ($q$cs-h81$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Words in Context$q$, 3, $q$Critics who expected the director's new film to be as ______ as her earlier work—which featured sprawling casts and elaborate, interlocking plotlines—were surprised by its stark simplicity: two characters, one room, and almost no dialogue.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical and precise word or phrase?$q$, $q$["austere","derivative","lighthearted","intricate"]$q$::jsonb, 3, $q$The earlier work is described as having "sprawling casts and elaborate, interlocking plotlines," which contrasts with the new film's "stark simplicity." "Intricate" captures that complexity. "Austere" describes the new film, not the earlier work.$q$, null),
  ($q$cs-h82$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Words in Context$q$, 3, $q$Although the senator's public statements on the trade agreement were ______, carefully avoiding any clear endorsement or rejection, her private letters reveal that she strongly opposed it from the beginning.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical and precise word or phrase?$q$, $q$["vehement","candid","equivocal","inflammatory"]$q$::jsonb, 2, $q$The statements "carefully avoid[ed] any clear endorsement or rejection," which is the meaning of "equivocal." "Vehement" and "inflammatory" suggest strong expression, and "candid" suggests openness, all of which contradict the description.$q$, null),
  ($q$cs-h83$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Words in Context$q$, 3, $q$Early accounts described the explorer as fearless, but his journals suggest a more ______ figure: he wrote repeatedly of his doubts, revised his routes to avoid danger, and once turned back within sight of his goal because a storm seemed likely.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical and precise word or phrase?$q$, $q$["reckless","circumspect","eminent","gregarious"]$q$::jsonb, 1, $q$The journals reveal caution—doubting, avoiding danger, turning back. "Circumspect" means cautious and wary. "Reckless" is the opposite, and "eminent" and "gregarious" are unrelated to caution.$q$, null),
  ($q$cs-h84$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Words in Context$q$, 3, $q$The connection between the two historical events is ______ at best; the only evidence linking them is a single letter whose authenticity has been questioned by several scholars.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical and precise word or phrase?$q$, $q$["tenuous","incontrovertible","deliberate","ubiquitous"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$The connection rests on one questionable letter, so it is weak—"tenuous." "Incontrovertible" means impossible to dispute, the opposite. "Deliberate" and "ubiquitous" do not describe the strength of a connection.$q$, null),
  ($q$cs-h85$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Words in Context$q$, 3, $q$The novelist's prose is famously ______; she rarely uses two words where one will do, and some of her chapters are only a few sentences long.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical and precise word or phrase?$q$, $q$["ornate","obscure","sentimental","economical"]$q$::jsonb, 3, $q$Using as few words as possible is economy of language—"economical." "Ornate" means elaborately decorated, the opposite. "Obscure" and "sentimental" don't relate to brevity.$q$, null),
  ($q$cs-h86$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Words in Context$q$, 3, $q$Though the new species of frog had been found only in a single valley, the biologists were reluctant to call it ______ to that region until they had surveyed the surrounding mountains, where similar habitat exists.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical and precise word or phrase?$q$, $q$["indigenous","hostile","endemic","incidental"]$q$::jsonb, 2, $q$The biologists hesitate to say the frog is found only in that region until they have searched nearby. "Endemic" means native to and restricted to a particular area. "Indigenous" means native but does not imply restriction, so it doesn't capture the biologists' caution about whether the frog occurs elsewhere.$q$, null),
  ($q$cs-h87$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Words in Context$q$, 3, $q$The company's apology was widely criticized as ______: it was issued only after a week of public outrage, consisted of two vague sentences, and made no commitment to change.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical and precise word or phrase?$q$, $q$["effusive","perfunctory","unprecedented","conciliatory"]$q$::jsonb, 1, $q$A brief, vague apology issued reluctantly and without commitment is one performed minimally, as a mere formality—"perfunctory." "Effusive" (overflowing) contradicts "two vague sentences." "Conciliatory" suggests a genuine effort to make peace, which critics did not see.$q$, null),
  ($q$cs-h88$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Words in Context$q$, 3, $q$The researchers' initial results seemed to show that the drug improved memory, but when the study was repeated with a larger sample, the effect disappeared, suggesting that the original finding had been ______.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical and precise word or phrase?$q$, $q$["spurious","robust","predictable","proprietary"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$An effect that disappears with a larger sample was likely not real—it was "spurious." "Robust" means strong and reliable, the opposite. "Predictable" and "proprietary" do not fit.$q$, null),
  ($q$cs-h89$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Words in Context$q$, 3, $q$Rather than ______ the rival theory outright, the physicist acknowledged that it explained several observations her own model could not and proposed that the two might be combined.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical and precise word or phrase?$q$, $q$["embracing","corroborating","inventing","dismissing"]$q$::jsonb, 3, $q$"Rather than" sets up a contrast with the physicist's open-minded response. She did not reject the rival theory, so "dismissing" is correct. "Embracing" and "corroborating" are closer to what she actually did, so they don't contrast with it.$q$, null),
  ($q$cs-h90$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Words in Context$q$, 3, $q$The mayor's plan has been called ______ by supporters and opponents alike: both sides agree that it would be impossible to fund with the city's current revenue.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical and precise word or phrase?$q$, $q$["visionary","contentious","impracticable","frugal"]$q$::jsonb, 2, $q$Both sides agree the plan cannot be funded, which means it cannot be carried out—"impracticable." "Contentious" means causing disagreement, but the sentence says both sides agree. "Visionary" and "frugal" don't match the funding problem.$q$, null),
  ($q$cs-h91$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Words in Context$q$, 3, $q$The archive's catalog is so ______ that researchers often spend days locating a single document: items are listed under inconsistent headings, and many entries contain errors.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical and precise word or phrase?$q$, $q$["exhaustive","haphazard","concise","pristine"]$q$::jsonb, 1, $q$Inconsistent headings and errors indicate a lack of order—"haphazard." "Exhaustive" means thorough, and "pristine" means in perfect condition; neither fits. "Concise" doesn't explain the difficulty.$q$, null),
  ($q$cs-h92$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Words in Context$q$, 3, $q$Although the artist's paintings from the 1960s were dismissed at the time as ______—mere imitations of styles then fashionable in New York—critics today see them as anticipating trends that emerged only decades later.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical and precise word or phrase?$q$, $q$["derivative","visionary","eccentric","monumental"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$The dash defines the blank: "mere imitations of styles then fashionable." "Derivative" means imitative and unoriginal. "Visionary" reflects today's view, not the view at the time.$q$, null),
  ($q$cs-h93$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Words in Context$q$, 3, $q$The witness's account was remarkably ______: she recalled not only the color of the car but the dent in its rear bumper and the first three characters of its license plate.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical and precise word or phrase?$q$, $q$["ambiguous","fanciful","terse","meticulous"]$q$::jsonb, 3, $q$Recalling small, precise details shows careful attention—"meticulous." "Ambiguous" and "fanciful" suggest unclear or imagined details, and "terse" means brief.$q$, null),
  ($q$cs-h94$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Words in Context$q$, 3, $q$Scientists have long debated whether the decline in the bird population is ______ to the new pesticide or to habitat loss that began around the same time.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical and precise word or phrase?$q$, $q$["susceptible","indifferent","attributable","comparable"]$q$::jsonb, 2, $q$The debate concerns the cause of the decline. "Attributable to" means caused by. "Susceptible to" means vulnerable to, which would describe the birds, not the decline.$q$, null),
  ($q$cs-h95$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Words in Context$q$, 3, $q$The theory was once ______ among economists, but a series of financial crises exposed its weaknesses, and today few defend it.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical and precise word or phrase?$q$, $q$["obscure","ascendant","controversial","nascent"]$q$::jsonb, 1, $q$The contrast ("but... today few defend it") indicates that the theory was once dominant—"ascendant." "Obscure" and "nascent" would not contrast with its current lack of support, and "controversial" doesn't set up the decline.$q$, null),
  ($q$cs-h96$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Words in Context$q$, 3, $q$The negotiator's tone was ______ throughout the talks; even when the other side made demands she considered unreasonable, she responded calmly and suggested possible compromises.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical and precise word or phrase?$q$, $q$["conciliatory","belligerent","sardonic","indifferent"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Responding calmly and proposing compromises reflects a desire to make peace—"conciliatory." "Belligerent" and "sardonic" are hostile or mocking, and "indifferent" suggests she didn't care, which contradicts suggesting compromises.$q$, null),
  ($q$cs-h97$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Words in Context$q$, 3, $q$The poet's late work is ______ to many readers: densely allusive, full of references to obscure medieval texts, and written in a syntax so compressed that individual lines can be read several ways.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical and precise word or phrase?$q$, $q$["accessible","trite","melodious","impenetrable"]$q$::jsonb, 3, $q$The description—densely allusive, obscure, compressed—suggests that the work is very hard to understand: "impenetrable." "Accessible" is the opposite. "Trite" and "melodious" don't relate to difficulty.$q$, null),
  ($q$cs-h98$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Words in Context$q$, 3, $q$The sudden drop in the lake's water level did not ______ the decline of the fishing industry so much as accelerate a process that had begun decades earlier with overfishing.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical and precise word or phrase?$q$, $q$["halt","reverse","initiate","conceal"]$q$::jsonb, 2, $q$The sentence contrasts starting the decline with speeding up an existing process. "Initiate" (start) is what the drop did not do. "Halt" and "reverse" don't contrast with "accelerate" in the right way.$q$, null),
  ($q$cs-h99$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Words in Context$q$, 3, $q$Biologist Rahul Menon's findings are ______ with earlier studies: although his team used different methods and studied a different population of whales, they reached nearly identical conclusions.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical and precise word or phrase?$q$, $q$["incompatible","consistent","contemporaneous","redundant"]$q$::jsonb, 1, $q$Reaching nearly identical conclusions means the findings agree with earlier studies—"consistent." "Incompatible" is the opposite. "Contemporaneous" refers to time, and "redundant" implies the study was unnecessary, which the use of different methods argues against.$q$, null),
  ($q$cs-h100$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Words in Context$q$, 3, $q$The tribe's oral histories had long been ______ by historians who considered them unreliable, but recent archaeological finds have confirmed several events the stories describe.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical and precise word or phrase?$q$, $q$["discounted","celebrated","transcribed","fabricated"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$The historians considered the stories unreliable, so they gave them little weight—"discounted." The contrast with recent confirmation supports this. "Fabricated" means made up, which would refer to the people who told the stories, not the historians.$q$, null),
  ($q$cs-h101$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Words in Context$q$, 3, $q$The microscope's resolution is ______ by the wavelength of visible light; no matter how well its lenses are made, it cannot distinguish objects smaller than about 200 nanometers.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical and precise word or phrase?$q$, $q$["enhanced","determined arbitrarily","obscured","constrained"]$q$::jsonb, 3, $q$The wavelength of light sets a limit that cannot be overcome, so resolution is "constrained" (limited). "Enhanced" is the opposite, and "obscured" suggests hidden rather than limited.$q$, null),
  ($q$cs-h102$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Words in Context$q$, 3, $q$The diplomat's memoir is unusually ______ about her own mistakes: she devotes an entire chapter to a negotiation she believes she mishandled, sparing herself no criticism.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical and precise word or phrase?$q$, $q$["evasive","nostalgic","forthright","grandiose"]$q$::jsonb, 2, $q$Openly discussing one's own mistakes without sparing oneself is "forthright" (direct and honest). "Evasive" is the opposite, and "nostalgic" and "grandiose" don't relate to self-criticism.$q$, null),
  ($q$cs-h103$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Words in Context$q$, 3, $q$To prevent the virus from spreading, health officials imposed ______ restrictions on travel to and from the affected region, allowing only medical personnel and emergency supplies to pass.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical and precise word or phrase?$q$, $q$["nominal","stringent","voluntary","temporary"]$q$::jsonb, 1, $q$Allowing only medical personnel and supplies indicates very strict rules—"stringent." "Nominal" means slight, and "voluntary" contradicts the imposed nature. "Temporary" doesn't describe how strict the restrictions were.$q$, null),
  ($q$cs-h104$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Words in Context$q$, 3, $q$The scientist's hypothesis was bold, but her colleagues found her evidence ______: the data were drawn from only three sites, and the patterns she identified could easily have arisen by chance.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical and precise word or phrase?$q$, $q$["inconclusive","compelling","fraudulent","abundant"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$The limited data and possibility of chance patterns mean the evidence does not settle the question—"inconclusive." "Compelling" and "abundant" are the opposite, and "fraudulent" suggests dishonesty, which the text does not imply.$q$, null),
  ($q$cs-h105$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Words in Context$q$, 3, $q$Many of the composer's contemporaries regarded his use of dissonance as ______, a deliberate violation of the rules of harmony meant to shock audiences rather than to serve any musical purpose.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical and precise word or phrase?$q$, $q$["integral","subtle","traditional","gratuitous"]$q$::jsonb, 3, $q$The contemporaries thought the dissonance served no musical purpose and was used only to shock—"gratuitous" (unnecessary, unjustified). "Integral" means essential, the opposite.$q$, null),
  ($q$cs-h106$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Words in Context$q$, 3, $q$Although the two languages share many words, their grammars are strikingly ______; one places verbs at the end of sentences, while the other places them at the beginning.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical and precise word or phrase?$q$, $q$["analogous","rudimentary","divergent","archaic"]$q$::jsonb, 2, $q$The grammars differ in a fundamental way, so they are "divergent." "Analogous" means similar, the opposite. "Rudimentary" and "archaic" don't describe difference.$q$, null),
  ($q$cs-h107$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Words in Context$q$, 3, $q$The engineer insisted that the cracks in the bridge's supports were ______ and posed no threat to its stability, but the city ordered a full inspection anyway.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical and precise word or phrase?$q$, $q$["structural","superficial","extensive","recent"]$q$::jsonb, 1, $q$The engineer claimed the cracks posed no threat, meaning they were only on the surface—"superficial." "Structural" and "extensive" would suggest a threat.$q$, null),
  ($q$cs-h108$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Words in Context$q$, 3, $q$The bird's song is ______ in its variety: a single male may produce more than two thousand distinct phrases, rarely repeating any of them during a morning's performance.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical and precise word or phrase?$q$, $q$["prodigious","monotonous","rudimentary","derivative"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Two thousand distinct phrases represents an enormous range—"prodigious" (remarkably great). "Monotonous" is the opposite, and "rudimentary" and "derivative" don't fit.$q$, null),
  ($q$cs-h109$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Words in Context$q$, 3, $q$Though often described as a recluse, the inventor was in fact quite ______, hosting weekly dinners for scientists and artists and maintaining a vast correspondence with friends abroad.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical and precise word or phrase?$q$, $q$["reticent","eccentric","prolific","sociable"]$q$::jsonb, 3, $q$Hosting dinners and corresponding with friends contradicts the image of a recluse, showing she enjoyed company—"sociable." "Reticent" fits the recluse description, not the contrast. "Prolific" means productive, which isn't the point.$q$, null),
  ($q$cs-h110$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Words in Context$q$, 3, $q$Because the treaty's language was ______, each nation interpreted it in its own favor, and disputes over its meaning continued for decades.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical and precise word or phrase?$q$, $q$["explicit","innocuous","ambiguous","eloquent"]$q$::jsonb, 2, $q$Language open to different interpretations is "ambiguous." "Explicit" is the opposite. "Innocuous" and "eloquent" don't explain the interpretive disputes.$q$, null),
  ($q$cs-h111$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Words in Context$q$, 3, $q$The museum's decision to display the forged paintings alongside the originals was intended to be ______: visitors could compare them side by side and learn to recognize the forger's telltale techniques.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical and precise word or phrase?$q$, $q$["deceptive","instructive","frivolous","punitive"]$q$::jsonb, 1, $q$The purpose was to teach visitors to recognize forgeries—"instructive." "Deceptive" is the opposite of the museum's intention, and "frivolous" and "punitive" don't fit.$q$, null),
  ($q$cs-h112$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Words in Context$q$, 3, $q$The new evidence does not ______ the theory entirely, but it does suggest that the theory's original formulation was too simple to account for the full range of observations.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical and precise word or phrase?$q$, $q$["invalidate","support","predict","clarify"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$The sentence says the evidence complicates but doesn't entirely disprove the theory. "Invalidate" (show to be false) is what the evidence does not do. "Support" wouldn't fit the contrast with "but... too simple."$q$, null),
  ($q$cs-h113$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Words in Context$q$, 3, $q$In contrast to the ______ predictions of many analysts, who expected the company to collapse within a year, the small firm not only survived but doubled its revenue.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical and precise word or phrase?$q$, $q$["optimistic","tentative","accurate","dire"]$q$::jsonb, 3, $q$The analysts expected collapse, so their predictions were extremely negative—"dire." "Optimistic" is the opposite. "Accurate" contradicts the outcome.$q$, null),
  ($q$cs-h114$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Words in Context$q$, 3, $q$The scholar argues that the folktale's many versions, though they differ in their details, share a ______ structure: a journey, a test, and a return home transformed.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical and precise word or phrase?$q$, $q$["chaotic","superficial","common","unique"]$q$::jsonb, 2, $q$Versions that differ in details but share the same elements have a shared, or "common," structure. "Unique" would mean each had a different structure.$q$, null),
  ($q$cs-h115$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Words in Context$q$, 4, $q$The council's response to the flooding was criticized as ______; officials waited nearly a week before sending aid to the hardest-hit neighborhoods.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical and precise word or phrase?$q$, $q$["precipitous","dilatory","draconian","meticulous"]$q$::jsonb, 1, $q$Waiting nearly a week reflects slowness—"dilatory" (tending to delay). "Precipitous" means overly hasty, the opposite. "Draconian" (harsh) and "meticulous" (careful) don't fit.$q$, null),
  ($q$cs-h116$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Words in Context$q$, 3, $q$The documentary's portrayal of the scientist is largely ______; it focuses on her achievements and glosses over the controversies that marked her career.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical and precise word or phrase?$q$, $q$["flattering","balanced","damning","dispassionate"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Focusing on achievements and ignoring controversies presents the scientist in a favorable light—"flattering." "Balanced" and "dispassionate" are contradicted by the glossing over, and "damning" is the opposite.$q$, null),
  ($q$cs-h117$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Words in Context$q$, 3, $q$The species' range has ______ dramatically in recent decades; once found across much of the continent, it now survives only in a few isolated mountain valleys.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical and precise word or phrase?$q$, $q$["expanded","fluctuated","stabilized","contracted"]$q$::jsonb, 3, $q$Going from much of a continent to a few valleys means the range has shrunk—"contracted." "Expanded" is the opposite, and "fluctuated" or "stabilized" don't describe a steady decline.$q$, null),
  ($q$cs-h118$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Words in Context$q$, 3, $q$The author's tone in the essay is ______: she describes the politicians' promises in such exaggerated, admiring terms that readers understand she means the opposite.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical and precise word or phrase?$q$, $q$["earnest","nostalgic","ironic","objective"]$q$::jsonb, 2, $q$Saying something in a way that conveys the opposite meaning is irony. "Earnest" (sincere) is the opposite, and "nostalgic" and "objective" don't fit.$q$, null),
  ($q$cs-h119$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Words in Context$q$, 3, $q$Because the manuscript survives only in fragments, scholars must ______ much of its content from quotations in later works by other authors.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical and precise word or phrase?$q$, $q$["translate","reconstruct","dismiss","publish"]$q$::jsonb, 1, $q$Without the full manuscript, scholars must piece together its content from other sources—"reconstruct." "Translate" and "publish" assume the content is available, and "dismiss" doesn't fit.$q$, null),
  ($q$cs-h120$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Words in Context$q$, 3, $q$The proposed law has drawn ______ support: environmental groups, business associations, and labor unions—organizations that rarely agree on anything—have all endorsed it.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical and precise word or phrase?$q$, $q$["broad","tepid","covert","partisan"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Support from many very different groups is wide-ranging—"broad." "Partisan" means from one side only, the opposite. "Tepid" (lukewarm) and "covert" (hidden) don't fit.$q$, null),
  ($q$cs-h121$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Words in Context$q$, 3, $q$Though the treatment helped some patients, the researchers stressed that its benefits were ______ and that it should not replace standard therapies.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical and precise word or phrase?$q$, $q$["dramatic","permanent","unanimous","modest"]$q$::jsonb, 3, $q$The researchers caution that the treatment shouldn't replace standard therapies, suggesting its benefits are limited—"modest." "Dramatic" would argue for wider use.$q$, null),
  ($q$cs-h122$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Words in Context$q$, 3, $q$The violinist's interpretation of the sonata was ______: rather than follow the tempos indicated in the score, she slowed some passages almost to a halt and rushed through others.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical and precise word or phrase?$q$, $q$["faithful","conventional","idiosyncratic","hesitant"]$q$::jsonb, 2, $q$Ignoring the score's tempos in a personal, unusual way is "idiosyncratic." "Faithful" and "conventional" are contradicted by her departures from the score.$q$, null),
  ($q$cs-h123$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Words in Context$q$, 3, $q$The historian argues that the king's reputation for cruelty is largely ______, the product of propaganda spread by the rival dynasty that overthrew him.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical and precise word or phrase?$q$, $q$["accurate","undeserved","inherited","irrelevant"]$q$::jsonb, 1, $q$If the reputation comes from enemy propaganda, it isn't justified by fact—"undeserved." "Accurate" contradicts the claim of propaganda.$q$, null),
  ($q$cs-h124$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Words in Context$q$, 3, $q$The two scientists' accounts of the discovery are ______: each claims to have been the first to notice the anomaly, and each describes the other as merely assisting.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical and precise word or phrase?$q$, $q$["irreconcilable","complementary","identical","corroborative"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$The accounts make conflicting claims that can't both be true—"irreconcilable." "Complementary" and "corroborative" suggest they fit together or support each other, and "identical" contradicts the conflicting claims.$q$, null),
  ($q$cs-h125$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Words in Context$q$, 3, $q$In her study of nineteenth-century letters, historian Ana Lucía Ferreira found that many writers used elaborate politeness formulas not out of genuine deference but as a ______ for criticism, softening complaints that would otherwise have seemed rude.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical and precise word or phrase?$q$, $q$["substitute","deterrent","prerequisite","vehicle"]$q$::jsonb, 3, $q$The politeness formulas carried and delivered criticism in a softened form; they served as a means of conveying it—a "vehicle." A "substitute" would replace criticism, but the formulas accompanied it. A "deterrent" would discourage it.$q$, null),
  ($q$cs-h126$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Words in Context$q$, 3, $q$The early drafts of the novel reveal how ______ the author's process was: chapters were rewritten a dozen times, characters were added and deleted, and the ending changed completely at least three times.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical and precise word or phrase?$q$, $q$["effortless","secretive","laborious","linear"]$q$::jsonb, 2, $q$Rewriting chapters a dozen times and repeatedly changing the ending shows the process required great effort—"laborious." "Effortless" is the opposite, and "linear" contradicts the repeated revisions.$q$, null),
  ($q$cs-h127$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Words in Context$q$, 3, $q$Though the economist's model accurately predicted the recession, some of her colleagues regarded its success as ______, noting that the same model had failed to predict three earlier downturns.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical and precise word or phrase?$q$, $q$["inevitable","fortuitous","deserved","systematic"]$q$::jsonb, 1, $q$The colleagues suspect the correct prediction was a matter of luck, since the model had failed before. "Fortuitous" means happening by chance. "Inevitable," "deserved," and "systematic" all imply the success was not due to luck.$q$, null),
  ($q$cs-h128$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Words in Context$q$, 3, $q$Because octopuses live only a year or two and rarely interact with their parents, researchers were surprised to find that their problem-solving abilities are so ______; such intelligence is usually associated with long-lived, social animals.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical and precise word or phrase?$q$, $q$["sophisticated","limited","instinctive","predictable"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$The researchers' surprise implies that octopuses' problem-solving is advanced despite the traits usually associated with intelligence. "Sophisticated" fits. "Limited" would not be surprising.$q$, null),
  ($q$cs-h129$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Words in Context$q$, 3, $q$The critic praised the novel's dialogue as ______, noting that the characters speak in the halting, overlapping, often ungrammatical way that real people do.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical and precise word or phrase?$q$, $q$["stilted","poetic","formal","naturalistic"]$q$::jsonb, 3, $q$Dialogue that mimics how real people speak is "naturalistic." "Stilted" and "formal" are the opposite, and "poetic" suggests artful rather than realistic.$q$, null),
  ($q$cs-h130$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Words in Context$q$, 3, $q$Despite its reputation as a ______ genre, interested only in cheap thrills, the gothic novel often explored serious questions about power, inheritance, and the constraints placed on women.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical and precise word or phrase?$q$, $q$["rigorous","obscure","frivolous","venerable"]$q$::jsonb, 2, $q$The contrast ("Despite... the gothic novel often explored serious questions") signals that the genre was thought to be unserious—"frivolous," which matches "interested only in cheap thrills."$q$, null),
  ($q$cs-h131$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Words in Context$q$, 3, $q$The researchers did not claim that the drug cured the disease; their claim was more ______: that it slowed the disease's progression in some patients.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical and precise word or phrase?$q$, $q$["sweeping","modest","dubious","novel"]$q$::jsonb, 1, $q$Saying the drug slowed progression in some patients is a smaller, more limited claim than saying it cured the disease—"modest." "Sweeping" is the opposite.$q$, null),
  ($q$cs-h132$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Words in Context$q$, 3, $q$The village's isolation, once a hardship, has become an unexpected ______: its pristine landscapes and traditional way of life now attract thousands of visitors each year.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical and precise word or phrase?$q$, $q$["asset","burden","anomaly","constraint"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$The isolation now brings benefits (attracting visitors), so it has become something valuable—an "asset." "Burden" and "constraint" describe it as a hardship, which the sentence says it no longer is.$q$, null),
  ($q$cs-h133$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Words in Context$q$, 3, $q$The witness's testimony was ______ by phone records showing that she had called the police at exactly the time she described.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical and precise word or phrase?$q$, $q$["contradicted","fabricated","dismissed","corroborated"]$q$::jsonb, 3, $q$Phone records matching her account confirm it—"corroborated." "Contradicted" is the opposite.$q$, null),
  ($q$cs-h134$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Words in Context$q$, 3, $q$The playwright's characters are often ______ figures, neither heroes nor villains, whose motives remain unclear even to themselves.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical and precise word or phrase?$q$, $q$["archetypal","sympathetic","ambiguous","flamboyant"]$q$::jsonb, 2, $q$Characters who are neither heroes nor villains and whose motives are unclear are "ambiguous." "Archetypal" suggests typical, clear-cut models, which contradicts the description.$q$, null),
  ($q$cs-h135$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Words in Context$q$, 3, $q$Researchers had assumed that the cave's paintings were created over a short period, but radiocarbon dating revealed a surprisingly ______ history of use, with some images added more than ten thousand years after the first.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical and precise word or phrase?$q$, $q$["abbreviated","protracted","violent","recent"]$q$::jsonb, 1, $q$Images added over ten thousand years indicates a long, extended history—"protracted." "Abbreviated" is the opposite.$q$, null),
  ($q$cs-h136$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Words in Context$q$, 3, $q$The candidate's speech was ______ with references to local history, mentioning the town's founding, its mills, and the flood of 1936 in a way that delighted the hometown crowd.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical and precise word or phrase?$q$, $q$["replete","devoid","fraught","tinged"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$The speech was full of references—"replete." "Devoid" means lacking. "Fraught" suggests danger or tension, and "tinged" suggests only a slight trace.$q$, null),
  ($q$cs-h137$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Words in Context$q$, 3, $q$The scientist's earlier work had been dismissed as ______, but her theory gained acceptance once new instruments allowed others to observe the phenomena she had predicted.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical and precise word or phrase?$q$, $q$["empirical","conclusive","plagiarized","speculative"]$q$::jsonb, 3, $q$Her earlier work was dismissed because it lacked observational confirmation; it was based on conjecture—"speculative." "Empirical" and "conclusive" are the opposite.$q$, null),
  ($q$cs-h138$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Words in Context$q$, 3, $q$Rather than ______ the controversy, the museum's statement inflamed it, as many readers interpreted the carefully worded apology as an admission of wrongdoing.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical and precise word or phrase?$q$, $q$["prolonging","documenting","defusing","provoking"]$q$::jsonb, 2, $q$"Rather than" contrasts with "inflamed"; the museum hoped to calm the controversy—"defusing." "Prolonging" and "provoking" are similar to inflaming.$q$, null),
  ($q$cs-h139$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Words in Context$q$, 3, $q$The species is remarkably ______: it thrives in deserts, grasslands, and even cities, eating everything from insects to discarded food.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical and precise word or phrase?$q$, $q$["fragile","adaptable","solitary","specialized"]$q$::jsonb, 1, $q$Thriving in many habitats and eating many foods shows flexibility—"adaptable." "Specialized" is the opposite.$q$, null),
  ($q$cs-h140$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Words in Context$q$, 3, $q$The essay's argument is ______; each point builds logically on the one before, leading the reader step by step to a conclusion that seems almost inevitable.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical and precise word or phrase?$q$, $q$["cogent","digressive","polemical","derivative"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$An argument that builds logically to a convincing conclusion is "cogent" (clear and persuasive). "Digressive" means wandering off topic, the opposite.$q$, null),
  ($q$cs-h141$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Words in Context$q$, 3, $q$Though the painting's colors now appear muted, chemical analysis suggests they were once quite ______, before centuries of exposure to light faded the pigments.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical and precise word or phrase?$q$, $q$["subdued","uniform","translucent","vivid"]$q$::jsonb, 3, $q$The contrast with "now appear muted" and "faded" indicates the colors were originally bright—"vivid." "Subdued" describes their current state.$q$, null),
  ($q$cs-h142$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Words in Context$q$, 3, $q$The negotiations stalled because both sides remained ______: neither would accept any proposal that required it to give up even a small portion of its original demands.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical and precise word or phrase?$q$, $q$["accommodating","ambivalent","intransigent","apathetic"]$q$::jsonb, 2, $q$Refusing to give up any part of their demands is "intransigent" (unwilling to compromise). "Accommodating" is the opposite. "Ambivalent" and "apathetic" suggest uncertainty or lack of interest.$q$, null),
  ($q$cs-h143$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Words in Context$q$, 3, $q$The author's memoir offers a ______ account of her childhood, mixing fond memories of summers by the lake with painful recollections of her parents' difficult marriage.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical and precise word or phrase?$q$, $q$["idealized","nuanced","perfunctory","fictitious"]$q$::jsonb, 1, $q$Mixing positive and painful memories shows a balanced, complex view—"nuanced." "Idealized" would omit the painful parts. "Perfunctory" means minimal effort.$q$, null),
  ($q$cs-h144$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Words in Context$q$, 3, $q$The initial reports of the earthquake's damage were ______; officials later discovered that hundreds more buildings had collapsed than first estimated.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical and precise word or phrase?$q$, $q$["understated","exaggerated","precise","sensational"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$If more damage was later found, the initial reports made it seem less severe than it was—"understated." "Exaggerated" and "sensational" are the opposite.$q$, null),
  ($q$cs-h145$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Words in Context$q$, 3, $q$Some critics view the director's reliance on silence and long, static shots as ______, but admirers argue that these techniques allow viewers to notice details that faster-paced films would obscure.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical and precise word or phrase?$q$, $q$["innovative","hectic","commercial","tedious"]$q$::jsonb, 3, $q$"But" signals that the critics' view contrasts with the admirers' praise. Critics therefore find the techniques boring—"tedious." "Innovative" would be praise, not criticism.$q$, null),
  ($q$cs-h146$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Words in Context$q$, 3, $q$The surgeon's hands were ______ even after twelve hours in the operating room; colleagues marveled that she could still perform delicate sutures without the slightest tremor.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical and precise word or phrase?$q$, $q$["weary","nimble","steady","callused"]$q$::jsonb, 2, $q$Performing delicate work without tremor shows her hands were "steady." "Weary" contradicts the point, and "nimble" focuses on quickness rather than lack of tremor.$q$, null),
  ($q$cs-h147$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Words in Context$q$, 3, $q$Though the professor's lectures were famous for their wit, his published writing was surprisingly ______, consisting mostly of dry technical reports.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical and precise word or phrase?$q$, $q$["whimsical","pedestrian","provocative","eloquent"]$q$::jsonb, 1, $q$"Surprisingly" signals contrast with "wit"; dry technical reports are dull and ordinary—"pedestrian." "Whimsical" and "eloquent" would match the wit.$q$, null),
  ($q$cs-h148$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Words in Context$q$, 3, $q$The two species look almost identical, but their songs are ______, which is how ornithologists first realized that they were not the same bird.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical and precise word or phrase?$q$, $q$["distinct","similar","faint","complex"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$The songs revealed that the birds were different species, so the songs must differ clearly—"distinct." "Similar" would not have helped distinguish them.$q$, null),
  ($q$cs-h149$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Words in Context$q$, 3, $q$The company's claims that its product was environmentally friendly proved ______; investigators found that its factories released pollutants well above legal limits.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical and precise word or phrase?$q$, $q$["modest","verifiable","candid","specious"]$q$::jsonb, 3, $q$Claims that look plausible but turn out false are "specious." "Verifiable" and "candid" suggest the claims were true, and "modest" doesn't fit.$q$, null),
  ($q$cs-h150$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Words in Context$q$, 3, $q$Because the senator's speech made no ______ mention of the controversial bill, reporters could only guess at her position from her general remarks about government spending.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical and precise word or phrase?$q$, $q$["oblique","lengthy","explicit","hostile"]$q$::jsonb, 2, $q$Reporters had to guess her position because she never directly mentioned the bill—"explicit." An "oblique" mention would be indirect, which matches what reporters had to work with.$q$, null),
  ($q$cs-h151$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Words in Context$q$, 3, $q$The archaeologists were careful not to ______ the burial site's contents, photographing each object in place before removing anything.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical and precise word or phrase?$q$, $q$["catalog","disturb","preserve","excavate"]$q$::jsonb, 1, $q$Being careful and photographing objects in place shows they wanted to avoid changing their arrangement—"disturb." "Catalog" and "preserve" are what they wanted to do.$q$, null),
  ($q$cs-h152$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Words in Context$q$, 3, $q$Although the ancient city was once a ______ center of trade, attracting merchants from three continents, by the tenth century it had dwindled to a small farming village.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical and precise word or phrase?$q$, $q$["thriving","modest","remote","fledgling"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$The contrast with "dwindled to a small farming village" shows the city was once very successful—"thriving." "Modest" and "fledgling" don't contrast with decline.$q$, null),
  ($q$cs-h153$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Words in Context$q$, 3, $q$The scientist's report was notable for its ______; she acknowledged every limitation of her data and avoided drawing any conclusion the evidence did not clearly support.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical and precise word or phrase?$q$, $q$["ambition","brevity","originality","rigor"]$q$::jsonb, 3, $q$Acknowledging limitations and avoiding unsupported conclusions shows strictness and care—"rigor."$q$, null),
  ($q$cs-h154$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Words in Context$q$, 3, $q$Many readers found the novel's ending ______, since the mystery at the center of the plot is never solved and several major characters simply disappear from the story.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical and precise word or phrase?$q$, $q$["predictable","triumphant","unsatisfying","sentimental"]$q$::jsonb, 2, $q$An unresolved mystery and disappearing characters would leave readers without closure—"unsatisfying." "Predictable" contradicts the unresolved ending.$q$, null),
  ($q$cs-h155$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Words in Context$q$, 3, $q$The governor's decision to veto the bill was ______: just a week earlier, she had publicly promised to sign it.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical and precise word or phrase?$q$, $q$["inevitable","unexpected","unanimous","routine"]$q$::jsonb, 1, $q$Vetoing a bill after promising to sign it would surprise people—"unexpected." "Inevitable" and "routine" are the opposite.$q$, null),
  ($q$cs-h156$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Words in Context$q$, 3, $q$The researchers' conclusion that the fish can recognize individual human faces may seem ______, but their experiments were carefully designed to rule out other explanations.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical and precise word or phrase?$q$, $q$["far-fetched","self-evident","uncontroversial","outdated"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$"May seem... but" signals that the conclusion appears implausible even though the experiments support it. "Far-fetched" fits. "Self-evident" and "uncontroversial" would not require the defense that follows.$q$, null),
  ($q$cs-h157$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Words in Context$q$, 3, $q$The musician's early recordings, made on cheap equipment in her bedroom, have a ______ quality that many fans prefer to the polished sound of her later studio albums.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical and precise word or phrase?$q$, $q$["refined","synthetic","monotonous","raw"]$q$::jsonb, 3, $q$The contrast with "polished" and the cheap equipment suggest an unrefined sound—"raw." "Refined" describes the later albums.$q$, null),
  ($q$cs-h158$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Words in Context$q$, 3, $q$The scientist was ______ about the results of her experiment, refusing to discuss them publicly until they had been reviewed by other researchers.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical and precise word or phrase?$q$, $q$["effusive","careless","guarded","indifferent"]$q$::jsonb, 2, $q$Refusing to discuss results publicly reflects caution—"guarded." "Effusive" is the opposite, and "careless" and "indifferent" contradict her deliberate restraint.$q$, null),
  ($q$cs-h159$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Words in Context$q$, 3, $q$The river's course is ______, changing every few years as floods carve new channels through the soft sediment of the plain.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical and precise word or phrase?$q$, $q$["fixed","mutable","navigable","subterranean"]$q$::jsonb, 1, $q$A course that changes every few years is changeable—"mutable." "Fixed" is the opposite.$q$, null),
  ($q$cs-h160$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Words in Context$q$, 3, $q$The art historian's claim that the painting is by Vermeer rests on ______ evidence: no document links it to the artist, and its attribution depends entirely on similarities in style.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical and precise word or phrase?$q$, $q$["circumstantial","documentary","irrefutable","anecdotal"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Evidence based on stylistic similarity rather than direct proof is indirect—"circumstantial." "Documentary" is explicitly ruled out, and "irrefutable" is too strong. "Anecdotal" refers to personal stories.$q$, null),
  ($q$cs-h161$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Words in Context$q$, 3, $q$Far from being ______, the island's ecosystem is extraordinarily fragile; the introduction of a single invasive species could disrupt the entire food web.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical and precise word or phrase?$q$, $q$["diverse","isolated","ancient","resilient"]$q$::jsonb, 3, $q$"Far from being" sets up the opposite of "extraordinarily fragile": able to withstand disturbance—"resilient."$q$, null),
  ($q$cs-h162$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Words in Context$q$, 3, $q$The essayist's style is ______; she moves from personal anecdote to scientific explanation to literary analysis within a single paragraph, often without transitions.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical and precise word or phrase?$q$, $q$["monotonous","methodical","eclectic","restrained"]$q$::jsonb, 2, $q$Moving among many different modes reflects a varied, wide-ranging style—"eclectic." "Monotonous" and "methodical" suggest uniformity or order.$q$, null),
  ($q$cs-h163$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Words in Context$q$, 3, $q$The proposal was criticized as ______ because it would address only the symptoms of the city's housing shortage, not its underlying causes.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical and precise word or phrase?$q$, $q$["radical","superficial","comprehensive","costly"]$q$::jsonb, 1, $q$Addressing only symptoms, not causes, is shallow—"superficial." "Comprehensive" is the opposite.$q$, null),
  ($q$cs-h164$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Words in Context$q$, 3, $q$The violin's sound is so ______ that it can be heard clearly over a full orchestra, even in the farthest rows of a large concert hall.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical and precise word or phrase?$q$, $q$["resonant","muted","shrill","delicate"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Being heard clearly over an orchestra in a large hall indicates a full, carrying sound—"resonant." "Muted" and "delicate" are the opposite, and "shrill" has a negative connotation not supported by the context.$q$, null),
  ($q$cs-h165$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Words in Context$q$, 3, $q$The general's reputation for ______ was well earned: in thirty years of service, she never made a decision without first consulting her officers and weighing every alternative.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical and precise word or phrase?$q$, $q$["impulsiveness","cruelty","eloquence","deliberation"]$q$::jsonb, 3, $q$Consulting others and weighing every alternative is careful consideration—"deliberation." "Impulsiveness" is the opposite.$q$, null),
  ($q$cs-h166$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Words in Context$q$, 3, $q$The theory remains ______ among physicists: some regard it as the most promising path toward a unified theory, while others dismiss it as untestable.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical and precise word or phrase?$q$, $q$["obscure","settled","divisive","outdated"]$q$::jsonb, 2, $q$Physicists hold sharply opposing views, so the theory causes disagreement—"divisive." "Settled" is the opposite.$q$, null),
  ($q$cs-h167$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Words in Context$q$, 3, $q$The committee's report did not reject the proposal outright; instead, it ______ its support, endorsing the plan only on the condition that an independent review of its costs be completed first.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical and precise word or phrase?$q$, $q$["withdrew","qualified","concealed","exaggerated"]$q$::jsonb, 1, $q$Endorsing a plan "only on the condition" that something happen limits that support—"qualified" it. "Withdrew" would mean removing support entirely, which the text says did not happen.$q$, null),
  ($q$cs-h168$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Words in Context$q$, 3, $q$In her account of the expedition, the botanist is ______ about her own role, crediting her guides and assistants for nearly every discovery and mentioning her own contributions only in passing.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical and precise word or phrase?$q$, $q$["self-effacing","self-serving","self-assured","self-evident"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Crediting others and barely mentioning her own contributions shows modesty—"self-effacing." "Self-serving" is the opposite, "self-assured" means confident, and "self-evident" means obvious.$q$, null),
  ($q$cs-h169$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Words in Context$q$, 3, $q$The two historians agree on the facts of the rebellion but differ sharply in their ______ of them: one sees a spontaneous uprising, the other a carefully planned revolt.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical and precise word or phrase?$q$, $q$["verification","recollection","presentation","interpretation"]$q$::jsonb, 3, $q$They agree on the facts but disagree about what the facts mean (spontaneous or planned)—their "interpretation." "Verification" would concern whether the facts are true, on which they agree.$q$, null),
  ($q$cs-h170$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Words in Context$q$, 3, $q$Though the study's sample was small, its findings were ______ enough—they held across every age group and in each of the four cities studied—to persuade the agency to fund a larger trial.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical and precise word or phrase?$q$, $q$["surprising","preliminary","consistent","controversial"]$q$::jsonb, 2, $q$The dashes explain the blank: the findings held across every group and city, meaning they were "consistent." "Surprising" and "controversial" don't match the explanation, and "preliminary" wouldn't explain why they were persuasive.$q$, null),
  ($q$cs-h171$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Words in Context$q$, 3, $q$The author's portrayal of the town is affectionate but not ______; she depicts its residents' warmth and generosity alongside their narrow-mindedness and suspicion of outsiders.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical and precise word or phrase?$q$, $q$["sincere","uncritical","detailed","humorous"]$q$::jsonb, 1, $q$The portrayal includes both virtues and flaws, so it is affectionate without ignoring problems—not "uncritical." Saying it is not "sincere," "detailed," or "humorous" would not fit the description of a balanced portrayal.$q$, null),
  ($q$cs-h172$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Words in Context$q$, 3, $q$Scholars have long ______ the origins of the manuscript, which bears no date, no author's name, and no clear indication of where it was written.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical and precise word or phrase?$q$, $q$["disputed","documented","celebrated","fabricated"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$With no date, author, or location, the origins are uncertain and therefore subject to disagreement—"disputed." "Documented" contradicts the lack of information.$q$, null),
  ($q$cs-h173$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Words in Context$q$, 3, $q$The new regulations are intended to ______ the risk of contamination rather than eliminate it entirely, since some exposure to bacteria in food processing is unavoidable.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical and precise word or phrase?$q$, $q$["eradicate","assess","disregard","mitigate"]$q$::jsonb, 3, $q$The regulations reduce rather than eliminate the risk—"mitigate" means lessen. "Eradicate" means eliminate, which the sentence contrasts with.$q$, null),
  ($q$cs-h174$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Words in Context$q$, 3, $q$Although the poem appears at first to be a simple description of a winter landscape, its imagery of silence and stillness ______ a deeper meditation on grief.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical and precise word or phrase?$q$, $q$["dismisses","precedes","conveys","interrupts"]$q$::jsonb, 2, $q$The imagery carries or expresses the deeper meaning—"conveys." "Dismisses" and "interrupts" suggest opposition, and "precedes" suggests the meditation comes later rather than within the imagery.$q$, null),
  ($q$cs-h175$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Words in Context$q$, 3, $q$The young composer's first symphony was ______ by older critics, who praised its technical skill but dismissed its emotional range as limited.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical and precise word or phrase?$q$, $q$["lauded unreservedly","damned with faint praise","ignored entirely","denounced vehemently"]$q$::jsonb, 1, $q$Praising technical skill while dismissing emotional range is a mix of mild praise and criticism—"damned with faint praise." "Lauded unreservedly" and "denounced vehemently" are too extreme, and "ignored entirely" contradicts the critics' comments.$q$, null),
  ($q$cs-h176$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Words in Context$q$, 3, $q$The species' decline was so ______ that, for decades, no one noticed it; only when researchers compared population counts from the 1970s with recent surveys did the scale of the loss become clear.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical and precise word or phrase?$q$, $q$["gradual","precipitous","erratic","localized"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$A decline that went unnoticed for decades must have happened slowly—"gradual." "Precipitous" (steep and sudden) would likely have been noticed.$q$, null),
  ($q$cs-h177$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Words in Context$q$, 3, $q$Rather than ______ her critics directly, the scientist responded by publishing a detailed reanalysis of her data, letting the evidence speak for itself.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical and precise word or phrase?$q$, $q$["acknowledge","cite","ignore","rebut"]$q$::jsonb, 3, $q$The contrast is between answering critics directly and responding indirectly with evidence. "Rebut" (argue against) captures the direct response she avoided. "Ignore" doesn't fit, because she did respond.$q$, null),
  ($q$cs-h178$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Words in Context$q$, 3, $q$The diplomat's remarks were deliberately ______, allowing both governments to claim that the agreement favored their own position.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical and precise word or phrase?$q$, $q$["candid","conciliatory","ambiguous","provocative"]$q$::jsonb, 2, $q$Remarks that each side could interpret in its own favor were open to more than one meaning—"ambiguous." "Conciliatory" is close in spirit but doesn't explain why both sides could claim victory.$q$, null),
  ($q$cs-h179$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Words in Context$q$, 3, $q$Critics have praised the biography for its ______, noting that the author draws on thousands of letters, interviews with nearly everyone who knew the subject, and archives in four countries.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical and precise word or phrase?$q$, $q$["brevity","thoroughness","objectivity","lyricism"]$q$::jsonb, 1, $q$Drawing on extensive sources demonstrates comprehensiveness—"thoroughness." "Objectivity" concerns bias, which the list of sources doesn't address.$q$, null),
  ($q$cs-h180$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Words in Context$q$, 3, $q$Because the medication's side effects are ______—appearing only after several months of use—they were not detected in the initial short-term trials.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical and precise word or phrase?$q$, $q$["delayed","severe","transient","unpredictable"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$The dashes explain that side effects appear only after months, so they are "delayed." "Transient" means short-lived, which is the opposite.$q$, null),
  ($q$cs-h181$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Words in Context$q$, 3, $q$Many readers mistake the narrator's calm, measured voice for indifference, but a closer reading reveals the ______ grief beneath her restraint.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical and precise word or phrase?$q$, $q$["feigned","fleeting","trivial","profound"]$q$::jsonb, 3, $q$A closer reading reveals strong feeling under her restraint, contrasting with apparent indifference—"profound" grief. "Feigned" means fake, and "fleeting" and "trivial" undercut the contrast.$q$, null),
  ($q$cs-h182$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Words in Context$q$, 3, $q$The city's architecture ______ its history of conquest: Roman columns, Moorish arches, and Gothic spires stand side by side in its oldest neighborhood.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical and precise word or phrase?$q$, $q$["obscures","contradicts","reflects","predates"]$q$::jsonb, 2, $q$The styles from different conquering cultures show the history of conquest—the architecture "reflects" it. "Obscures" and "contradicts" are the opposite.$q$, null),
  ($q$cs-h183$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Words in Context$q$, 3, $q$The researcher's methods were ______: she replicated each experiment at least five times and published all of her raw data so that others could check her analysis.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical and precise word or phrase?$q$, $q$["unorthodox","scrupulous","expedient","covert"]$q$::jsonb, 1, $q$Replicating experiments and publishing raw data reflects careful, honest practice—"scrupulous." "Expedient" suggests convenience over principle, and "covert" means hidden, contradicting publication.$q$, null),
  ($q$cs-h184$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Words in Context$q$, 3, $q$The playwright's dialogue is so ______ that a single line often carries two or three meanings, rewarding audiences who see the play more than once.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical and precise word or phrase?$q$, $q$["layered","sparse","colloquial","repetitive"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Lines with multiple meanings are "layered." "Sparse" refers to quantity, "colloquial" to informality, and "repetitive" to repetition, none of which explains multiple meanings.$q$, null),
  ($q$cs-h185$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Words in Context$q$, 3, $q$The flooding was not the cause of the town's economic decline but rather a ______ event, hastening the closure of businesses that were already struggling.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical and precise word or phrase?$q$, $q$["mitigating","concurrent","negligible","precipitating"]$q$::jsonb, 3, $q$The flood hastened closures that were already likely, so it triggered or sped up the outcome—"precipitating." "Mitigating" means lessening, the opposite.$q$, null),
  ($q$cs-h186$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Words in Context$q$, 3, $q$Though the inventor was celebrated in her lifetime, her reputation ______ after her death, and by the 1950s her name was rarely mentioned even in histories of her field.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical and precise word or phrase?$q$, $q$["flourished","stabilized","waned","endured"]$q$::jsonb, 2, $q$Her name being rarely mentioned shows her reputation declined—"waned." "Flourished" and "endured" are the opposite.$q$, null),
  ($q$cs-h187$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Words in Context$q$, 3, $q$The critic described the singer's voice as ______, capable of shifting in a single phrase from a whisper to a full-throated cry.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical and precise word or phrase?$q$, $q$["monotonous","versatile","strident","fragile"]$q$::jsonb, 1, $q$Shifting easily from a whisper to a cry shows range and flexibility—"versatile." "Strident" (harsh) and "fragile" don't describe that range.$q$, null),
  ($q$cs-h188$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Words in Context$q$, 3, $q$The report's authors were careful to ______ their conclusions, noting that the data came from a single region and might not apply elsewhere.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical and precise word or phrase?$q$, $q$["hedge","overstate","publicize","abandon"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Noting limitations to avoid overcommitting to conclusions is "hedging." "Overstate" is the opposite, and "abandon" goes too far.$q$, null),
  ($q$cs-h189$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Words in Context$q$, 3, $q$The general's memoir has been criticized as ______: it presents every battle he fought as a triumph and blames his subordinates for every setback.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical and precise word or phrase?$q$, $q$["self-deprecating","self-contained","self-critical","self-aggrandizing"]$q$::jsonb, 3, $q$Presenting himself as always triumphant and blaming others exaggerates his own importance—"self-aggrandizing." "Self-deprecating" and "self-critical" are the opposite.$q$, null),
  ($q$cs-h190$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Words in Context$q$, 3, $q$The study's findings, though intriguing, are ______; until other laboratories reproduce them, most scientists will treat them with caution.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical and precise word or phrase?$q$, $q$["unfounded","unprecedented","unconfirmed","unremarkable"]$q$::jsonb, 2, $q$Results not yet reproduced by others are "unconfirmed." "Unfounded" means without basis, which is too strong—the findings are described as intriguing. "Unremarkable" contradicts "intriguing."$q$, null),
  ($q$cs-h191$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Words in Context$q$, 3, $q$The landscape painter was ______ in her choice of subjects, returning again and again to the same stretch of coastline over a career of more than forty years.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical and precise word or phrase?$q$, $q$["capricious","steadfast","indiscriminate","reluctant"]$q$::jsonb, 1, $q$Returning to the same subject for forty years shows loyalty and persistence—"steadfast." "Capricious" (unpredictable) and "indiscriminate" (unselective) are the opposite.$q$, null),
  ($q$cs-h192$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Words in Context$q$, 3, $q$Though the new tax was intended to reduce smoking, critics argue that its effects have been ______: it falls hardest on low-income smokers, who spend a larger share of their income on cigarettes.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical and precise word or phrase?$q$, $q$["regressive","negligible","progressive","beneficial"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$A tax that takes a larger share of income from lower-income people is "regressive." "Progressive" means the opposite, and "negligible" doesn't match the hardship described.$q$, null),
  ($q$cs-h193$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Words in Context$q$, 3, $q$The novel's narrator is ______: she claims to have been at home on the night of the crime, but later chapters reveal that she was seen across town.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical and precise word or phrase?$q$, $q$["omniscient","sympathetic","detached","unreliable"]$q$::jsonb, 3, $q$A narrator whose account is contradicted by later evidence cannot be trusted—"unreliable." "Omniscient" means all-knowing, which doesn't fit.$q$, null),
  ($q$cs-h194$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Words in Context$q$, 4, $q$Although the scientist's theory was ______ when she first proposed it, it is now taught in introductory biology courses as established fact.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical and precise word or phrase?$q$, $q$["canonical","rudimentary","heterodox","redundant"]$q$::jsonb, 2, $q$The contrast with "now taught... as established fact" implies the theory once went against accepted views—"heterodox." "Canonical" means accepted, which describes its current status.$q$, null),
  ($q$cs-h195$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Words in Context$q$, 3, $q$The negotiators reached only a ______ agreement, settling the dispute over fishing rights but leaving the more contentious question of the maritime border for future talks.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical and precise word or phrase?$q$, $q$["unanimous","partial","binding","secret"]$q$::jsonb, 1, $q$Settling one issue while leaving another unresolved is an incomplete agreement—"partial."$q$, null),
  ($q$cs-h196$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Words in Context$q$, 3, $q$Her argument is ______ on a single assumption: that consumers always act in their own financial interest. If that assumption is wrong, the entire argument collapses.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical and precise word or phrase?$q$, $q$["predicated","silent","lenient","fixated"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$An argument that depends on an assumption is "predicated" on it. "Fixated" suggests obsession, and "silent" and "lenient" don't fit.$q$, null),
  ($q$cs-h197$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Words in Context$q$, 3, $q$The mayor's ______ remarks about the budget—brief and noncommittal—did little to reassure residents worried about cuts to library hours.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical and precise word or phrase?$q$, $q$["inflammatory","exhaustive","impassioned","cursory"]$q$::jsonb, 3, $q$"Brief and noncommittal" describes hasty, superficial remarks—"cursory." "Exhaustive" and "impassioned" contradict "brief" and "noncommittal."$q$, null),
  ($q$cs-h198$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Words in Context$q$, 3, $q$Some biologists argue that the trait is ______, a remnant of an earlier stage of evolution that no longer serves any purpose.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical and precise word or phrase?$q$, $q$["adaptive","acquired","vestigial","dominant"]$q$::jsonb, 2, $q$A remnant that no longer serves a purpose is "vestigial." "Adaptive" means useful for survival, the opposite.$q$, null),
  ($q$cs-h199$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Words in Context$q$, 3, $q$Because the poet's handwriting was nearly ______, editors disagreed about dozens of words in her final manuscript and published several competing versions.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical and precise word or phrase?$q$, $q$["elegant","illegible","uniform","archaic"]$q$::jsonb, 1, $q$Disagreement about many words indicates the handwriting was very hard to read—"illegible."$q$, null),
  ($q$cs-h200$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Words in Context$q$, 3, $q$The government's response to the crisis was ______: it announced new policies almost daily, only to reverse many of them within the week.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical and precise word or phrase?$q$, $q$["erratic","decisive","sluggish","measured"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Announcing and then reversing policies constantly shows inconsistency—"erratic." "Decisive" and "measured" are contradicted by the reversals, and "sluggish" contradicts the daily announcements.$q$, null),
  ($q$cs-h201$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Words in Context$q$, 3, $q$The scientist's claim was ______ by later research: experiments in three countries confirmed every major prediction her model had made.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical and precise word or phrase?$q$, $q$["refuted","anticipated","obscured","vindicated"]$q$::jsonb, 3, $q$Later confirmation of her predictions proved her right—her claim was "vindicated." "Refuted" is the opposite.$q$, null),
  ($q$cs-h202$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Words in Context$q$, 3, $q$The architect's design is deceptively simple; its ______ facade conceals an interior of complex, interlocking spaces.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical and precise word or phrase?$q$, $q$["ostentatious","crumbling","unassuming","intricate"]$q$::jsonb, 2, $q$The facade looks simple, which contrasts with the complex interior—"unassuming." "Ostentatious" and "intricate" contradict "deceptively simple."$q$, null),
  ($q$cs-h203$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Words in Context$q$, 3, $q$The historian cautions that the diaries, written decades after the events they describe, may be ______; memory, she notes, tends to reshape the past to fit the present.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical and precise word or phrase?$q$, $q$["fabricated","distorted","illegible","redundant"]$q$::jsonb, 1, $q$Memory reshaping the past suggests the diaries may misrepresent events unintentionally—"distorted." "Fabricated" implies deliberate invention, which the historian does not suggest.$q$, null),
  ($q$cs-h204$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Words in Context$q$, 3, $q$The young chess player's style was ______ for someone her age: rather than launching risky attacks, she preferred slow, patient maneuvering that gradually wore down her opponents.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical and precise word or phrase?$q$, $q$["uncharacteristically cautious","typically aggressive","notably erratic","deliberately reckless"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Her slow, patient play is unusual for a young player, who would typically take risks. "Uncharacteristically cautious" captures both the style and the contrast with her age.$q$, null),
  ($q$cs-h205$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Words in Context$q$, 3, $q$The species was thought to be extinct, so the discovery of a small population in a remote forest was ______ for conservationists, offering a second chance to protect it.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical and precise word or phrase?$q$, $q$["a setback","a formality","an inconvenience","a windfall"]$q$::jsonb, 3, $q$Finding a species thought extinct is an unexpected gift—"a windfall." The other choices are negative or neutral.$q$, null),
  ($q$cs-h206$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Words in Context$q$, 3, $q$The film's critics found its message ______, complaining that it lectured audiences about environmental responsibility rather than letting the story speak for itself.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical and precise word or phrase?$q$, $q$["subtle","ambiguous","heavy-handed","outdated"]$q$::jsonb, 2, $q$Lecturing audiences instead of letting the story speak suggests an unsubtle approach—"heavy-handed." "Subtle" and "ambiguous" are the opposite.$q$, null),
  ($q$cs-h207$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Words in Context$q$, 3, $q$Though often described as a pioneer of abstract art, the painter regarded her work as ______ representational: each of her apparently abstract canvases, she insisted, depicted a specific landscape.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical and precise word or phrase?$q$, $q$["superficially","fundamentally","occasionally","reluctantly"]$q$::jsonb, 1, $q$She insisted that each canvas depicted a specific landscape, so she saw her work as representational at its core—"fundamentally." "Superficially" would mean only on the surface, which contradicts her insistence.$q$, null),
  ($q$cs-h208$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Words in Context$q$, 3, $q$The council's decision was ______ by a single vote, and opponents immediately announced plans to challenge it in court.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical and precise word or phrase?$q$, $q$["narrowly approved","unanimously passed","quietly withdrawn","overwhelmingly rejected"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$A decision decided "by a single vote" that opponents want to challenge was "narrowly approved." "Unanimously" and "overwhelmingly" contradict "a single vote."$q$, null),
  ($q$cs-h209$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Cross-Text Connections$q$, 3, $q$Text 1<br>Many ecologists have attributed the decline of North American bumblebees primarily to pesticides, particularly neonicotinoids, which can impair bees' ability to forage and navigate. Laboratory studies have repeatedly shown that exposure to these chemicals reduces colony growth.<br><br>Text 2<br>A team led by ecologist Priya Raman analyzed bumblebee records spanning more than a century. They found that species declined most sharply in regions where summer temperatures had risen beyond the bees' tolerance, regardless of local pesticide use. Raman acknowledges that pesticides harm bees but argues that their effects may be secondary to those of a warming climate.$q$, $q$Based on the texts, how would Raman and her team (Text 2) most likely respond to the ecologists' explanation in Text 1?$q$, $q$["The researchers in Text 2 would likely argue that the laboratory studies described in Text 1 were poorly designed and that pesticides do not harm bees.","The researchers in Text 2 would likely agree that pesticides are the primary cause of bumblebee decline but argue that warming makes their effects worse.","The researchers in Text 2 would likely argue that the ecologists in Text 1 should have studied bee populations over a longer period of time.","The researchers in Text 2 would likely argue that the explanation in Text 1 identifies a real factor but may overstate its importance relative to another factor."]$q$::jsonb, 3, $q$Raman "acknowledges that pesticides harm bees" but argues that their effects "may be secondary" to climate. She would therefore accept the factor in Text 1 as real while disputing its primacy.$q$, null),
  ($q$cs-h210$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Cross-Text Connections$q$, 3, $q$Text 1<br>Some historians argue that the printing press caused the Protestant Reformation. By making it possible to reproduce Martin Luther's writings quickly and cheaply, the press spread his ideas faster than church authorities could suppress them; without it, they contend, the Reformation would likely have been contained, as earlier reform movements were.<br><br>Text 2<br>Historian Mateo Ruiz cautions against treating the printing press as a cause of the Reformation. Earlier reformers, he notes, also had followers who copied and circulated their writings. What distinguished Luther's era was a combination of factors: political rulers who saw advantage in breaking with Rome, widespread resentment of church taxes, and, yes, a new technology that amplified dissent. The press, Ruiz argues, accelerated a movement whose causes lay elsewhere.$q$, $q$Based on the texts, how would Ruiz (Text 2) most likely respond to the historians' argument in Text 1?$q$, $q$["Ruiz would argue that the printing press played no meaningful role in the spread of Luther's ideas during the Reformation.","Ruiz would agree that earlier reform movements failed mainly because they lacked a technology like the printing press.","Ruiz would agree that the printing press helped spread Luther's ideas but would dispute that it was the cause of the Reformation.","Ruiz would argue that political rulers used the printing press to suppress Luther's writings rather than to spread them."]$q$::jsonb, 2, $q$Ruiz grants that the press "amplified dissent" and "accelerated" the movement but argues that its causes "lay elsewhere," disagreeing with Text 1's causal claim.$q$, null),
  ($q$cs-h211$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Cross-Text Connections$q$, 3, $q$Text 1<br>Urban planner Hannah Cole argues that cities should eliminate minimum parking requirements, which force developers to build a set number of parking spaces with every new building. These rules, she contends, raise the cost of housing, encourage driving, and waste land that could be used for homes or parks.<br><br>Text 2<br>Transportation researcher David Ng agrees that parking requirements often produce more parking than is needed. But he warns that eliminating them abruptly in neighborhoods with poor public transit could push parking onto residential streets, frustrating residents and generating political backlash. He recommends reducing requirements gradually while improving bus and rail service.$q$, $q$Based on the texts, Cole (Text 1) and Ng (Text 2) would most likely agree with which statement?$q$, $q$["Both would agree that cities should eliminate all parking requirements immediately in every neighborhood.","Both would agree that minimum parking requirements often lead to more parking than is needed.","Both would agree that improving public transit is less important than reducing the cost of housing.","Both would agree that political backlash is the main reason cities have kept parking requirements."]$q$::jsonb, 1, $q$Cole argues the requirements waste land, and Ng "agrees that parking requirements often produce more parking than is needed." They disagree about how quickly to change them.$q$, null),
  ($q$cs-h212$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Cross-Text Connections$q$, 3, $q$Text 1<br>For decades, the standard view held that the domestication of dogs began when humans deliberately captured and tamed wolf pups. Hunters, according to this view, recognized the value of wolves as hunting partners and guards and selectively bred the tamest animals.<br><br>Text 2<br>Biologist Laura Fenn proposes a different account. Wolves that were less fearful of humans, she suggests, began scavenging near human camps, where food scraps were plentiful. Over generations, these wolves became increasingly tolerant of people—in effect, domesticating themselves—long before humans began deliberately breeding them.$q$, $q$Which choice best describes a difference in how the authors of the two texts view the origins of dog domestication?$q$, $q$["Text 1 emphasizes deliberate human action in starting domestication, while Text 2 suggests the process began with changes in the wolves' own behavior.","Text 1 argues that domestication happened quickly, while Text 2 argues that it happened over thousands of years through deliberate breeding.","Text 1 argues that wolves were domesticated as guards, while Text 2 argues that they were domesticated mainly as hunting partners.","Text 1 describes evidence from fossils, while Text 2 relies mainly on observations of how modern wolves behave around people."]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Text 1 describes humans deliberately capturing and breeding wolves, while Fenn suggests that wolves' own scavenging behavior began the process ("domesticating themselves").$q$, null),
  ($q$cs-h213$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Cross-Text Connections$q$, 3, $q$Text 1<br>Many literary critics regard the novel's ambiguous ending—in which the protagonist's fate is never revealed—as a weakness. The author, they argue, raises questions throughout the book that readers reasonably expect to be answered, and the refusal to resolve them feels like an evasion.<br><br>Text 2<br>Critic Amara Diallo contends that the ending is the novel's greatest strength. The book, she notes, is about the uncertainty of life in a city undergoing rapid change, where people disappear from one another's lives without explanation. A tidy resolution, Diallo argues, would betray the experience the novel sets out to depict.$q$, $q$Based on the texts, how would Diallo (Text 2) most likely respond to the critics' view in Text 1?$q$, $q$["Diallo would agree that the ending is a weakness but argue that it is outweighed by the novel's other strengths.","Diallo would argue that the protagonist's fate is in fact revealed if the novel is read carefully enough.","Diallo would agree that readers expect answers but argue that most novels should not provide them.","Diallo would argue that the critics fail to see how the unresolved ending reflects the experience the novel portrays."]$q$::jsonb, 3, $q$Diallo argues that the ambiguity reflects the novel's theme of uncertainty and that a resolution would "betray the experience the novel sets out to depict," directly countering the critics' view that it's a weakness.$q$, null),
  ($q$cs-h214$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Cross-Text Connections$q$, 3, $q$Text 1<br>Advocates of four-day workweeks point to trials in which companies that reduced hours without cutting pay reported stable or even improved productivity. Employees in these trials reported less stress and burnout, and many companies chose to keep the shorter schedule after the trials ended.<br><br>Text 2<br>Economist Sofia Brandt notes that most companies in such trials volunteered to participate, and that they were disproportionately in office-based industries where output is hard to measure precisely. Before concluding that a four-day week works generally, she argues, researchers need to study companies that did not volunteer, including those in manufacturing, health care, and retail.$q$, $q$Based on the texts, how would Brandt (Text 2) most likely respond to the advocates' claims in Text 1?$q$, $q$["Brandt would likely argue that the employees in the trials described in Text 1 overstated how much their stress and burnout had decreased.","Brandt would likely argue that four-day workweeks reduce productivity in office-based industries but not in manufacturing or retail.","Brandt would likely caution that the trials described in Text 1 may not reflect how a four-day week would work across all kinds of businesses.","Brandt would likely agree that the trials prove four-day workweeks are beneficial and urge all companies to adopt them."]$q$::jsonb, 2, $q$Brandt points out that participating companies volunteered and were concentrated in certain industries, so the results may not generalize.$q$, null),
  ($q$cs-h215$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Cross-Text Connections$q$, 3, $q$Text 1<br>Astronomer Elena Ruiz argues that the search for life beyond Earth should focus on planets similar to our own: rocky worlds orbiting at a distance from their stars where liquid water can exist on the surface. Life as we know it requires liquid water, she notes, and searching for familiar conditions is the most efficient use of limited telescope time.<br><br>Text 2<br>Planetary scientist Jonah Webb agrees that liquid water is likely essential for life but argues that the search has been too narrow. Some of the most promising environments in our own solar system, he notes, are not Earth-like at all: Jupiter's moon Europa and Saturn's moon Enceladus are icy worlds with oceans hidden beneath their frozen surfaces, kept liquid by internal heat rather than by sunlight.$q$, $q$Based on the texts, how would Webb (Text 2) most likely respond to Ruiz's argument in Text 1?$q$, $q$["Webb would argue that liquid water is not necessary for life and that scientists should search for life in completely dry environments.","Webb would agree that liquid water is important but argue that Ruiz's approach could overlook places where water exists under very different conditions.","Webb would agree with Ruiz's approach and argue that Europa and Enceladus are too far away to be studied with telescopes.","Webb would argue that scientists should stop searching for life on rocky, Earth-like planets entirely and focus their limited telescope time only on icy moons with hidden oceans."]$q$::jsonb, 1, $q$Webb "agrees that liquid water is likely essential" but says the search "has been too narrow," pointing to icy moons with subsurface oceans—places Ruiz's Earth-like criteria would miss.$q$, null),
  ($q$cs-h216$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Cross-Text Connections$q$, 3, $q$Text 1<br>The long-held explanation for the collapse of Classic Maya cities around 900 CE is a prolonged drought. Sediment cores from lakes in the region show sharp declines in rainfall during the period, and many cities were abandoned as the drought intensified.<br><br>Text 2<br>Archaeologist Rosa Castillo accepts that a severe drought occurred but notes that it affected cities unevenly: some cities in the driest areas survived for centuries, while others in relatively wetter areas collapsed. She argues that the cities' responses to drought—shaped by warfare, political rivalries, and how they managed their water systems—determined which endured.$q$, $q$Based on the texts, how would Castillo (Text 2) most likely respond to the explanation in Text 1?$q$, $q$["Castillo would argue that the drought alone cannot explain why some cities collapsed and others survived.","Castillo would argue that the sediment cores in Text 1 provide no reliable evidence about rainfall during the period.","Castillo would argue that the Classic Maya cities collapsed mainly because of warfare, not because of any drought.","Castillo would agree that the drought explains the collapse and add that it was even more severe than the cores suggest."]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Castillo accepts the drought but notes its uneven effects, arguing that cities' responses, not the drought alone, determined their fate.$q$, null),
  ($q$cs-h217$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Cross-Text Connections$q$, 3, $q$Text 1<br>Psychologist Mark Ellison argues that the habit of multitasking—texting while studying, for instance—harms learning. In his experiments, students who switched between tasks remembered less and made more errors than those who focused on one task at a time. The brain, he concludes, cannot truly do two demanding things at once.<br><br>Text 2<br>Psychologist Dana Kim agrees that switching between demanding tasks reduces performance. Her research, however, found that some combinations of tasks interfere very little: people can listen to instrumental music while doing math, for example, with no measurable loss. The key, Kim argues, is whether the tasks draw on the same mental resources.$q$, $q$Based on the texts, how would Kim (Text 2) most likely respond to Ellison's conclusion in Text 1?$q$, $q$["Kim would argue that Ellison's experiments were flawed because students who multitask perform as well as those who focus.","Kim would argue that listening to music while studying harms learning more than texting while studying does.","Kim would agree with Ellison that people can never perform two activities at once without some loss of performance.","Kim would agree with Ellison's findings about demanding tasks but argue that his conclusion does not apply to every pair of tasks."]$q$::jsonb, 3, $q$Kim agrees that switching between demanding tasks hurts performance but shows some combinations don't interfere, limiting how broadly Ellison's conclusion applies.$q$, null),
  ($q$cs-h218$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Cross-Text Connections$q$, 3, $q$Text 1<br>Supporters of rewilding—restoring ecosystems by reintroducing species that once lived there—point to the return of wolves to Yellowstone National Park in 1995. The wolves reduced the number of elk, they argue, which allowed willows and aspens along rivers to recover, which in turn stabilized riverbanks and brought back beavers and songbirds.<br><br>Text 2<br>Ecologist Tomas Berg considers this story oversimplified. Elk numbers in Yellowstone also fell because of hunting outside the park, drought, and growing populations of bears and cougars. And in many places, he notes, willows have not recovered, because decades of elk browsing and the loss of beavers lowered water tables beyond what wolves alone can reverse.$q$, $q$Based on the texts, how would Berg (Text 2) most likely respond to the supporters' account in Text 1?$q$, $q$["Berg would argue that wolves had no effect on the number of elk in Yellowstone National Park.","Berg would agree with the account in Text 1 and argue that the return of wolves fully explains the recovery of willows, aspens, beavers, and songbirds.","Berg would argue that the account in Text 1 attributes too much of the change to wolves and overlooks other factors.","Berg would argue that the elk population in Yellowstone increased after wolves were reintroduced."]$q$::jsonb, 2, $q$Berg calls the story "oversimplified," citing other causes of elk decline and noting that willows haven't recovered everywhere—so the account gives wolves too much credit.$q$, null),
  ($q$cs-h219$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Cross-Text Connections$q$, 3, $q$Text 1<br>Many art historians regard photography's invention in the 1830s as a threat that pushed painters away from realism. Since cameras could now record appearances faithfully, they argue, painters turned to styles—such as Impressionism—that emphasized what cameras could not capture: color, light, and the subjective experience of seeing.<br><br>Text 2<br>Art historian Nadia Okafor argues that photography did not simply push painters away from realism; it also shaped how they saw. Many Impressionists studied photographs closely, she notes, and borrowed features such as blurred motion and unusual cropping, in which figures are cut off by the edge of the frame—effects first seen in snapshots.$q$, $q$Based on the texts, how would Okafor (Text 2) most likely respond to the art historians' view in Text 1?$q$, $q$["Okafor would argue that photography had little effect on painting during the nineteenth century.","Okafor would argue that the relationship between photography and painting involved influence as well as competition.","Okafor would agree that photography pushed painters away from realism and add that it made painting less popular.","Okafor would argue that Impressionist painters were the first to develop blurred motion and unusual cropping."]$q$::jsonb, 1, $q$Okafor argues that photography "did not simply push painters away" but "also shaped how they saw," with Impressionists borrowing its effects—influence alongside the competition described in Text 1.$q$, null),
  ($q$cs-h220$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Cross-Text Connections$q$, 3, $q$Text 1<br>Economist Rafael Ortiz argues that remote work will permanently reduce demand for office space in large cities. Companies have discovered that many employees can work productively from home, he notes, and they are unlikely to keep paying for offices that sit mostly empty.<br><br>Text 2<br>Real-estate analyst June Park agrees that overall demand for office space has fallen but argues that the decline is uneven. Older buildings with poor ventilation and few amenities are emptying, she notes, while newer buildings with modern features are attracting tenants. Companies, Park contends, are not abandoning offices so much as trading down in quantity and up in quality.$q$, $q$Based on the texts, Ortiz (Text 1) and Park (Text 2) would most likely agree with which statement?$q$, $q$["Both would agree that overall demand for office space has declined.","Both would agree that remote work has made employees less productive.","Both would agree that newer office buildings are losing tenants as quickly as older ones.","Both would agree that companies will soon return to using as much office space as before."]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Ortiz predicts reduced demand, and Park "agrees that overall demand for office space has fallen," though she sees the decline as uneven.$q$, null),
  ($q$cs-h221$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Cross-Text Connections$q$, 3, $q$Text 1<br>Some linguists argue that emojis are degrading written communication. Instead of crafting precise sentences to express feelings, they contend, writers increasingly rely on a limited set of small pictures whose meanings are vague and easily misunderstood.<br><br>Text 2<br>Linguist Carmen Ibáñez sees emojis differently. In face-to-face conversation, she notes, people convey tone through facial expressions and gestures; in text messages, those cues are missing, and misunderstandings are common. Emojis, she argues, restore some of this lost information, functioning less as replacements for words than as a kind of written body language.$q$, $q$Based on the texts, how would Ibáñez (Text 2) most likely respond to the linguists' argument in Text 1?$q$, $q$["Ibáñez would agree that emojis are vague and easily misunderstood but argue that writers should use even more of them in order to make their meaning clearer to readers.","Ibáñez would argue that emojis are more precise than words and should replace written sentences in most messages.","Ibáñez would agree that emojis degrade writing but argue that face-to-face conversation is degrading as well.","Ibáñez would argue that the linguists misunderstand the purpose emojis serve, which is to add cues that text otherwise lacks rather than to replace precise writing."]$q$::jsonb, 3, $q$Ibáñez says emojis function "less as replacements for words than as a kind of written body language," countering the claim that they replace precise sentences.$q$, null),
  ($q$cs-h222$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Cross-Text Connections$q$, 3, $q$Text 1<br>Nutrition researchers have long recommended that people eat breakfast, citing studies showing that breakfast eaters tend to weigh less than people who skip the meal. Breakfast, the reasoning goes, prevents overeating later in the day.<br><br>Text 2<br>Epidemiologist Kenji Watanabe points out that most such studies were observational: they compared people who already ate breakfast with people who didn't. Breakfast eaters may differ in other ways, such as exercising more or sleeping better. In randomized trials, in which participants were assigned to eat or skip breakfast, Watanabe notes, breakfast had little effect on weight.$q$, $q$Based on the texts, how would Watanabe (Text 2) most likely respond to the reasoning in Text 1?$q$, $q$["Watanabe would argue that skipping breakfast causes people to weigh less than people who eat breakfast.","Watanabe would agree with the recommendation in Text 1 and argue that it is supported by randomized trials.","Watanabe would argue that the evidence cited in Text 1 does not show that breakfast itself causes lower weight.","Watanabe would argue that people who eat breakfast exercise less and sleep worse than people who skip it."]$q$::jsonb, 2, $q$Watanabe notes that observational studies can't separate breakfast from other differences and that randomized trials found little effect—so the cited evidence doesn't establish causation.$q$, null),
  ($q$cs-h223$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Cross-Text Connections$q$, 3, $q$Text 1<br>Architect Paolo Ferri argues that new buildings in historic districts should closely imitate the styles of their older neighbors. Buildings that clash with their surroundings, he contends, disrupt the visual harmony that gives such districts their character and value.<br><br>Text 2<br>Architect Lin Zhao believes that imitation produces lifeless copies. New buildings in historic districts, she argues, should be recognizably contemporary but respectful of their surroundings—matching the height, scale, and materials of nearby buildings without reproducing their decorative details.$q$, $q$Based on the texts, Ferri (Text 1) and Zhao (Text 2) would most likely agree with which statement?$q$, $q$["Both would agree that new buildings in historic districts should reproduce the decorative details of older buildings.","Both would agree that new buildings in historic districts should be designed with their surroundings in mind.","Both would agree that historic districts should not allow any new buildings to be constructed.","Both would agree that contemporary styles disrupt the character of historic districts."]$q$::jsonb, 1, $q$Ferri wants imitation to preserve harmony; Zhao wants buildings to be "respectful of their surroundings" in height, scale, and materials. Both think surroundings should shape the design.$q$, null),
  ($q$cs-h224$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Cross-Text Connections$q$, 3, $q$Text 1<br>For much of the twentieth century, scientists believed that the human appendix was a useless remnant of our evolutionary past, a structure that had once helped our ancestors digest plants but now served no function.<br><br>Text 2<br>Immunologist Grace Odum argues that the appendix may serve as a "safe house" for beneficial gut bacteria. After an illness such as severe diarrhea flushes the intestines, she proposes, bacteria sheltered in the appendix can recolonize the gut. Supporting this idea, she notes that people without an appendix appear somewhat more likely to experience recurrent intestinal infections.$q$, $q$Based on the texts, how would Odum (Text 2) most likely respond to the view described in Text 1?$q$, $q$["Odum would argue that the appendix may have a function that the earlier view failed to recognize.","Odum would agree that the appendix is a useless remnant but argue that it should not be removed.","Odum would argue that the appendix once helped human ancestors digest plants more efficiently.","Odum would argue that bacteria in the appendix cause most intestinal infections in humans."]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Odum proposes a function (sheltering beneficial bacteria), contradicting the view that the appendix is useless.$q$, null),
  ($q$cs-h225$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Cross-Text Connections$q$, 3, $q$Text 1<br>Many educators argue that students learn best when they discover concepts on their own. Rather than being told how to solve a problem, students who explore and experiment, these educators contend, develop deeper understanding and remember what they learn longer.<br><br>Text 2<br>Cognitive scientist Paul Mercer acknowledges that discovery can be engaging, but he argues that for beginners it is often inefficient. Novices, he notes, lack the background knowledge to know what to look for, so they may struggle for long periods or draw incorrect conclusions. Explicit instruction, followed by opportunities to practice and explore, tends to produce better results for students new to a subject.$q$, $q$Based on the texts, how would Mercer (Text 2) most likely respond to the educators' argument in Text 1?$q$, $q$["Mercer would argue that students should never be allowed to explore or experiment on their own.","Mercer would agree that discovery learning produces deeper and longer-lasting understanding for all students, regardless of their background knowledge.","Mercer would argue that experienced students learn less from discovery than beginners do.","Mercer would argue that the approach in Text 1 may be less effective for students who are new to a subject."]$q$::jsonb, 3, $q$Mercer says discovery is "often inefficient" for beginners and that explicit instruction works better for novices.$q$, null),
  ($q$cs-h226$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Cross-Text Connections$q$, 3, $q$Text 1<br>Historian Liam O'Connor argues that the Irish Famine of the 1840s was caused by a natural disaster: a potato blight that destroyed the main food source of millions of people. Once the blight struck, he contends, mass starvation was nearly unavoidable given the limited agricultural technology of the time.<br><br>Text 2<br>Historian Siobhán Murphy agrees that the blight triggered the famine but argues that its devastating scale was not inevitable. During the worst years, she notes, Ireland continued to export large quantities of grain and livestock to Britain, and relief efforts were limited by policies that favored free markets over aid.$q$, $q$Based on the texts, how would Murphy (Text 2) most likely respond to O'Connor's argument in Text 1?$q$, $q$["Murphy would argue that the potato blight played no role in causing the famine.","Murphy would agree that mass starvation was unavoidable once the blight struck.","Murphy would argue that O'Connor overlooks human decisions that worsened the famine.","Murphy would argue that Ireland imported too much grain from Britain during the famine."]$q$::jsonb, 2, $q$Murphy accepts the blight as the trigger but points to exports and policy choices as reasons the famine was so severe, challenging O'Connor's claim that starvation was "nearly unavoidable."$q$, null),
  ($q$cs-h227$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Cross-Text Connections$q$, 3, $q$Text 1<br>Some researchers argue that social media use is a major cause of rising rates of anxiety and depression among teenagers. They point to the fact that these rates began climbing sharply around 2012, the year smartphones became widespread among adolescents.<br><br>Text 2<br>Psychologist Ana Ferreira cautions that the timing alone does not establish cause. Many other changes occurred around the same time, she notes, including economic pressures following the 2008 financial crisis and changes in how mental health conditions were diagnosed and reported. Studies that track individual teenagers over time, Ferreira says, have found links between social media use and well-being, but those links are generally small.$q$, $q$Based on the texts, how would Ferreira (Text 2) most likely respond to the researchers' claim in Text 1?$q$, $q$["Ferreira would argue that social media has no effect at all on teenagers' mental health.","Ferreira would argue that the evidence in Text 1 is not sufficient to show that social media is a major cause of the increase.","Ferreira would agree that social media is the main cause of the increase and argue that studies tracking individual teenagers confirm a strong effect.","Ferreira would argue that smartphones became widespread among adolescents well before 2012."]$q$::jsonb, 1, $q$Ferreira says timing alone doesn't establish cause, notes other changes, and reports that measured links are small—so the evidence doesn't show social media is a major cause.$q$, null),
  ($q$cs-h228$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Cross-Text Connections$q$, 3, $q$Text 1<br>Composer and critic Rhea Lindgren argues that recordings have narrowed how classical music is performed. When listeners grow familiar with a few famous recordings, she contends, they come to expect every performance to sound the same, and performers feel pressure to conform rather than to offer fresh interpretations.<br><br>Text 2<br>Musicologist Ivan Petrov notes that recordings have also made a far wider range of interpretations available than ever before. A listener today can compare dozens of versions of the same symphony, including historical recordings that reveal performance styles that have since disappeared. This access, he argues, has inspired many performers to experiment.$q$, $q$Based on the texts, how would Petrov (Text 2) most likely respond to Lindgren's argument in Text 1?$q$, $q$["Petrov would argue that recordings have encouraged variety in performance as well as the conformity Lindgren describes.","Petrov would agree that recordings have led listeners to expect every performance of a symphony to sound like the few most famous recordings of it.","Petrov would argue that listeners today are less familiar with classical music than listeners in the past.","Petrov would argue that historical recordings should replace live performances of classical music."]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Petrov stresses that recordings make many interpretations available and have "inspired many performers to experiment," a counterweight to Lindgren's claim of narrowing.$q$, null),
  ($q$cs-h229$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Cross-Text Connections$q$, 3, $q$Text 1<br>Many conservationists argue that zoos play a vital role in protecting endangered species. Breeding programs in zoos, they note, have saved several species from extinction, including the California condor and the Arabian oryx, both of which have since been returned to the wild.<br><br>Text 2<br>Biologist Maya Torres agrees that a handful of zoo breeding programs have been remarkable successes. But she points out that the vast majority of animals in zoos belong to species that are not endangered, and that few zoo breeding programs ever release animals into the wild. Zoos, she argues, should devote far more of their resources to the species most at risk.$q$, $q$Based on the texts, how would Torres (Text 2) most likely respond to the conservationists' argument in Text 1?$q$, $q$["Torres would argue that the condor and oryx programs failed to return animals to the wild successfully.","Torres would argue that zoos should stop all breeding programs and focus only on public education.","Torres would agree that most animals in zoos belong to endangered species that will eventually be released.","Torres would accept the examples in Text 1 but argue that they are not typical of what most zoos do."]$q$::jsonb, 3, $q$Torres "agrees that a handful of zoo breeding programs have been remarkable successes" but notes that most zoo animals aren't endangered and few programs release animals—so the examples are exceptions.$q$, null),
  ($q$cs-h230$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Cross-Text Connections$q$, 3, $q$Text 1<br>Literary scholar Ruth Albright argues that the poet's frequent images of birds should be read as symbols of freedom. In poem after poem, birds escape cages, cross borders, and rise above the troubles of the human characters, embodying the liberty the poet's speakers long for.<br><br>Text 2<br>Scholar Hassan Ali reads the same images differently. Many of the poet's birds, he notes, are injured, trapped, or migrating under threat; in one poem, a flock is driven from its marsh by drainage. For Ali, the birds represent not escape from danger but the vulnerability of creatures caught up in forces they cannot control.$q$, $q$Which choice best describes a difference in how Albright and Ali interpret the poet's bird images?$q$, $q$["Albright focuses on the poet's early poems, while Ali focuses on the poet's later poems.","Albright argues that the birds are realistic descriptions, while Ali argues that they are symbols.","Albright sees the bird images as expressing liberation, while Ali sees them as expressing vulnerability.","Albright argues that the poet admired birds, while Ali argues that the poet feared them."]$q$::jsonb, 2, $q$Albright reads the birds as symbols of freedom; Ali reads them as images of creatures caught in forces beyond their control.$q$, null),
  ($q$cs-h231$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Cross-Text Connections$q$, 3, $q$Text 1<br>Proponents of nuclear power argue that it is essential for reducing carbon emissions. Nuclear plants produce large amounts of electricity without burning fossil fuels, and unlike solar and wind power, they generate electricity continuously regardless of the weather.<br><br>Text 2<br>Energy analyst Rosa Lindqvist does not dispute nuclear power's low emissions. But she notes that new nuclear plants in several countries have taken more than a decade to build and have run billions of dollars over budget. In the time and with the money required to build one plant, she argues, a country could install enough wind, solar, and battery storage to cut more emissions sooner.$q$, $q$Based on the texts, how would Lindqvist (Text 2) most likely respond to the proponents' argument in Text 1?$q$, $q$["Lindqvist would argue that nuclear power produces more carbon emissions than its proponents admit.","Lindqvist would argue that the advantages described in Text 1 may be outweighed by the cost and time needed to build new plants.","Lindqvist would agree that nuclear power is essential for reducing emissions and argue that countries should build many more plants as quickly as possible.","Lindqvist would argue that wind and solar power generate electricity continuously regardless of the weather."]$q$::jsonb, 1, $q$Lindqvist accepts low emissions but argues that slow, costly construction makes renewables a faster, cheaper way to cut emissions.$q$, null),
  ($q$cs-h232$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Cross-Text Connections$q$, 3, $q$Text 1<br>The traditional view of Neanderthals portrays them as brutish and unsophisticated, incapable of the symbolic thought that characterized early modern humans. For decades, the absence of Neanderthal art seemed to support this view.<br><br>Text 2<br>Recent discoveries have challenged that picture. In caves in Spain, researchers have found red pigment designs dated to more than 64,000 years ago—before modern humans are known to have arrived in Europe. Archaeologist Elena Duarte argues that these designs, if their dating holds up, can only have been made by Neanderthals, suggesting a capacity for symbolic thought.$q$, $q$Based on the texts, how would Duarte (Text 2) most likely respond to the traditional view described in Text 1?$q$, $q$["Duarte would argue that new evidence, if confirmed, undermines a key support for the traditional view.","Duarte would argue that modern humans arrived in Europe much earlier than researchers previously believed.","Duarte would agree that Neanderthals were incapable of symbolic thought but argue that they used pigments.","Duarte would argue that the absence of Neanderthal art proves that they lived only in Spain."]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Text 1 says the absence of Neanderthal art supported the traditional view; Duarte argues that cave designs, if correctly dated, show Neanderthals made art.$q$, null),
  ($q$cs-h233$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Cross-Text Connections$q$, 3, $q$Text 1<br>Economist Henry Cho argues that tariffs—taxes on imported goods—protect domestic industries and the jobs they provide. When foreign goods become more expensive, he contends, consumers buy more domestic products, and factories that might otherwise have closed are able to stay open.<br><br>Text 2<br>Economist Leila Haddad acknowledges that tariffs can save jobs in protected industries. But she notes that they also raise prices for consumers and for businesses that rely on imported materials. A tariff on steel, for example, may save jobs at steel mills while costing jobs at the many companies that use steel to make cars, appliances, and machinery.$q$, $q$Based on the texts, how would Haddad (Text 2) most likely respond to Cho's argument in Text 1?$q$, $q$["Haddad would argue that tariffs never save jobs in the industries they are designed to protect.","Haddad would agree with Cho's argument and add that tariffs lower prices for consumers.","Haddad would argue that a tariff on steel costs more jobs at steel mills than it saves at the companies that use steel to make cars and appliances.","Haddad would accept part of Cho's argument but argue that it overlooks costs that tariffs impose elsewhere in the economy."]$q$::jsonb, 3, $q$Haddad "acknowledges that tariffs can save jobs in protected industries" but points to higher prices and job losses in industries that use imported materials.$q$, null),
  ($q$cs-h234$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Cross-Text Connections$q$, 3, $q$Text 1<br>Some researchers believe that animals such as chimpanzees and dolphins possess a form of culture: behaviors, such as tool use or particular calls, that are learned from others and differ from one group to another. Different chimpanzee communities, for example, use different techniques to crack nuts.<br><br>Text 2<br>Psychologist Gregor Weiss is skeptical. Group differences in behavior, he argues, may reflect differences in local environments—the types of nuts or stones available—rather than socially learned traditions. Only experiments that rule out environmental explanations, he contends, can establish that animals have culture in a meaningful sense.$q$, $q$Based on the texts, how would Weiss (Text 2) most likely respond to the researchers' view in Text 1?$q$, $q$["Weiss would argue that chimpanzees do not use tools to crack nuts in any of their communities.","Weiss would agree that animals possess culture and argue that dolphins have more complex culture than chimpanzees.","Weiss would argue that the evidence described in Text 1 may have an explanation other than culture.","Weiss would argue that environmental differences cannot affect animal behavior in any meaningful way."]$q$::jsonb, 2, $q$Weiss suggests group differences may reflect local environments rather than learned traditions, offering an alternative explanation for the evidence in Text 1.$q$, null),
  ($q$cs-h235$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Cross-Text Connections$q$, 3, $q$Text 1<br>Many historians credit Florence Nightingale's reforms during the Crimean War with dramatically reducing deaths among British soldiers. When she arrived at the army hospital in Scutari in 1854, they note, conditions were filthy, and the death rate fell sharply after she introduced better nursing practices.<br><br>Text 2<br>Historian Philip Grant points out that the death rate at Scutari actually rose during Nightingale's first months there. It fell sharply only after a government sanitary commission arrived in 1855 and flushed out the hospital's sewers, which had been contaminating its water supply. Nightingale, Grant notes, later studied these statistics and became a lifelong champion of sanitation.$q$, $q$Based on the texts, how would Grant (Text 2) most likely respond to the historians' claim in Text 1?$q$, $q$["Grant would argue that conditions at the hospital in Scutari were clean when Nightingale arrived in 1854.","Grant would argue that the decline in deaths at Scutari is better explained by the sanitary commission's work than by Nightingale's initial reforms.","Grant would agree that Nightingale's improved nursing practices caused the death rate at Scutari to fall immediately after she arrived at the hospital in 1854.","Grant would argue that Nightingale opposed the sanitary commission's efforts to repair the hospital's sewers."]$q$::jsonb, 1, $q$Grant notes deaths fell only after the sanitary commission fixed the sewers, suggesting the decline credited to Nightingale is better attributed to the commission.$q$, null),
  ($q$cs-h236$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Cross-Text Connections$q$, 3, $q$Text 1<br>Critics of standardized testing argue that test scores largely reflect students' family income rather than their abilities. Wealthier families can afford tutoring and test preparation, they note, giving their children an unfair advantage.<br><br>Text 2<br>Education researcher Monica Hale agrees that test scores correlate with family income. But she notes that other measures used in admissions, such as essays, extracurricular activities, and recommendation letters, correlate with income even more strongly. Eliminating tests, Hale argues, may make admissions less fair to low-income students, not more.$q$, $q$Based on the texts, the critics in Text 1 and Hale (Text 2) would most likely agree with which statement?$q$, $q$["Both would agree that test scores are related to students' family income.","Both would agree that standardized tests should be eliminated from admissions.","Both would agree that essays are a fairer measure of ability than test scores.","Both would agree that tutoring has no effect on students' test scores."]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$The critics argue that scores reflect income, and Hale "agrees that test scores correlate with family income," though they disagree about what to do.$q$, null),
  ($q$cs-h237$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Cross-Text Connections$q$, 3, $q$Text 1<br>Novelist Sarah Kim argues that fiction is best written from personal experience. Writers who invent worlds they have never known, she contends, inevitably produce work that feels false, because readers can sense when an author does not truly understand what she describes.<br><br>Text 2<br>Novelist Tomás Reyes argues that imagination is the essence of fiction. Some of the most convincing novels ever written, he notes, depict places and times their authors never experienced. What matters, Reyes says, is not whether a writer has lived something but whether she has done the work—through research, observation, and empathy—to understand it.$q$, $q$Based on the texts, how would Reyes (Text 2) most likely respond to Kim's argument in Text 1?$q$, $q$["Reyes would agree that novels about unfamiliar places inevitably feel false to readers.","Reyes would argue that writers should avoid researching the settings of their novels.","Reyes would argue that personal experience makes fiction less convincing to readers.","Reyes would argue that understanding can come from sources other than direct personal experience."]$q$::jsonb, 3, $q$Reyes says the key is understanding, which writers can gain "through research, observation, and empathy," not only through living something.$q$, null),
  ($q$cs-h238$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Cross-Text Connections$q$, 3, $q$Text 1<br>Marine biologists have long assumed that the ocean's deepest trenches are nearly lifeless, too cold, dark, and high-pressure to support more than a few hardy species of microbes and scavengers.<br><br>Text 2<br>In 2022, a team led by biologist Ana Silva used robotic landers to survey a trench more than 8,000 meters deep. They recorded dozens of species, including fish, shrimp-like amphipods, and sea cucumbers, many feeding on organic material that drifts down from the surface and collects at the trench floor. Silva argues that trenches may be hotspots of deep-sea life rather than barren extremes.$q$, $q$Based on the texts, how would Silva (Text 2) most likely respond to the assumption described in Text 1?$q$, $q$["Silva would agree that deep trenches are nearly lifeless and argue that the species she found were rare exceptions.","Silva would argue that most life in deep trenches consists of microbes rather than animals.","Silva would argue that the assumption in Text 1 underestimates the variety of life in deep trenches.","Silva would argue that organic material from the surface never reaches the floors of deep trenches."]$q$::jsonb, 2, $q$Silva's survey found dozens of animal species and led her to suggest trenches may be "hotspots" of life, contradicting the assumption of near-lifelessness.$q$, null),
  ($q$cs-h239$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Cross-Text Connections$q$, 3, $q$Text 1<br>Historian Daniel Reiss argues that the Industrial Revolution began in Britain because of its abundant coal deposits. Cheap, accessible coal powered steam engines and iron furnaces, he contends, giving British manufacturers an advantage no other country could match.<br><br>Text 2<br>Historian Priya Nair agrees that coal was important but argues that it cannot be the whole story: China also had large coal deposits. Britain's distinctive advantage, she contends, was its unusually high wages, which gave manufacturers a strong incentive to invest in labor-saving machines. Where labor was cheap, there was less reason to mechanize.$q$, $q$Based on the texts, how would Nair (Text 2) most likely respond to Reiss's argument in Text 1?$q$, $q$["Nair would argue that coal played no meaningful role in the Industrial Revolution and that Britain's high wages alone explain why it industrialized first.","Nair would agree that coal mattered but argue that Reiss's explanation cannot account for why industrialization began in Britain rather than elsewhere.","Nair would agree with Reiss and add that China lacked the coal needed to industrialize.","Nair would argue that low wages in Britain discouraged manufacturers from investing in machines."]$q$::jsonb, 1, $q$Nair "agrees that coal was important" but notes that China also had coal, so coal alone can't explain Britain's lead; she adds high wages as the distinctive factor.$q$, null),
  ($q$cs-h240$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Cross-Text Connections$q$, 3, $q$Text 1<br>Some city leaders argue that building more housing will lower rents. When the supply of apartments rises faster than demand, they reason, landlords must compete for tenants, and prices fall.<br><br>Text 2<br>Urban economist Jada Brooks agrees that increasing supply tends to moderate rents over time. But she notes that new construction is usually expensive, high-end housing, and its effects on rents in lower-income neighborhoods can take years to appear. In the meantime, she argues, cities need policies such as rental assistance to protect vulnerable tenants.$q$, $q$Based on the texts, how would Brooks (Text 2) most likely respond to the city leaders' argument in Text 1?$q$, $q$["Brooks would accept the reasoning in Text 1 but argue that its benefits may be slow to reach lower-income renters.","Brooks would argue that building more housing, especially expensive high-end housing, causes rents to rise in nearby lower-income neighborhoods.","Brooks would argue that cities should stop building high-end housing altogether.","Brooks would agree that new housing immediately lowers rents in all neighborhoods."]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Brooks "agrees that increasing supply tends to moderate rents" but notes that effects reach lower-income areas slowly, so other measures are needed meanwhile.$q$, null),
  ($q$cs-h241$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Cross-Text Connections$q$, 3, $q$Text 1<br>Ornithologist Kevin Marsh argues that songbirds learn their songs primarily by imitating adult birds during a critical period early in life. Birds raised in isolation, he notes, produce abnormal songs, which shows that hearing adults is essential.<br><br>Text 2<br>Ornithologist Hana Sato agrees that young birds learn from adults but has found that, in some species, birds continue to modify their songs throughout their lives, adding new phrases they hear from neighbors. Song learning, she argues, is not confined to a single early period in every species.$q$, $q$Based on the texts, how would Sato (Text 2) most likely respond to Marsh's claim in Text 1?$q$, $q$["Sato would argue that birds raised in isolation produce normal songs.","Sato would argue that young birds do not learn songs from adult birds.","Sato would agree with Marsh that, in every species, song learning ends after a critical period early in life, once young birds have heard adults sing.","Sato would agree that learning from adults is important but argue that Marsh's account of when learning occurs does not apply to all species."]$q$::jsonb, 3, $q$Sato agrees birds learn from adults but finds that some species keep modifying songs throughout life, so learning isn't always confined to an early period.$q$, null),
  ($q$cs-h242$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Cross-Text Connections$q$, 3, $q$Text 1<br>Many critics argue that film adaptations of novels inevitably fall short of their sources. A novel can explore a character's inner thoughts at length, they note, while a film is limited to what can be shown and heard.<br><br>Text 2<br>Film scholar Olivia Grant contends that this comparison misses the point. Film has its own resources—camera movement, editing, music, an actor's face in close-up—that can convey inner life in ways prose cannot. The best adaptations, she argues, do not try to reproduce the novel but to translate it into a different art form.$q$, $q$Based on the texts, how would Grant (Text 2) most likely respond to the critics' argument in Text 1?$q$, $q$["Grant would agree that film adaptations inevitably fall short of the novels on which they are based.","Grant would argue that novels are unable to explore characters' inner thoughts in depth.","Grant would argue that the critics underestimate film's own ways of conveying characters' inner lives.","Grant would argue that the best adaptations reproduce their source novels as closely as possible."]$q$::jsonb, 2, $q$Grant says film has "its own resources" that can convey inner life, countering the critics' claim that film is limited.$q$, null),
  ($q$cs-h243$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Cross-Text Connections$q$, 3, $q$Text 1<br>Some nutrition experts argue that people should avoid all processed foods. Processed foods, they contend, are generally high in salt, sugar, and unhealthy fats, and diets rich in them are linked to obesity and heart disease.<br><br>Text 2<br>Food scientist Raj Patel notes that "processed" covers an enormous range of foods, from sugary snacks to frozen vegetables, canned beans, and whole-grain bread. Many processed foods, he argues, are nutritious and affordable, and advice to avoid all of them may discourage people from eating healthy options that fit their budgets.$q$, $q$Based on the texts, how would Patel (Text 2) most likely respond to the experts' argument in Text 1?$q$, $q$["Patel would argue that sugary snacks are as nutritious as frozen vegetables and canned beans.","Patel would argue that the experts' advice treats a very diverse category of foods as if all were equally unhealthy.","Patel would agree that people should avoid all processed foods, including frozen vegetables and canned beans, because of their salt, sugar, and fat.","Patel would argue that diets high in salt and sugar are not linked to heart disease."]$q$::jsonb, 1, $q$Patel points out that "processed" includes many nutritious foods, so blanket avoidance is misguided.$q$, null),
  ($q$cs-h244$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Cross-Text Connections$q$, 3, $q$Text 1<br>Many astronomers believe that Planet Nine—a hypothetical planet several times the mass of Earth—orbits far beyond Neptune. They point to a group of distant icy objects whose orbits are oddly clustered, which they argue could be explained by the gravitational pull of an unseen planet.<br><br>Text 2<br>Astronomer Felix Wong is unconvinced. The clustering, he notes, may be an illusion created by where telescopes have looked: surveys have observed only certain regions of the sky, so they may have detected only objects whose orbits happen to point in those directions. More complete surveys, Wong argues, are needed before inferring a new planet.$q$, $q$Based on the texts, how would Wong (Text 2) most likely respond to the astronomers' argument in Text 1?$q$, $q$["Wong would argue that the evidence cited in Text 1 might reflect how the observations were made rather than a real pattern.","Wong would argue that Planet Nine has already been observed directly by telescopes.","Wong would agree that the clustered orbits of the distant icy objects prove that a planet several times Earth's mass exists beyond Neptune.","Wong would argue that the distant icy objects do not orbit the Sun at all."]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Wong suggests the clustering may be an artifact of survey coverage (an observational bias) rather than evidence of a planet.$q$, null),
  ($q$cs-h245$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Cross-Text Connections$q$, 3, $q$Text 1<br>Sociologist Martin Hale argues that public libraries are becoming obsolete. With so much information available online, he contends, fewer people need to visit a building to borrow books or look up facts, and cities should reconsider how much they spend on libraries.<br><br>Text 2<br>Librarian and researcher Teresa Cruz notes that library visits in many cities have remained steady or grown. Libraries, she explains, increasingly serve as community centers: they provide free internet access, job-search help, children's programs, and quiet places to study—services especially valuable to people who lack them at home.$q$, $q$Based on the texts, how would Cruz (Text 2) most likely respond to Hale's argument in Text 1?$q$, $q$["Cruz would agree that libraries are becoming obsolete and argue that cities should close them.","Cruz would argue that most people can find all the information they need without using the internet.","Cruz would argue that libraries should stop offering internet access and job-search help and should instead focus on lending books to their patrons.","Cruz would argue that Hale's view overlooks the many roles libraries play beyond lending books and providing facts."]$q$::jsonb, 3, $q$Cruz describes libraries' expanded community roles and steady use, countering Hale's narrow view of their function.$q$, null),
  ($q$cs-h246$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Cross-Text Connections$q$, 3, $q$Text 1<br>Researchers studying sleep have found that teenagers' internal clocks shift during puberty, making them naturally inclined to fall asleep and wake later. Many sleep scientists therefore recommend that high schools start no earlier than 8:30 a.m.<br><br>Text 2<br>When one school district moved its high school start time from 7:30 to 8:45 a.m., researchers tracked the results. Students slept about forty minutes more per night on average, attendance improved, and grades rose modestly. Some parents complained about the later schedule's effect on after-school activities and jobs.$q$, $q$Based on the texts, which choice best describes the relationship between Text 1 and Text 2?$q$, $q$["The findings in Text 2 show that the recommendation in Text 1 had no measurable effect on how much sleep students got or on their attendance.","The findings in Text 2 suggest that teenagers' internal clocks do not actually shift during puberty.","The findings in Text 2 provide support for the recommendation described in Text 1, though they also note a drawback.","The findings in Text 2 show that earlier start times improve students' attendance and grades."]$q$::jsonb, 2, $q$Text 2's results (more sleep, better attendance and grades after a later start) support Text 1's recommendation, while noting parents' complaints.$q$, null),
  ($q$cs-h247$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Cross-Text Connections$q$, 3, $q$Text 1<br>Many scholars have described the medieval Silk Road trade as dominated by luxury goods: silk, spices, and precious stones that only the wealthy could afford. Because these goods were light and valuable, they argue, they were worth transporting over thousands of miles.<br><br>Text 2<br>Historian Mei Lin argues that this emphasis on luxuries distorts the picture. Documents from oasis towns along the route, she notes, record large volumes of ordinary goods—grain, livestock, cloth, and pottery—traded over shorter distances between neighboring communities. The long-distance luxury trade, she contends, rested on a much larger foundation of local exchange.$q$, $q$Based on the texts, how would Lin (Text 2) most likely respond to the scholars' description in Text 1?$q$, $q$["Lin would argue that luxury goods were never transported over long distances on the Silk Road.","Lin would argue that the scholars' account neglects the everyday trade that made up much of the commerce along the routes.","Lin would agree that the Silk Road trade consisted almost entirely of light, valuable luxury goods that only the wealthy could afford to buy.","Lin would argue that grain and livestock were more valuable than silk and spices."]$q$::jsonb, 1, $q$Lin argues that documents show large volumes of ordinary goods traded locally, so a focus on luxuries leaves out much of the trade.$q$, null),
  ($q$cs-h248$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Cross-Text Connections$q$, 3, $q$Text 1<br>Psychologist Laura Benson argues that praising children for their intelligence can backfire. Children praised for being "smart," her experiments show, tend to avoid challenging tasks afterward, apparently fearing that failure would reveal they are not smart after all.<br><br>Text 2<br>Psychologist Omar Haddad has tried to replicate Benson's experiments with larger groups of children. He found the same pattern, but the effect was considerably smaller than in her original studies. Haddad suggests that the way children are praised matters, but perhaps less than early studies implied.$q$, $q$Based on the texts, how would Haddad (Text 2) most likely respond to Benson's findings in Text 1?$q$, $q$["Haddad would agree with the direction of Benson's finding but suggest that its size may have been overstated.","Haddad would argue that praising children for intelligence makes them more likely to seek challenges.","Haddad would argue that Benson's experiments could not be replicated with larger groups.","Haddad would agree that the effect Benson found is as large as her original studies suggested."]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Haddad "found the same pattern" but a smaller effect, suggesting the original studies overstated its size.$q$, null),
  ($q$cs-h249$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Cross-Text Connections$q$, 3, $q$Text 1<br>Some art critics argue that museums should display art without explanatory labels. Labels, they contend, tell viewers what to think and see, preventing them from forming their own responses to the work.<br><br>Text 2<br>Curator Daniela Ross agrees that labels can be overbearing, especially when they dictate a single interpretation. But for many visitors, she argues, a short label providing context—when and where a work was made, or what its symbols meant to its original audience—opens up the work rather than closing it off, particularly for art from unfamiliar cultures.$q$, $q$Based on the texts, how would Ross (Text 2) most likely respond to the critics' argument in Text 1?$q$, $q$["Ross would argue that labels should always tell viewers how a work should be interpreted, especially when the art comes from an unfamiliar culture.","Ross would agree that museums should remove all labels from works of art.","Ross would argue that art from unfamiliar cultures should not be displayed in museums.","Ross would agree that some labels limit viewers' responses but argue that well-designed labels can enrich them."]$q$::jsonb, 3, $q$Ross concedes labels can be "overbearing" but argues contextual labels open up works for many visitors.$q$, null),
  ($q$cs-h250$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Cross-Text Connections$q$, 3, $q$Text 1<br>Geologists have traditionally explained the Grand Canyon as the product of the Colorado River slowly cutting through rock over about six million years.<br><br>Text 2<br>Geologist Karen Holt argues that parts of the canyon are far older. Analyzing minerals in the canyon walls that record when the rock was last near the surface, her team concluded that some segments were carved by ancient rivers as much as 70 million years ago, long before the modern Colorado River existed. The modern river, Holt suggests, linked together and deepened canyons that already existed.$q$, $q$Based on the texts, how would Holt (Text 2) most likely respond to the traditional explanation in Text 1?$q$, $q$["Holt would argue that the Colorado River played no role in shaping the Grand Canyon.","Holt would agree that the entire Grand Canyon was carved by the Colorado River within the last six million years, as geologists have long believed.","Holt would argue that the traditional explanation leaves out an earlier history of carving by other rivers.","Holt would argue that the Grand Canyon was formed by glaciers rather than by rivers."]$q$::jsonb, 2, $q$Holt argues that ancient rivers carved some segments long before the Colorado existed, which the six-million-year explanation does not account for; she still assigns the modern river a role.$q$, null),
  ($q$cs-h251$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Cross-Text Connections$q$, 3, $q$Text 1<br>Economist Nina Park argues that universal basic income—regular cash payments to all citizens regardless of employment—would reduce people's motivation to work. If people could meet their basic needs without a job, she contends, many would leave the workforce.<br><br>Text 2<br>Economist Samuel Okoye points to several pilot programs in which participants received guaranteed payments. In most, employment declined only slightly or not at all; some recipients used the money to search for better jobs, start businesses, or complete training. Okoye cautions, though, that pilots were small and temporary, and that permanent programs might have different effects.$q$, $q$Based on the texts, how would Okoye (Text 2) most likely respond to Park's argument in Text 1?$q$, $q$["Okoye would agree with Park that guaranteed payments cause most people to leave the workforce.","Okoye would argue that pilot evidence does not support Park's prediction, while acknowledging the evidence has limits.","Okoye would argue that permanent guaranteed-income programs have already proven more effective at encouraging work than the small pilots did.","Okoye would argue that universal basic income should be given only to people without jobs."]$q$::jsonb, 1, $q$Okoye's evidence shows little decline in work, countering Park, but he cautions that small, temporary pilots may not predict permanent programs.$q$, null),
  ($q$cs-h252$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Cross-Text Connections$q$, 3, $q$Text 1<br>Some historians argue that the Great Wall of China was ineffective, pointing out that invading armies repeatedly breached it or simply went around it.<br><br>Text 2<br>Historian Wei Zhang contends that judging the wall by whether it stopped every invasion misunderstands its purpose. The wall, she argues, was designed less to halt large armies than to slow raiding parties, channel trade through controlled gates, and allow signal fires to warn of approaching threats. By those measures, she suggests, it was often quite successful.$q$, $q$Based on the texts, how would Zhang (Text 2) most likely respond to the historians' argument in Text 1?$q$, $q$["Zhang would argue that the historians evaluate the wall by a standard that does not match its intended functions.","Zhang would argue that no invading army ever breached or went around the Great Wall.","Zhang would agree that the wall was ineffective because it failed to stop the large armies that repeatedly breached it or went around it.","Zhang would argue that the wall was built mainly to keep Chinese citizens from leaving."]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Zhang says judging the wall by whether it stopped every invasion "misunderstands its purpose," since it was meant to slow raids, control trade, and give warning.$q$, null),
  ($q$cs-h253$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Cross-Text Connections$q$, 3, $q$Text 1<br>Researchers who study creativity have often argued that people are most creative when they are free from constraints. Limits on time, materials, or subject matter, they contend, restrict the range of ideas people can generate.<br><br>Text 2<br>Psychologist Elena Moss found the opposite in a series of experiments. Participants given strict constraints—such as designing a toy using only five specific materials—produced ideas that judges rated as more original than those produced by participants with no limits. Constraints, Moss suggests, push people beyond their first, most obvious ideas.$q$, $q$Based on the texts, how would Moss (Text 2) most likely respond to the researchers' argument in Text 1?$q$, $q$["Moss would agree that people are most creative when they are free from all constraints.","Moss would argue that judges are unable to rate the originality of ideas fairly.","Moss would argue that participants with no limits produced more ideas than those with constraints.","Moss would argue that her findings contradict the view that constraints restrict creativity."]$q$::jsonb, 3, $q$Moss found that constrained participants produced more original ideas, contradicting the claim that constraints restrict creativity.$q$, null),
  ($q$cs-h254$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Cross-Text Connections$q$, 3, $q$Text 1<br>Many wildlife managers advocate culling—deliberately killing a portion of a population—to control invasive species such as feral pigs, which damage crops and native ecosystems.<br><br>Text 2<br>Ecologist Ben Archer notes that culling feral pigs often fails to reduce their numbers for long. Pigs reproduce so quickly that surviving animals can replace the losses within a year or two, and culling can scatter groups into new areas. Archer argues that culling works only when it removes nearly an entire local population at once, which requires coordinated effort across large areas.$q$, $q$Based on the texts, how would Archer (Text 2) most likely respond to the wildlife managers' position in Text 1?$q$, $q$["Archer would argue that feral pigs do not damage crops or native ecosystems.","Archer would agree that culling small numbers of pigs is the most effective way to control them.","Archer would argue that culling as commonly practiced may not achieve the goal its advocates intend.","Archer would argue that feral pigs reproduce too slowly to recover from culling."]$q$::jsonb, 2, $q$Archer says culling often fails because pigs reproduce quickly and scatter, unless nearly whole populations are removed—so typical culling may not work.$q$, null),
  ($q$cs-h255$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Cross-Text Connections$q$, 3, $q$Text 1<br>Psychologist Irene Walsh argues that eyewitness memory is far less reliable than juries tend to believe. In her studies, participants who watched a staged crime often confidently identified the wrong person, especially when investigators asked leading questions.<br><br>Text 2<br>Psychologist Tomás Vega agrees that eyewitness memory can be distorted. But his research shows that a witness's first identification, made quickly and under fair conditions, is often quite accurate; errors tend to accumulate when witnesses are questioned repeatedly or shown the suspect more than once. Vega argues that the problem lies less in memory itself than in how it is tested.$q$, $q$Based on the texts, Walsh (Text 1) and Vega (Text 2) would most likely agree with which statement?$q$, $q$["Both would agree that eyewitness identifications should never be used as evidence in court.","Both would agree that the way witnesses are questioned can affect the accuracy of their identifications.","Both would agree that a witness's confidence is a reliable sign that an identification is accurate.","Both would agree that a witness's first identification is usually less accurate than later ones."]$q$::jsonb, 1, $q$Walsh notes errors occur "especially when investigators asked leading questions," and Vega says errors accumulate with repeated questioning—both see questioning as affecting accuracy.$q$, null),
  ($q$cs-h256$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Cross-Text Connections$q$, 3, $q$Text 1<br>Some economists argue that inflation is caused mainly by governments printing too much money. When the money supply grows faster than the economy's output, they contend, prices inevitably rise.<br><br>Text 2<br>Economist Adaeze Nwosu acknowledges that rapid growth in the money supply can cause inflation. But she points to recent episodes in which prices rose sharply because of disruptions in supply—shipping bottlenecks, poor harvests, and spikes in energy costs—even when money growth was modest. Inflation, she argues, can have several distinct causes, and the right policy response depends on which is at work.$q$, $q$Which choice best describes a difference in how the authors of the two texts view the causes of inflation?$q$, $q$["Text 1 presents a single main cause of inflation, while Text 2 argues that inflation can arise from different causes that call for different responses.","Text 1 argues that inflation is caused by supply disruptions, while Text 2 argues that it is caused mainly by growth in the money supply.","Text 1 treats inflation as a problem governments can control, while Text 2 argues that inflation caused by supply disruptions cannot be addressed by policy at all.","Text 1 argues that governments should print more money, while Text 2 argues that they should print less money."]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Text 1 attributes inflation mainly to money printing; Nwosu argues it has "several distinct causes," including supply disruptions, requiring different policies.$q$, null),
  ($q$cs-h257$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Cross-Text Connections$q$, 3, $q$Text 1<br>Scholars of the ancient world often describe the city of Athens as the birthplace of democracy. In the fifth century BCE, Athenian citizens voted directly on laws and on decisions of war and peace, a system that has inspired democratic movements ever since.<br><br>Text 2<br>Historian Lena Petridis cautions that Athenian democracy excluded most of the people who lived in Athens. Women, enslaved people, and foreign residents—together the large majority of the population—could not vote. Petridis does not deny Athens's influence but argues that calling it a democracy in the modern sense obscures how narrow its political community was.$q$, $q$Based on the texts, how would Petridis (Text 2) most likely respond to the scholars' description in Text 1?$q$, $q$["Petridis would argue that the scholars overstate Athens's influence on later democratic movements, since most of those movements drew on other models instead.","Petridis would agree that Athens was a democracy in the modern sense and that it included most residents.","Petridis would argue that Athens had no influence on the democratic movements that followed it.","Petridis would acknowledge the influence described in Text 1 but argue that the description overlooks the system's exclusions."]$q$::jsonb, 3, $q$Petridis "does not deny Athens's influence" but stresses that most residents couldn't vote, so the label "democracy" obscures its narrowness.$q$, null),
  ($q$cs-h258$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Cross-Text Connections$q$, 3, $q$Text 1<br>Engineer Sam Kowalczyk argues that self-driving cars will dramatically reduce traffic deaths. More than 90 percent of crashes involve human error, he notes, such as distraction, fatigue, or impaired driving—problems that automated systems do not have.<br><br>Text 2<br>Transportation safety researcher Ingrid Holm points out that automated systems introduce their own kinds of errors, such as misreading unusual road conditions or failing to recognize objects they were not trained on. She agrees that automation could prevent many crashes caused by human error but argues that its overall effect on safety cannot be known until such systems have been tested over billions of miles in varied conditions.$q$, $q$Based on the texts, how would Holm (Text 2) most likely respond to Kowalczyk's argument in Text 1?$q$, $q$["Holm would argue that Kowalczyk overstates the role of human error, since most crashes are actually caused by poor road conditions and unusual objects.","Holm would agree that self-driving cars will certainly reduce traffic deaths dramatically.","Holm would agree that automation could prevent certain crashes but argue that Kowalczyk's conclusion is premature.","Holm would argue that automated systems will eventually make more errors than human drivers once they are tested over billions of miles."]$q$::jsonb, 2, $q$Holm "agrees that automation could prevent many crashes caused by human error" but argues that the overall effect is unknown until extensive testing is done.$q$, null),
  ($q$cs-h259$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Cross-Text Connections$q$, 3, $q$Text 1<br>Ecologist Rachel Stone argues that planting trees is one of the most effective ways to fight climate change. Trees absorb carbon dioxide as they grow, she notes, and large-scale planting programs could remove billions of tons of carbon from the atmosphere.<br><br>Text 2<br>Ecologist Marco Silva agrees that forests store large amounts of carbon. But he warns that planting trees in the wrong places can backfire: in grasslands and savannas, which naturally have few trees, new plantations can dry out soils, displace native species, and even release carbon stored underground. Protecting existing forests, Silva argues, is often more effective than planting new ones.$q$, $q$Based on the texts, Stone (Text 1) and Silva (Text 2) would most likely agree with which statement?$q$, $q$["Both would agree that trees should be planted in grasslands and savannas.","Both would agree that trees can store significant amounts of carbon.","Both would agree that planting trees is always more effective than protecting forests.","Both would agree that forests release more carbon than they absorb."]$q$::jsonb, 1, $q$Stone says trees absorb carbon, and Silva "agrees that forests store large amounts of carbon," though they differ on where and how to act.$q$, null),
  ($q$cs-h260$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Cross-Text Connections$q$, 3, $q$Text 1<br>Music historians have often treated the blues as a purely rural tradition, born among Black farmworkers in the Mississippi Delta and only later brought to cities like Chicago.<br><br>Text 2<br>Historian Darnell Price argues that the blues developed through constant movement between country and city. Musicians traveled widely, he notes, performing in rural juke joints and urban theaters alike, and early recordings made in cities shaped how rural musicians played. The rural-to-urban story, Price contends, reduces a two-way exchange to a one-way journey.$q$, $q$Based on the texts, how would Price (Text 2) most likely respond to the historians' view in Text 1?$q$, $q$["Price would argue that the historians' account simplifies a relationship in which influence flowed in both directions.","Price would argue that the blues originated in Chicago and only later spread to the Mississippi Delta.","Price would agree that the blues began among rural farmworkers but argue that it developed its distinctive sound only after musicians moved permanently to Chicago.","Price would argue that rural musicians never performed in urban theaters."]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Price says the rural-to-urban story "reduces a two-way exchange to a one-way journey," describing influence moving both ways.$q$, null),
  ($q$cs-h261$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Cross-Text Connections$q$, 3, $q$Text 1<br>Medical researcher Hannah Fields argues that annual physical exams for healthy adults are largely unnecessary. Large reviews of the evidence, she notes, have found that routine checkups do not reduce deaths from heart disease or cancer, and they may lead to unnecessary tests.<br><br>Text 2<br>Physician Arjun Mehta agrees that annual exams have not been shown to reduce deaths. But he argues that they serve other purposes: they allow doctors to update vaccinations, discuss changes in health habits, and build relationships with patients that make them more likely to seek care when problems arise.$q$, $q$Based on the texts, how would Mehta (Text 2) most likely respond to Fields's argument in Text 1?$q$, $q$["Mehta would argue that Fields relies on flawed reviews and that annual exams do in fact reduce deaths from heart disease and cancer among healthy adults.","Mehta would agree with Fields that annual exams should be eliminated for all healthy adults.","Mehta would argue that unnecessary tests are the main benefit of annual exams.","Mehta would accept the evidence Fields cites but argue that annual exams have benefits that the evidence does not measure."]$q$::jsonb, 3, $q$Mehta "agrees that annual exams have not been shown to reduce deaths" but points to other benefits not captured by mortality data.$q$, null),
  ($q$cs-h262$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Cross-Text Connections$q$, 3, $q$Text 1<br>Many linguists have predicted that globalization will cause most of the world's roughly 7,000 languages to disappear within a century, as speakers shift to dominant languages such as English, Spanish, and Mandarin.<br><br>Text 2<br>Linguist Kaya Begay acknowledges that many languages are endangered. But she points to communities that have reversed language loss through immersion schools, recordings of elders, and the use of social media in their heritage languages. These efforts, Begay argues, show that language decline is not inevitable when communities have resources and support.$q$, $q$Based on the texts, how would Begay (Text 2) most likely respond to the linguists' prediction in Text 1?$q$, $q$["Begay would argue that the linguists exaggerate the threat of globalization, since most endangered languages are declining for reasons unrelated to dominant languages.","Begay would agree that most of the world's languages will certainly disappear within a century.","Begay would argue that the linguists' prediction underestimates what communities can do to preserve their languages.","Begay would argue that social media is the main cause of language loss around the world."]$q$::jsonb, 2, $q$Begay acknowledges endangerment but argues that revitalization efforts show decline "is not inevitable," challenging the confident prediction.$q$, null),
  ($q$cs-h263$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Cross-Text Connections$q$, 3, $q$Text 1<br>Some biologists argue that the brightly colored feathers of male birds evolved mainly to attract mates. Females, they note, often choose the most colorful males, and over many generations this preference would favor brighter plumage.<br><br>Text 2<br>Biologist Nora Kim suggests that bright colors can also serve as warnings. In some species, she notes, males with brighter plumage are more aggressive defenders of territory, and rivals avoid confronting them. Coloration, Kim argues, may often be shaped by competition among males as well as by female choice.$q$, $q$Based on the texts, how would Kim (Text 2) most likely respond to the biologists' argument in Text 1?$q$, $q$["Kim would argue that females never choose mates based on the color of their feathers.","Kim would argue that the biologists' explanation may be incomplete because coloration can also be shaped by competition among males.","Kim would argue that the biologists are mistaken about female choice, since in most species females prefer the least colorful and least aggressive males.","Kim would argue that brightly colored males are less aggressive than duller males."]$q$::jsonb, 1, $q$Kim says coloration "may often be shaped by competition among males as well as by female choice," adding to rather than rejecting the biologists' explanation.$q$, null),
  ($q$cs-h264$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Cross-Text Connections$q$, 3, $q$Text 1<br>Many reviewers have praised the documentary for its objectivity. The filmmaker, they note, never appears on screen, uses no narration, and simply lets the people she filmed speak for themselves.<br><br>Text 2<br>Film critic Jonah Abrams argues that no documentary is truly objective. Every film, he points out, is shaped by decisions about whom to film, which moments to include, and how to order them. A documentary without narration, Abrams contends, may actually conceal the filmmaker's perspective more effectively than one that states it openly.$q$, $q$Based on the texts, how would Abrams (Text 2) most likely respond to the reviewers' praise in Text 1?$q$, $q$["Abrams would argue that the absence of narration does not make a documentary objective and may hide the filmmaker's choices.","Abrams would agree that the documentary is objective because the filmmaker never appears on screen.","Abrams would argue that documentaries with narration are more honest than those without it, because narration always reveals the filmmaker's true opinions.","Abrams would argue that the people in the documentary did not actually speak for themselves."]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Abrams argues that editing choices shape every film and that lack of narration may conceal the filmmaker's perspective, countering the reviewers' praise.$q$, null),
  ($q$cs-h265$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Cross-Text Connections$q$, 3, $q$Text 1<br>Historian George Lyle argues that the transcontinental railroad, completed in the United States in 1869, was a triumph of engineering and national unity. It cut travel time across the continent from months to about a week, binding the country's eastern and western regions together.<br><br>Text 2<br>Historian Mei Chen does not dispute the railroad's importance but focuses on the people who built it. Thousands of Chinese immigrants did much of the most dangerous work in the Sierra Nevada, she notes, at lower pay than white workers, and many died in accidents and avalanches. Their contributions, Chen argues, were largely erased from celebrations of the railroad's completion.$q$, $q$Based on the texts, how would Chen (Text 2) most likely respond to Lyle's account in Text 1?$q$, $q$["Chen would argue that the railroad did not significantly reduce travel time across the continent.","Chen would argue that Lyle overstates the railroad's role in uniting the country, since its completion did little to change travel between the East and the West.","Chen would argue that the railroad was built mainly by workers who were paid more than others.","Chen would accept the railroad's significance but argue that accounts like Lyle's leave out the experiences of the workers who built it."]$q$::jsonb, 3, $q$Chen "does not dispute the railroad's importance" but emphasizes that Chinese workers' dangerous labor was erased from celebrations—an omission in triumphal accounts.$q$, null),
  ($q$cs-h266$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Cross-Text Connections$q$, 3, $q$Text 1<br>Some neuroscientists argue that learning a second language in childhood leads to lasting cognitive benefits, such as an improved ability to switch between tasks and ignore distractions. Bilingual children, they note, often outperform monolingual children on tests of these skills.<br><br>Text 2<br>A large study by psychologist Kirsten Dahl compared thousands of bilingual and monolingual children and found no consistent differences in task-switching or attention once family income and parental education were taken into account. Dahl suggests that earlier studies, which were often small, may have detected differences caused by other factors.$q$, $q$Based on the texts, how would Dahl (Text 2) most likely respond to the neuroscientists' claim in Text 1?$q$, $q$["Dahl would argue that bilingual children perform much worse than monolingual children on tests of attention.","Dahl would agree that bilingualism produces lasting improvements in task-switching for all children.","Dahl would argue that the advantage described in Text 1 may reflect other factors rather than bilingualism itself.","Dahl would argue that family income has no effect on children's performance on cognitive tests."]$q$::jsonb, 2, $q$Dahl's large study found no difference after controlling for income and education, suggesting earlier findings reflected those factors.$q$, null),
  ($q$cs-h267$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Cross-Text Connections$q$, 3, $q$Text 1<br>Many urban planners favor building tall residential towers to house growing city populations. Towers, they argue, fit many people onto small plots of land, preserving open space and supporting public transit.<br><br>Text 2<br>Architect Ingrid Sørensen argues that cities can achieve similar densities with mid-rise buildings of five to eight stories arranged around shared courtyards, as in many European cities. Such buildings, she notes, are cheaper to build per apartment, use less energy for elevators and heating, and encourage residents to know their neighbors.$q$, $q$Based on the texts, how would Sørensen (Text 2) most likely respond to the planners' position in Text 1?$q$, $q$["Sørensen would argue that cities should avoid dense housing and build single-family homes instead.","Sørensen would agree that dense housing is valuable but argue that it need not take the form the planners favor.","Sørensen would agree that tall towers are the only way to house growing city populations.","Sørensen would argue that mid-rise buildings achieve lower densities than towers but are worth building anyway because they help residents know their neighbors."]$q$::jsonb, 1, $q$Sørensen says mid-rise buildings can "achieve similar densities" with added benefits—accepting density as a goal but proposing a different form.$q$, null),
  ($q$cs-h268$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Cross-Text Connections$q$, 3, $q$Text 1<br>Wildlife biologist Owen Price argues that domestic cats are a major threat to birds. In the United States alone, he notes, outdoor cats kill an estimated 1 to 4 billion birds each year, making them one of the leading human-related causes of bird deaths.<br><br>Text 2<br>Ecologist Leah Marsh agrees that cats kill enormous numbers of birds but notes that most of those killed are common species whose populations are stable. Habitat loss and collisions with buildings, she argues, pose greater threats to declining species and deserve more attention than they receive.$q$, $q$Based on the texts, Price (Text 1) and Marsh (Text 2) would most likely agree with which statement?$q$, $q$["Both would agree that outdoor cats kill very large numbers of birds.","Both would agree that cats are the main threat to declining bird species.","Both would agree that collisions with buildings kill more birds than cats do.","Both would agree that the bird species killed by cats are mostly declining species."]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Price cites billions of birds killed, and Marsh "agrees that cats kill enormous numbers of birds," though they differ about which threats matter most.$q$, null)
